window.__ALUMNI_BUNDLE__ = {"last_update_at":"2026-11-05 12:56:52","total_records":93,"data":{"merchants":[{"id":1,"alumni_name":"Tong Mow Chee (aka Tang Tinggui / Tong A-chick)","alumni_name_chi":"唐茂枝（唐廷桂／唐植）","lifespan":"(1828–1897)","grad_year":"1851","chi_alias":"糖廠之父","eng_alias":"Father of Sugary Refinery","chi_desc":"<p>唐茂枝於一八四九至五一年間就讀聖保羅書院。後來隨父赴美國加州，再返中國，成爲滬港知名商人。唐氏曾入資本港首間製糖企業，入股各洋商企業，後來出任怡和洋行買辦。其弟爲洋務運動實業家唐景星（唐廷樞）。</p>\r\n<p>唐氏父親本身在馬禮遜學堂任職，至一八四九年學堂倒閉，唐氏與七位同學轉讀剛開辦的聖保羅書院，同時在巡理府出任翻譯。</p>\r\n<p>唐氏除商界外亦一直活躍公共事業，於加州營商時多次就排華問題與當局交涉；又屢次向上海租界工部局及淸廷上海道抗議外灘公園禁止華人進入的歧視措施。一八七〇年香港東華醫院創立，唐氏爲創院協理之一。</p>\r\n<p>唐氏逝世後，上海各國人士紛紛哀悼，葬禮有三百五十名來自各界人士出席。他的長子唐傑臣承繼父親出任怡和洋行買辦，並完成父親遺願，創辦上海內地自來水公司，將自來水引進上海縣城。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Tong Mow Chee studied at St. Paul’s College between 1849 and 1851, then went to California with his father before returning to China, where he became a renowned merchant in Shanghai and Hong Kong. He invested in Hong Kong’s first sugar refinery, held investments in several foreign enterprises, and was a comprador for Jardine Matheson. Tong is an elder brother of Tong King Sing (aka Tang Tingshu), the entrepreneur associated with the late Qing Self-Strengthening Movement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tong’s father originally taught at the Morrison Education Society School, but its abrupt closure in 1849 meant Tong transferred to Stanton’s newly-opened Anglo-Chinese school, along with seven of his schoolmates. During this time, Tong worked as an interpreter for the magistrate’s court.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Beyond business, Tong was also a prominent public figure. While in California, he frequently pushed back against the local government’s anti-Chinese proposals. The Tung Wah Hospital, opened in 1870, was partially funded by Tong. In Shanghai, Tong lodged complaints with the international settlement’s Municipal Council and the local Qing authorities, protesting against the blanket ban on Chinese in the Public Garden within the Bund.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tong’s death was mourned by many in Shanghai, with 350 attending his funeral. His eldest son, Tong Kit Sing, succeeded his father as Jardine Matheson’s comprador, while also completing Tong Mow Chee’s final wish of supplying potable water to Shanghai’s Old City.</p>\r\n\r\n　　","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/1.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":2,"alumni_name":"David Au Wai Kwok","alumni_name_chi":"歐偉國 ","lifespan":"(1898–1959)","grad_year":"1913","chi_alias":"商界奇才","eng_alias":"The legend of the business world ","chi_desc":"<p>歐偉國生於澳洲悉尼，於一九〇九至一三年間就讀聖保羅書院，其後活躍於港滬商界零售業、金融業等。一九一八年起，他先後出任先施公司祕書和司理，及後任太古洋行遠東副總經理。一九二九年，他出任上海商業儲蓄銀行襄理，五年後開始掌管香港分行，成為聞名兩地之商界領袖。</p>\r\n<p>此外，歐氏亦涉足敎育界，在滬時曾任聖約翰大學校董，在港爲崇基學院創辦人之一，曾任校董會主席，又曾出任代理校長。歐氏與香港政府交涉，終促成崇基學院於一九五三年六月獲肯定為專上學院及「香港敎育制度中的永久部份」；一九六三年，崇基學院與新亞書院和聯合書院合倂成香港中文大學。</p>\r\n<p>歐氏於商業及敎育之外，不忘關注公眾福祉。他先後擔任香港中華基督敎育青年會會長、香港中華慈善會會長、中華聖公會港粵敎區財政部司庫等職務。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Sydney, Australia, David Au studied at St. Paul’s College between 1909 and 1913. After graduating, Au worked primarily in retail and banking. From 1918, he served successively as secretary and manager of Sincere and was also a deputy general manager of Swire. In 1929, he was appointed assistant manager of the Shanghai Commercial and Savings Bank; after managing the Hong Kong branch for five years, he became a renowned business figure across both cities.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Au was additionally a noted educational figure. He was a councillor at St. John’s University in Shanghai and also co-founded Chung Chi College in 1951, chairing the board of trustees and, at one point, becoming its acting principal. After liaising with the government, Chung Chi College was recognised as a higher education institution in June 1953 as ‘a permanent part of Hong Kong’s education system’. The college later became part of the Chinese University of Hong Kong.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Au also cared for the broader public’s well-being: he has served as the Chinese YMCA of Hong Kong president, Chinese Benevolent Association president, and treasurer of the Chung Hua Sheng Kung Hui’s South China Diocese.</p>\r\n\r\n　　\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/2.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":3,"alumni_name":"Tse Yu Chuen OBE JP","alumni_name_chi":"謝雨川","lifespan":"(1899–1976)","grad_year":"1916","chi_alias":"穀米之王\r\n","eng_alias":"King of Rice","chi_desc":"<p>謝雨川生於番禺，一九一六年畢業於聖保羅書院，先後於各洋行任職會計及華人經理，戰後與友人合辦光華洋行。謝氏於香港實行配給制時獲委穀米批發處主任，管理食米進口，力遏奸商於食米攙雜劣質米和砂石；一九五二年獲擢升爲中式食品組主管，負責配給食米、糧油及麪粉。</p>\r\n<p>謝氏一生熱心社會事務，戰前爲反對蓄婢會委員，戰後致力於香港各處成立街坊福利會，又曾任中華總商會副理事長、南華體育會主席、鐘聲慈善社監督等。一九五八年獲委太平紳士及獲頒授ＯＢＥ勛章；一九六四至七〇年間任立法局非官守議員。</p>\r\n<p>謝氏學成後回饋書院，一九五三年起任書院校董，一九六〇年校舍重建時任籌建委員會主席。後來書院興建泳池，獲謝氏家族捐贈鉅款。泳池於一九七九年落成，遂命名「聖保羅書院同學會謝雨川泳池」以作紀念。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Tse Yu Chuen graduated from the College in 1916 and successively worked in various merchant companies as an accountant and manager. After the war, Tse co-founded Cornwell & Co with his acquaintances. He was appointed to supervise rice rationing in Hong Kong after the war, preventing dishonest sellers from mixing in poor-quality stock or even gravel. In 1952, he was promoted to Chinese Food Section Head, which additionally included supervising cooking oil and flour rationing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>An active social welfare figure, Tse was an Anti-<I>Mui Tsai </I>Society committee member and spent his life establishing<I> kaifong</I> (mutual aid) associations across the city after the war. Tse had been a vice-chair of the Chinese General Chamber of Commerce, had chaired the South China Athletic Association, and was a Chung Sing Benevolent Society supervisor. He was appointed JP and appointed OBE in 1958, and served the Legislative Council between 1964 and 1970.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tse also gave back to the College. He had been on the College Council since 1953 and led fundraising efforts for the 1960s Campus Reconstruction Project. When the College built a swimming pool, both the Alumni Association and Tse’s family donated towards its construction; the pool, completed in 1979, was in turn named in his memory.</p>\r\n\r\n　　","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/3.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":4,"alumni_name":"Ho Ka Lau","alumni_name_chi":"何家鎏","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1919","chi_alias":"柴薪之王","eng_alias":"King of Firewood","chi_desc":"<p>何家鎏，一九一九年畢業於聖保羅書院，活躍於體育界，並於戰後對香港復原有所貢獻，亦曾力推聖保羅書院男校獨立復課。</p>\r\n<p>何氏奉一生於體育業界，於一九二〇年代曾多次代表中國參與遠東運動會，其中包括於一九二一年爲國家排球隊奪金、並於一九二三年爲國家籃球隊摘銀。後來何氏擔任本校體育顧問，戰後又經營公司，專營體育用品及木材柴炭。此外，何氏力推中港體育發展，於一九五一年曾任香港業餘體育協會曁奧林匹克委員會常務理事。於中國改革開放之際，又助京、滬鋪設網球場。</p>\r\n<p>二戰後香港物資匱乏，政府實行配給，何氏獲政府委託出任坡柴統制處主任，負責柴薪配給。</p>\r\n<p>何氏曾任本校同學會主席，戰後反對本校與聖保羅女書院合倂，力挽歷史學府，遂發起行動要求聖公會復辦男校，最終於一九五〇年男校於般咸道重開。何氏後來亦於本校校董會擔任義務司庫，至一九七一年退任。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Ho Ka Lau graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1919. He is remembered for his athletic achievements, his contribution to Hong Kong’s post-war recovery, and his instrumental role in reopening the boys’ college.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ho’s life was inseparable from that of sports. In the 1920s, he represented China in multiple Far Eastern Championship Games, winning gold in volleyball in 1921 and silver in basketball in 1923. Later, Ho was a sports consultant for the College. He also had his own business, which sold sporting goods and firewood. In 1951, Ho served on the predecessor to Hong Kong’s Olympic Committee. As China opened up in 1979, Ho helped surface tennis courts in Beijing and Shanghai.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After the Second World War, Hong Kong implemented rationing due to material shortages; Ho was appointed to be in charge of firewood rationing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ho formerly chaired the Old Boys’ Union. When St. Paul’s College was merged with St. Paul’s Girls’ College in 1945, Ho was determined not to let the boys’ school fade into history. Organising activities and negotiating with the diocese, St. Paul’s College eventually reopened in 1950, albeit relocated to Bonham Road. Ho later served on the College Council as honorary treasurer until 1971.</p>\r\n\r\n　　\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/4.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":5,"alumni_name":"Robert Der","alumni_name_chi":"謝伯昌","lifespan":" (1903–1981)","grad_year":"1920","chi_alias":"夠甜又夠辣-糖薑經理 ","eng_alias":"Ginger Manager","chi_desc":"<p>謝伯昌生於台山，於一九一五至二〇年間就讀聖保羅書院。後赴美國攻讀商科，先後任職上海先施部長及南華糖薑廠經理等職。一九三四年，謝氏參與創辦香港中華廠商聯合會，擔任會董至一九六二年。一九四六年起任九龍總商會理事長。</P>\r\n<p>謝氏於一九一八年受洗，自此一生奉獻基督，尤好敎育工作。一九五一年，謝氏創立九龍總商會英文中學（一九八〇年停辦），同年又創立聖公會諸聖中學。一九七九年，政府邀請諸聖中學改爲政府津貼中學，但謝氏反對，堅守敎會辦學自由的理念。</P>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Taishan, Robert Der studied at St. Paul’s College between 1915 and 1920. He furthered his studies in the United States, and worked as a department lead for Sincere Shanghai and a manager for the Nam Wah Ginger Factory, among other positions. In 1934, he co-founded the Chinese Manufacturers’ Association of Hong Kong, serving on the board until 1962. Between 1946 and his death, Der also chaired the Kowloon Chamber of Commerce.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Baptised in 1918, Der served with passion, and was particularly active in educational work. He founded the Kowloon Chamber of Commerce English School and the SKH All Saints’ Middle School in 1951; the former closed in 1980. Der’s faith in the Christian cause can be seen in his stern refusal against a government proposal to turn All Saints’ Middle School into a subsidised school, voicing his belief in religious freedoms.</p>\r\n\r\n　　","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/5.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":6,"alumni_name":"Wong Cheuk Um (aka C.P. Wong)","alumni_name_chi":"黃焯菴（黃炳耀）","lifespan":"(?–1955)","grad_year":"1920","chi_alias":"食糖之王","eng_alias":" King of Sugar ","chi_desc":"<p>黃焯菴生於澳門，一九二〇年畢業於聖保羅書院。一九三〇年代太古洋行廢除買辦制後，黃氏出任太古洋行華人經理一職，為行內華人最高職位。黃氏曾掌管位於鰂魚涌的太古糖廠，時爲全球最大糖廠之一。戰後黃氏獲政府委任主理香港食糖配給。</p>\r\n<p>黃氏一生着重香港敎育發展，加入太古洋行前曾投身敎育工作。香港戰後人口急增，日校學位供不應求，黃氏遂與聖保羅校友倡議設夜校——後來聖保羅英文夜校於一九五一年成立（即為後來的聖保羅夜中學）。學校營運五十年間獲益學子無數，至二〇〇〇年代停辦。同年見書院遷址後地方不敷應用，黃氏遂捐建書院。一九五三年，以其父命名紀念的黃鳴謙堂落成。此外，其姊黃鳳翎篤信佛敎，於黃氏病逝後捐建佛敎黃焯菴紀念小學以作紀念。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Macau, Wong Cheuk Um graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1920. He was most well-known for being one of Swire’s first Chinese managers, which was the company’s highest Chinese rank as it abandoned the comprador system in the 1930s. Wong managed the Taikoo Sugar Refinery in Quarry Bay, at the time one of the largest in the world. After the Second World War, when Hong Kong rationed supplies, Wong was appointed to be in charge of sugar distribution.</p>\r\n<p>Before joining Swire, Wong had been an educator, and he remained interested in furthering the cause. After 1945, as Hong Kong rebuilt itself, a rapid population influx strained the city’s limited number of schools. Together with the old Paulines, Wong co-founded St. Paul’s English Evening School in 1951 (later St. Paul’s Secondary Evening School), which helped children in need throughout its five decades of operation. That same year, he donated towards a new building on the College’s Bonham Road campus, which would be completed in 1953 as the Wong Ming Him Hall, named in memory of his father. The Buddhist Wong Cheuk Um Primary School, founded in 1957, is named in his memory and was donated by his Buddhist elder sister, Wong Fung Ling.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/6.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":7,"alumni_name":"Yip Yeuk Lam","alumni_name_chi":"葉若林 ","lifespan":"(1916–2007)","grad_year":"1936","chi_alias":"供水大隊長","eng_alias":"Water Captain","chi_desc":"<p>葉若林在一九三六年畢業於聖保羅書院，其後從事副食品業，曾擔任香港蛋品企業有限公司董事長、香港果菜營業有限公司董事長、香港菜聯行總經理等職，又曾任港中醫院董事及中華總商會司庫及副會長。</p>\r\n<p>葉氏熱心促進中港交流。自一九五〇年代起從事中國副食品供港的業務。期間內地政治不穩，致大批難民湧入香港，葉氏保持業務正常運作，令糧食維持穩定供應。</p>\r\n<p>一九五〇年代，香港人口劇增，工業迅速發展，食水用量大幅上升，制水成日常問題，因此香港需要從深圳水庫引水。一九六三年，深圳水庫水量不足，香港限制供水至每四日一次。有見及此，葉氏提議直接從東江駁喉運水，隨後三赴廣州，終於取得省政府與港府同意供水安排，並在一九六五年正式開始供水，對戰後香港穩步發展影響甚深。</p>\r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Yip Yeuk Lam graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1936. He worked in food distribution and wholesale and was the CEO of the Hong Kong Union Egg Corporation, Hong Kong Fruit & Vegetable Merchants Association, and general manager of the Hong Kong Choi Luen Hong, to name but a few. He also once served on the board of the (now-closed) Hong Kong Central Hospital and was a treasurer and vice-chairman of the Chinese General Chamber of Commerce.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Since the 1950s, Yip has worked to improve mainland–Hong Kong cooperation. He engaged in importing non-staple food items from mainland China. Despite China’s early political upheavals, which led to a mass influx of refugees, Yip managed to maintain a constant supply of food in Hong Kong.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>During the 1950s, Hong Kong’s growth outpaced its water supply. Despite additional imports from Shenzhen Reservoir, low water levels meant the government had to introduce a once-every-four-days water ration during 1963. Yip proposed building a series of pipelines to transfer water directly from the Dong River, after negotiating with Guangdong’s provincial government. An agreement was eventually reached, and the pipeline was operational in 1965, proving vital for Hong Kong’s stable post-war development.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n　","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/7.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":8,"alumni_name":"Wong Chung Ying","alumni_name_chi":"黃頌盈 ","lifespan":"(1919–2012)","grad_year":"1937","chi_alias":"民安行創辦人 (東芝代理) ","eng_alias":"Founder of Man On Hong (Present-day Toshiba Hong Kong)","chi_desc":"<p>黃頌盈以走讀生身份入讀聖保羅書院，於一九三七年畢業。畢業後，黃氏遵父命接手家族生意，從事出入口貿易，包括創辦民安行，經營民泰行等——前者代理東芝家庭電器，後者代理日本海味，爲南北行之佼佼者。商務以外，黃氏亦熱心公務、貢獻家鄕，曾任獅子會三〇三區（港澳）總監、華商會所主席等職務。</p>\r\n<p>就讀書院時，令黃氏印象深刻是當時（以至現今）每日上課前設有早會的傳統，以及與他親如兄弟的師長，包括性格隨和的史超域和史伊尹校長。兩位校長均會邀請學生到宿舍茶聚及交流。黃氏畢業後仍熱心舊校事務，曾任​​書院校董，並在一九八一年於本校成立黃潤棠獎學金，以記念其父親。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Wong Chung Ying entered St. Paul’s College as a day student, graduating in 1937. After further studies, Wong took over the family business: goods trading and distribution. Wong founded Man On Hong (present-day Toshiba Hong Kong) and operated Mantile Company, which imported dried seafood from Japan. Such entrepreneurship established him as one of the top merchants among Chinese-operated trading houses. Wong was additionally active in public affairs, once serving as the district governor of Lions Club International in Hong Kong and Macau, as a former president of the Chinese Club, and so on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>St. Paul’s left a deep impression on Wong. He particularly remembered the daily assemblies, a tradition that continues to this day. Another was the tight-knit student-teacher relationship; then-principals Arthur Stewart and later Evan Stewart would often invite students to the College hostel for casual conversation. Wong remained committed to his alma mater and once sat on the College council. He also set up the Wong Yun Tong Scholarship in 1981, named in memory of his father.</p>\r\n\r\n　　","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/8.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":9,"alumni_name":"Yau Fu Hong","alumni_name_chi":"丘福雄 ","lifespan":"(1919–2004)","grad_year":"1940","chi_alias":"慈善商人","eng_alias":"Promoter of Philanthropy","chi_desc":"<p>丘福雄生於廣東梅縣，家族爲客家商人，在荷屬東印度（今印尼）營商。丘氏幼居巴達維亞（今雅加達）及汕頭，抗戰爆發移居香港，入讀聖保羅書院，於一九四〇年畢業。香港淪陷期間，丘氏返鄕敎書、經商。及後日本投降，丘氏返港輔助家族事業榮南興，後任董事總經理。後來又創萬順企業、柏樂發展，經營港、臺、印尼、歐、美業務，從事貿易及地產業。</p>\r\n<p>一九六八年，丘氏皈依粉嶺蓬瀛仙館學道，於一九八四至九〇年間任館長，期間擴大仙館慈善事業，成立幼稚園、自修室、老人服務中心等，貫徹家族既往敎育慈善事業，一九九二年更被推仙館永遠館長。一九九五年，丘氏獲聘港事顧問，助香港平安回歸，一九九六年獲選香港特區第一屆政府推選委員，二〇〇三年任香港道敎聯合會會長，二〇〇四年仙逝。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Yau Fu Hong was born in Meixian (Mei-hsien). His family were Hakka merchants (the Hioe family) who made their fortune in the Dutch East Indies (now Indonesia); Yau’s childhood was spent between Batavia (now Jakarta) and Shantou (Swatow). As the Second Sino-Japanese War broke out, Yau moved to Hong Kong, enrolling in St. Paul’s College. He graduated in 1940. After Hong Kong fell, Yau returned to his birthplace (which remained under Chinese control), where he entered teaching and trading. Yau went back to Hong Kong after the war and assisted the family business, Yoeng Nam Hien, later becoming the company’s general manager. Yau had also founded Vanson Enterprises and Proud Luck Development, involving himself in the import-export trade across Hong Kong, Taiwan, and Indonesia, and property development in Europe and America.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In 1968, Yau joined the Fung Ying Seen Koon, a Taoist temple in Fanling. He served as the temple’s chairman from 1984 to 1990, during which he expanded upon the family tradition of philanthropy by building kindergartens, study facilities, elderly centres, and so on; he was further named the temple’s eternal chairman in 1992, and he was chairman of the Hong Kong Taoist Association from 2003 to 2004. He served as a Hong Kong Affairs Adviser to Beijing between 1995 and 1997 and as an HKSAR election committee member from 1996 until his death.</p>\r\n\r\n　　","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/9.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":10,"alumni_name":"Philip Wu Po Him BBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"伍步謙","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1692","chi_alias":"前永隆常務監事董事","eng_alias":"Former Wing Lung Bank managing director","chi_desc":"<p>伍步謙出身銀行世家，一九六二年畢業於聖保羅書院，其後赴海外深造，於一九八二至二〇〇八年間任永隆銀行常務董事。\r\n<p>除銀行業務外，伍氏活躍於香港歷史保育，於一九九九至二〇一三年間任衞奕信勳爵文物信託受託人委員會，其中二〇〇七年起任主席；二〇〇四至〇六年任博物館委員會主席、二〇〇七至一三年任尤德爵士紀念基金主席、二〇〇八至一四年間任政府活化歷史建築諮詢委員會成員、二〇一〇至一四年任歷史博物館諮詢委員會主席。一九九七年，伍氏獲委太平紳士，二〇〇五年更獲頒銅紫荊星章。\r\n<p>伍氏又熱心敎育，於二〇〇七至一三年間曾任香港浸會大學校董，二〇一三至二〇一九年間加入香港公開大學（今都會大學）諮議會。同時，伍氏爲中國燭光敎育基金贊助人，支援內地貧困山區敎育發展。\r\n<p>就讀聖保羅書院時，伍氏中一加入書院新開設之網球班，從此愛上了網球；及後就讀香港大學時，曾入選大學網球代表隊。","eng_desc":"<p>Philip Wu Po Him graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1962. Born into a banking family, after further studies overseas, Wu served as a managing director of Wing Lung Bank from 1982 to 2008.\r\n\r\n<p>Outside of banking, Wu is known for participating in the city’s historical education work. He was a trustee of the Lord Wilson Heritage Trust from 1999 to 2013, chairing the trust from 2007; he chaired the government museums committee from 2004 to 2006; he was chairman of trustees within the Sir Edward Youde Memorial Fund from 2007 to 2013; he was part of the government advisory committee for revitalising historic buildings from 2008 to 2014; and he chaired the History Museum advisory panel from 2010 to 2014. He was appointed JP in 1997 and awarded the BBS in 2005.\r\n\r\n<p>Wu is also an educational contributor and has served on the Hong Kong Baptist University Council (2007–13) and the Hong Kong Open University (now Metropolitan University) Court (2013–19). He has also sponsored the China Candlelight Education Fund since 2000, helping mainland children living in poverty gain access to education. \r\n\r\n<p>While at St. Paul’s, Wu learnt tennis and became an avid player; while at the University of Hong Kong, he played for the university.\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/10.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":11,"alumni_name":"Kenneth Ting Woo Shou SBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"丁午壽","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1963","chi_alias":"玩具大王","eng_alias":"King of Toys","chi_desc":"<p>丁午壽在一九六三年於聖保羅書院初六畢業。身爲工業家丁熊照之子，丁氏就讀聖保羅書院時已對勞作科大感興趣，曾用舊雜誌自製獨木舟，奠定他未來工業發展的基石。他參與過不少學校活動，亦爲學校童軍之一。</p>\r\n<p>一九六八年由美國畢業後，丁氏回流香港，繼承家族開達實業。他看準中國改革開放的機會，北遷工廠，用以降低生產成本。隨後，開達於一九八一年收購美國模型火車公司Bachmann，一九八五年丁氏又協助開達上市，於一九九三年正式成為開達實業公司主席。現在，開達已成爲全球最大玩具及模型火車生產商之一。</p>\r\n<p>丁氏致力為社會服務，曾任東華三院總理，兩屆立法會工業界功能組別議員，香港工業總會及香港中華廠商聯合會名譽會長，現任自由黨委員。一九八六年獲委太平紳士，二〇〇四年獲頒特區銀紫荊星章，並於二〇一〇年獲香港傑出工業家獎。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Kenneth Ting Woo Shou completed his studies at St. Paul’s in 1963. Born into an industrialist family, Ting’s interest in arts and crafts was already apparent during his time at the College; he even fashioned a full-size canoe out of old magazines. He was a scout and was active in College events.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After furthering his studies in the USA, he returned to Hong Kong in 1968 to take over the family business, Kader. China’s opening up presented an opportunity, and Ting relocated Kader’s factories to reduce costs. In 1981, Kader acquired Bachmann, a well-known model train maker. The company turned public in 1985, and Ting became its chairman in 1993. Today, Kader is among the largest toy and model train manufacturers in the world.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Outside of his industrial work, Ting was a director of the Tung Wah Group of Hospitals, served two terms in the Legislative Council (Industrial (First) functional constituency, 1998–2004), and remains a committee member of the Liberal Party. He has been named honorary president of both the Federation of Hong Kong Industries and the Chinese Manufacturers’ Association of Hong Kong. Ting was appointed JP in 1986, awarded the SBS in 2004 and the Hong Kong Industrialist of the Year Award in 2010.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/11.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":12,"alumni_name":"Chan Kei Biu MH","alumni_name_chi":"陳其鑣","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1965","chi_alias":"中三畢業 — 香港電子業商會榮譽會長","eng_alias":"HKEIA Honourary  Chairman","chi_desc":"<p>陳其鑣於一九六五年在聖保羅書院完成中三後赴英升學，爲電子工程師及商人。大學於倫敦大學瑪麗皇后學院（時稱Queen Mary College）就讀電機工程學，其後主攻光纖通訊，一九七二年獲博士學位，及後曾短暫進行博士後硏究。</p>\r\n<p>陳氏於一九七三年回港，旋即投身電子業，生產電子錶和電話等。一九八二年自立門戶，一九八六年成立新進科技集團，從事印製及組裝電路板等代工服務。二〇〇三至一一年間擔任香港電子業商會會長，期間特別着重環保和硏發。二〇〇四年，見歐盟實施環保電子產品法規，陳氏創立香港綠色製造聯盟，監察不同地區綠色製造法規的轉變。同年見日本禁止含鉛焊鍚，於是在港發展無鉛焊鍚技術，成為業界先驅。</p>\r\n<p>陳氏歷任廣東工業大學、香港理工大學、香港城市大學客座敎授，二〇〇八年獲頒香港榮譽勳章。他曾言自己特別喜歡工業無限的發展空間，讓熱愛科技研究的他能學以致用。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Chan Kei Biu is an electronics engineer and businessman. He briefly studied at St. Paul’s College, leaving for Britain after his secondary 3 year in 1965. He obtained his doctorate in 1972 at Queen Mary College (now Queen Mary, University of London) for his thesis on fibre optic communications. After a brief foray into postdoctoral research, Chan returned to Hong Kong in 1973.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Initially producing electronic watches and telephones, Chan established his own business in 1982. In 1986, he established Surface Mount Technology, which, as its name suggests, specialises in circuit board manufacturing and assembly. Between 2003 and 2011, he chaired the Hong Kong Electronic Industries Association (HKEIA). In 2004, seeing the European Union tightening its environmental regulations, Chan set up the Hong Kong Green Manufacturing Alliance to monitor changing environmental policies internationally. That same year, Japan banned lead solder. Chan was among the first to research implementing lead-free soldering in Hong Kong, giving him a head start. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chan has taught as a guest professor at the Guangdong University of Technology and as an adjunct professor at the Hong Kong Polytechnic University and at City University. He was awarded the Medal of Honour in 2008. He has said he enjoys the unlimited possibilities industrial development has to offer, allowing him to put his enthusiasm for technological research into practice.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/12.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":13,"alumni_name":"Ian Chan Yau Nam SBS MH","alumni_name_chi":"陳幼南 ","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1969","chi_alias":"中華廠商會永遠榮譽會長","eng_alias":"The Life Honourary Chairman of the Chinese General Chamber of Commerce, Hong Kong ","chi_desc":"<p>陳幼南在一九六〇至六九年間於聖保羅書院就讀，一九六七年畢業。博士畢業後，陳氏曾爲美國布魯克黑文國家實驗室及蜆殼公司進行有關石油提煉硏究。一九八五年，陳氏返港繼承父業，任屏山企業及穗屏企業董事總經理，從事動物飼料業。</p>\r\n<p>商務以外，陳氏歷任中華總商會副會長、香港潮屬社團總會主席、廣東省政協委員、汕頭市政協委員等職，熱心社會事務。 陳氏於二〇一三年獲頒榮譽勳章，二〇一八年獲頒銀紫荊星章。</p>\r\n<p>陳氏自言深受聖保羅書院自由氣氛影響，認爲是造就師生於多方面有成就之原因。在學期間最令他感自豪的往事是一九六七年在代理校長衞理士支持下，參與創辦聯校科學展覽會，十所中學於香港大會堂擧行展覽，合共二萬人參觀。畢業多年，陳氏不忘回饋本校，曾任校董會成員，現任聖保羅書院基金有限公司副主席。</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Ian Chan studied at St. Paul’s from 1960 to 1969, graduating in 1967. He worked as an oil refinement researcher for Brookhaven National Laboratory and Shell after attaining his doctorate. In 1985, Chan returned to Hong Kong to succeed his father’s businesses, Ping Shan and Sui Ping, which specialise in fodder (animal feed) manufacturing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Not one to forget his roots, Chan has also been vice chairman of the Chinese General Chamber of Commerce, life honorary chairman of the Federation of Hong Kong Chiu Chow Community Organisations, a member of the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference (Guangdong province and Shantou municipality), among other positions. He was awarded the Medal of Honour in 2013 and the SBS in 2018.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The freedom found within St. Paul’s is a catalyst for the varied achievements among its teachers and students, according to Chan. One of his proudest achievements was organising the inaugural Joint School Science Exhibition in 1968 with the support of the Acting Principal R.G. Wells; with ten schools’ projects in City Hall, the exhibition drew a total of 20,000 visitors. Chan remained committed to the College since his graduation, and is a former College council member. He now serves as Vice Chairman of St. Paul’s College Foundation Limited.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n　　","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/13.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":14,"alumni_name":"Roger Luk Koon Hoo BBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"陸觀豪","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1970","chi_alias":"商道敎授","eng_alias":"Business Professor ","chi_desc":"<p>陸觀豪一九六〇年入讀聖保羅書院小學小四班，一九七〇年高六畢業，投身金融界，一九七五年加入恒生銀行，一九九五年升至常務董事兼副行政總裁，二〇〇四年退休。後擔任市區重建局及有線寬頻等組織非執行董事。</p>\r\n<p>銀行界外，陸氏亦熱心社會建設，退休前後出任多項公職，在一九九二至九五年間任立法局議員（委任議席），又曾任廣播事務管理局成員、港府中央政策組成員等；一九九九年獲委太平紳士，二〇〇四年獲頒銅紫荊星章。敎育方面，陸氏爲本校、恒生大學及香港中文大學校董，退休後曾分別於香港大學及香港城市大學任助理敎授及客席敎授，現任中大商學院客席敎授，繼續培育人才。</p>\r\n<p>曾在童軍、合唱團和戲劇團活躍的他坦言，從課外活動中，從結繩到即興合作，以至解決各項難題，都讓他學到終生受用的技巧，為日後的成就鋪路。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Roger Luk enrolled at St. Paul’s College Primary School in 1960 as a fourth-year student, graduating sixth form from the College in 1970. He joined Hang Seng Bank in 1975 and was its managing director and deputy CEO from 1995 until his retirement in late 2004. Post-retirement, Luk remains a non-executive director across organisations such as the Urban Renewal Authority and i-Cable Communications.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Luk is particularly enthusiastic about Hong Kong’s bettermen, and has served on the Legislative Council (appointed, 1992–95) and on multiple governmental advisory committees, including the Hong Kong Broadcasting Authority and the Central Policy Unit. He was appointed JP in 1999 and awarded the BBS in 2004. Luk is also an active educator, serving on the councils of our College and the Chinese University of Hong Kong (CUHK), as well as Hang Seng University’s board of governors. He has taught at the University of Hong Kong as an assistant professor and at City University as an adjunct professor, and he is currently an adjunct professor at CUHK’s Business School.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Such varied achievements were, in part, down to what Luk observed as St. Paul’s emphasis on versatility. As a former scout, chorister, and drama club member, Luk notes that such extracurricular activities taught him skills that set him up for life, from knot-tying to teamwork to improvisation to problem-solving.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/14.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":15,"alumni_name":"Saimond Ip","alumni_name_chi":"葉松茂","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1982","chi_alias":"科網創業家","eng_alias":"Dot-com entrepreneur","chi_desc":"<p>葉松茂於一九七一年入讀聖保羅書院小一，一九八二年畢業，爲創業家及作家。曾於管理顧問公司麥肯錫就職，曾與楊國猛等聖保羅舊生創辦媒體科網公司36.com，曾任一路通行政總裁等。</p>\r\n<p>葉氏曾著《101 間香港敎會經驗分析》，曾於建道神學院任敎，二〇一〇至二〇一七年間爲政府助學金聯席委員會委員，當中二〇一一至一七年間任副主席。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Saimond Ip entered St. Paul’s College Primary School in 1971 and graduated in 1982. An entrepreneur and writer, Ip has worked at the management consultancy McKinsey & Co., co-founded the media dot-com company 36.com with fellow Pauline Ken Yeung, was CEO of OnePort, and so on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ip’s literary works include <i>The Hong Kong Local Church 101</i>. He has previously taught at the Alliance Bible Seminary. From 2010 to 2017, Ip was part of the government student finance joint committee, serving as its deputy chairman from 2011 to 2017. </p>\r\n\r\n　\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/15.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":16,"alumni_name":"Ken Yeung Kwok Mung","alumni_name_chi":"楊國猛","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1982","chi_alias":"TOM 執行董事","eng_alias":"CEO of Tom Group","chi_desc":"<p>楊國猛在一九八二年於聖保羅書院中五畢業，現任ＴＯＭ集團執行長兼執行董事，爲香港上市科技及媒體公司。旗下業務包括城邦媒體集團、社媒網站痞客邦、中國內地網購平臺郵樂等。ＴＯＭ集團本身爲長江和記實業旗下企業。</p>\r\n<p>楊氏於聖保羅書院以佳績畢業後赴美升學，曾任昇陽電腦工程師，及後回流返港，一九九九年與舊時書院同學創辦網絡媒體公司３６．ｃｏｍ，曾於香港交易所創業板上市，二〇〇八年出任現職。</p>\r\n<p>今時除公司業務外，楊氏亦於本校師友計劃擔任導師，望用綿力助後輩成才。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Ken Yeung graduated from the College in 1982, and is the incumbent director and CEO of the publicly-traded technology and media company Tom Group. Projects under the Tom Group umbrella include the publisher Cite Media Holdings, the social media platform Pixnet, and Ule, an online shopping service in mainland China. Tom Group is itself a member of the Hong Kong conglomerate, CK Hutchison Holdings.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After graduating from St. Paul’s, Yeung furthered his studies in the United States, where he was an engineer for Sun Microsystems before returning to Hong Kong. In 1999, he co-created the dot-com company 36.com together with past Paulines. It was once listed as part of the Growth Enterprise Market on Hong Kong’s stock exchange. Yeung was appointed to his current position in 2008.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In addition to corporate work, Yeung is also a mentor on the College’s mentorship programme, helping current students find their future career.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/16.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":92,"alumni_name":"Helen Wong Pik Kuen JP","alumni_name_chi":"黃碧娟","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1980","chi_alias":"新加坡銀行業首名女總裁","eng_alias":"First Female Banking Chief in Singapore","chi_desc":"<p>黃碧娟一九八〇年於聖保羅書院預科畢業，後晉身銀行業，先以實習生身份加入華僑銀行，至一九九二年加入滙豐銀行，二〇一五年獲擢升為該行大中華地區總裁。黃氏現任華僑銀行集團總裁，為新加坡銀行業史上首名女總裁。</p> <p>任職滙豐廿七年間，黃氏以把握機遇而擴張銀行中國業務為己任，在二〇一〇至一五年間於上海任內地分行總裁。身為大中華地區總裁，她曾領導全行最大分行，統籌兩岸四地業務，維持滙豐作為內地最大規模外資銀行的地位，又重視香港作為內地聯繫世界的國際地位。黃氏轉職華僑銀行後，除運用卅餘年經驗以拓展銀行東南亞業務，亦着重於持續發展——華僑銀行於二〇二二年加入聯合國淨零銀行業聯盟。</p> <p>金融界以外，黃氏又曾於二〇〇五至〇九年間任廉政公署防止貪污諮詢委員會委員，二〇〇八至一〇年間任香港中樂團理事會成員，於二〇一七年獲委太平紳士。</p> <p>憶述「男校女生」的兩年，黃氏深受聖保羅男生的勤奮精神啟發。聖保羅是她開始接觸籃球運動的園地，她後來更代表香港出戰一九八三年世界大學生運動會。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Helen Wong graduated sixth form from St. Paul’s College in 1980. A banker, Wong began her career with Singapore-based OCBC (Oversea-Chinese Banking Corporation) Bank as a management trainee. In 1992, she joined HSBC, where she rose to become the bank’s Greater China chief in 2015. She is now the group CEO of OCBC Bank, making her the first woman chief in the city-state’s banking history.</p> <p>Throughout her twenty-seven-year career in HSBC, Wong has focused on expansion opportunities in China. From 2010 to 2015, she was the CEO of HSBC China (in Shanghai). As Greater China chief, Wong was in charge of the bank’s largest division. Coordinating Hong Kong, Macau, Taiwan, and mainland China, Wong helped maintain HSBC’s position as the mainland’s largest external bank, while valuing Hong Kong’s position as China’s gateway to the world. At OCBC Bank, in addition to applying her decades-long experience in expanding its presence in South East Asia, Wong has also placed an emphasis on sustainable banking, with the bank joining the United Nations’ Net-Zero Banking Alliance in 2022.</p> <p>Outside of finance, Wong has also served on the Corruption Prevention Advisory Committee (2005–09) of the Independent Commission Against Corruption (ICAC) and was a councillor of the Hong Kong Chinese Orchestra (2008–10). She was appointed JP in 2017.</p> <p>Reminiscing on her two years as ‘a girl in a boys’ school’, Wong remembers being inspired by the boys’ can-do spirit. St. Paul’s was also the place where Wong began playing basketball; while at university, Wong represented Hong Kong in the 1983 Universiade.</p>","category":"merchants","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/92.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"}],"artists":[{"id":17,"alumni_name":"Lai Man Wai","alumni_name_chi":"黎民偉","lifespan":" (1893–1953)","grad_year":"1912","chi_alias":"香港電影之父 ","eng_alias":"Father of Hong Kong Cinema","chi_desc":"<p>黎民偉生於橫濱，六歲舉家來港定居，一九一二年畢業於聖保羅書院。黎氏是專業導演、攝製、紀錄攝影師，在一九一三年拍攝香港首部劇情短片《莊子試妻》，於一九二五年製作香港首部長片《胭脂》，他一生拍製逾八十齣作品，獲「香港電影之父」及「中國電影拓荒者」等美譽。</p>\r\n<p>受革命思潮影響，黎氏在一九〇九年加入同盟會，一生懷着「電影救國」的抱負。黎氏爲中華歷史留下珍貴影片，包括一九二一年孫中山於廣州就任非常大總統，國民革命軍北伐等，多段影片於一九四一年剪輯成《勳業千秋》。此外，黎氏在一九二二年曾記錄伍廷芳國葬，於一九三七年又攝錄淞滬會戰，於銀幕上展現自己以電影作敎化的理念。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>黎氏又與兄輩合作，在一九二一年與黎海山及黎北海創辦香港首間華資戲院「新世界戲院」，其後在一九二五年遷至上海，合辦民新電影公司，製作多部電影，當中以《西廂記》（一九二七年）及《神女》（一九三四年）最爲膾炙人口，乃中國默片經典。</p>\r\n<p>抗戰期間及過後，黎氏輾轉中國多處，最終回港，任洗印及技術顧問。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Lai Man Wai was born in Yokohama. His family moved to Hong Kong when he was six, and he graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1912. A pioneering film director, cinematographer, and documentarian, Lai wrote and starred in Hong Kong’s first film production, <i>Chuang Tzu Tests His Wife</i> (1913), and directed the city’s first full-length feature, <i>Rouge</i> (1925). He is regarded as the ‘father of Hong Kong cinema’.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Influenced by the air of revolution in 1900s China, Lai joined the Tongmenghui in 1909. Believing in the educational capacity of film, Lai set out to document key moments in Chinese history, including Sun Yat-sen’s installation as extraordinary president in 1921 and the 1926–28 Northern Expedition. These snippets were then recompiled as <i>A Page of History</i> (1941). Together with his 1922 record of Wu Ting Fang’s state funeral, and his 1937 documentary on the Battle of Shanghai, Lai’s ideals for cinema — and hopes for China’s future — were projected onto the silver screen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lai was also business-minded: together with his brothers, they built the World Theatre, the first Chinese-funded theatre in Hong Kong. Lai moved to Shanghai in 1925, founding China Sun Shanghai. He was involved in Chinese cinema’s silent-era classics such as <i>Romance of the Western Chamber</i> (1927) and <i>The Goddess</i> (1934).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>During and after the Second Sino-Japanese War, Lai moved across China with no fixed abode, before finally returning to Hong Kong, where he served as a technical consultant.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/17.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":34,"alumni_name":"Ho Chak Man (aka Ho Tai So) ","alumni_name_chi":"何澤民（何大儍／何福如）","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1919","chi_alias":"廣東音樂天王","eng_alias":"Heavenly king of Cantonese music","chi_desc":"<p> 何澤民，廣東三水人，在一九一一至一九年間就讀聖保羅書院，期間因其個性好動諧趣，得「大儍」諢號。何氏於一九一五年與同校錢廣仁等創辦鐘聲劇社，爲鐘聲慈善社的前身。</p>\r\n<p>何氏二十餘歲時已於上海巡迴演出，精通中西樂器，演唱上中西合璧，曾把夏威夷結他改造成三弦式，使之更能融合到廣東音樂中。他創造了諧趣的「何腔」，灌錄唱片逾五十張，曾爲十部電影提供配樂，並參演逾八十部電影，在一九五〇年代獲譽為「四大廣東音樂天王」之一。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Ho Tai So was a native of Sansui, Guangdong. He studied at St. Paul’s College between 1911 and 1919, during which his vivacity got him the nickname Tai So (‘Great Silly’). Together with his schoolmate, Chin Kwong Yan, among others, he co-founded a theatre troupe that became the Chung Sing Benevolent Society. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ho began touring Shanghai in his early twenties as a musician, fusing Chinese and western styles: he once converted a ukulele into a three-stringed variant to better suit Cantonese music. His eponymous performance style was known for its comedic, energetic charm. A prolific artist, Ho had over fifty records, ten film scores, and eighty movie appearances to his name. Before the ‘four heavenly kings of Cantopop’ in the 1990s, Ho was regarded as one of the ‘four heavenly kings of Cantonese music’ in the 1950s. </p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/34.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":35,"alumni_name":"Sit Kok Sin","alumni_name_chi":"薛覺先","lifespan":"(1904–1956)","grad_year":"1919","chi_alias":"薛腔粵劇鬼才","eng_alias":"Maestro of Cantonese Opera","chi_desc":"<p>薛覺先，廣東順德人，在一九一六至一九年間於聖保羅書院就讀，爲粵劇名師。就讀聖保羅期間頻常參與話劇演出。在一九一八年其父親病逝，薛覺先需靠賣藥幫補家計，不得已需退學。</p>\r\n<p>十八歲時拜姊夫新少華爲師學藝，因天資聰穎，在二十歲已做正印。於一九二五年赴上海，其間吸收京劇、崑劇及北派武術元素，在一九二九年返回廣州，先後創辦覺先聲劇團，發展獨特的「薛腔」，並參與多部戲班，同時改革粵劇，改良化妝、服飾、佈景，並革除陋習，讓粵劇於二十世紀時在華南廣受歡迎，與京劇名師梅蘭芳合稱「南薛北梅」，最著名劇目包括《胡不歸》、《姑緣嫂劫》、《璇宮艷史》等。</p>\r\n<p>薛氏意識到戲劇對政治、文化之重要，曾指「戲劇藝術，應隨着時代同行並進，才不致落伍泯滅」。他活躍於中國早期電影製作，參演一九三三年有聲粵語片《白金龍》，將粵劇帶上銀幕。薛氏亦熱衷政治，少時曾爲推廣五四運動而投稿、演講等。九一八事變及廣州淪陷後亦爲中國軍隊及災民籌款、義演。薛氏晚年又曾任中國人民政治協商會議全國委員會委員。</p>\r\n<p>受敎於薛氏名伶包括白雪仙、林家聲等，承傳對粵劇不懈的精神。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Shunde in 1904, Sit Kok Sin was a maestro of Cantonese opera. He enrolled at St. Paul’s College in 1916. His father’s death in 1918 forced him to drop out in 1919; Sit worked at a pharmacist’s to make ends meet. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>When Sit was eighteen, he began his theatrical apprenticeship. By the age of twenty, Sit was already playing lead female roles. He went to Shanghai in 1925, absorbing northern performance art influences. In 1929, Sit founded the Kok Sin Sing Opera Troupe in Guangzhou. There, Sit reformed Cantonese opera in earnest, ridding it of outdated practices, developing his eponymous vocal style, and introducing new norms in make-up and stagecraft. Consequently, Cantonese opera grew to become a favourite form of entertainment in mid-to-late twentieth-century southern China; Sit’s legacy on Cantonese opera in the south is often compared to Mei Lanfang’s on Peking opera in the north. His output includes classics such as <i>Time to Go Home</i>, <i>Marriage Made in Heaven</i>, and <i>My Kingdom for a Husband</i>.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Keenly aware of changing times, Sit was active in movie productions, starring in the Cantonese talkie <i>The White Gold Dragon</i> (1933), bringing the theatrical art form to the silver screen. Sit was also politically minded: he was active in the May Fourth Movement, and raised funds for Chinese troops and victims after the Mukden Incident (1931) and after Guangzhou fell (1938). Sit’s final years also saw him serve the National Committee of the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Sit’s disciples went on to become the art form’s biggest stars, including Bak Sheut Sin and Lam Kar Sing.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/35.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":36,"alumni_name":"Chin Kwong Yan (Uncle Chin) ","alumni_name_chi":"錢廣仁（錢大叔）","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1920","chi_alias":"粵曲歌影之才","eng_alias":"Genius of Cantonese Music","chi_desc":"<p>錢廣仁在一九二〇年畢業於聖保羅書院，曾合創鐘聲劇社（今鐘聲慈善社），又創辦香港首家華資唱片公司新月唱片，同爲演員、唱作、編劇、導演，一生推動粵曲發展。</p>\r\n<p>錢氏自小熱衷音樂，十歲起開始硏習奏樂曲，在一九一五年與友人創立鐘聲劇社，當中包括聖保羅同學何澤民。一九二六年，經友人（同爲聖保羅舊生）薛覺先鼓勵獨資開辦新月。當時碰上省港大罷工，廣州劇團未能來港表演，唱片便成爲替代品；同時，社會風氣普遍鼓吹購買國貨，引致新月一時銷量急升。雖戰後倒閉，新月爲戰前粵曲留下珍貴紀錄，亦對香港戰後唱片業發展有相當影響。</p>\r\n<p>錢氏本人也擅粵曲，少年時已擅唱大喉，及後透過新月與一眾粵樂名家合奏錄音。在一九三五年，錢廣仁投身電影業，共參與電影廿八部。</p>\r\n<p>除音樂外，錢氏亦爲體育健將，就讀聖保羅時曾爲中國排球隊代表，前往遠東運動會參賽，在滬時期又活躍於精武體育會。</p>\r\n<p>錢氏熱心培養人材，對傳播粵曲貢獻良多。後人對錢氏評價甚高，他既能接受近代科學槪念及產品，亦熱心推崇傳統音樂文化。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Chin Kwong Yan graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1920. He co-founded the forerunner to the Chung Sing Benevolent Society and founded Hong Kong’s first Chinese-funded record company, New Moon. Chin was an actor, singer-songwriter, screenwriter, and director who dedicated his life to advancing traditional Cantonese music. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chin began learning music when he was ten and co-founded Chung Sing in 1915, one of the other co-founders being his schoolmate, Ho Chak Man. Chin founded New Moon in 1926, with the help of his performer friend (and fellow Pauline) Sit Kok Sin. This coincided with the Canton–Hong Kong Strike; with cancelled performances, records became substitutes. This, plus public support for Chinese goods, meant New Moon experienced early success. Though the label folded shortly after the war, it left behind invaluable recordings of Cantonese music and influenced the industry’s post-war development.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Aside from producing records, Chin himself was a talented singer, specialising in the <I>dahou</I> (大喉 [daai-hau], ‘heroic voice’) style of Cantonese operatic music. He joined the world of film in 1935, involving himself in a total of twenty-eight productions. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chin was also an active sportsman. Back at St. Paul’s, he had already represented China’s volleyball team in the Far East Championship Games and joined the Chin Woo Athletic Association in Shanghai.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chin’s legacy on Cantonese music is palpable. New Moon brought many new talents into the spotlight. Embracing new formats, he brought the art towards a new, preservable medium.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/36.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":37,"alumni_name":"Lau Kwan Yam (aka Lau Shu Shing)","alumni_name_chi":"劉君任（劉樹聲）","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1922","chi_alias":"慈善敎育之士","eng_alias":"Devotee of Arts Education","chi_desc":"<p>劉君任，名樹聲，生於一九〇〇年，在一九二二年曾就讀聖保羅書院，後赴美國深造，爲畫家及敎育家，返港後偕妻創辦志賢女子中學（今已倒閉），在一九三六年創立萬國美術專科學院（今已倒閉），至身故時出任校長。</p>\r\n<p>劉氏兼擅國畫、書法及西洋畫，尤擅繪蝦蟹，透過畫筆及敎育工作，竭盡一生推廣國粹。抗戰爆發時，志賢女子中學活躍於救亡運動，在一九三六年曾辦畫展籌款，爲華北災民賑濟，又製作宣傳救國之油畫。劉氏一生於國內外參與多個畫展，在一九四〇年創中國藝術協會擔任會長，今已解散。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Lau Kwan Yam studied at St. Paul’s during 1922 and then continued his education in the United States. Lau was a painter, Chinese calligrapher, and educator; he co-founded Chi Yin Girls’ College in 1925 with his wife and founded the International Art School (萬國藝專) in 1936, all now defunct. He served as principal of the art school until his death. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lau was proficient across genres, including both Chinese and Western paintings and Chinese calligraphy, with a particular talent for drawing crustaceans. He devoted his life to advancing the Chinese cause: Chi Yin was active in the ‘national salvation’ movement across the 1930s; in 1936, he raised funds for aiding victims of war in northern China and had also produced pro-Chinese art at the time, in the hopes of rousing national consciousness. In 1940, he founded the Chinese Art Association in Hong Kong (now defunct), and his work had been featured in exhibitions both national and international.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/37.jpg","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":38,"alumni_name":"Ng Cho Fan","alumni_name_chi":"吳楚帆","lifespan":"(1911–1993)","grad_year":"1926","chi_alias":"華南影帝","eng_alias":"King of Southern Chinese Films","chi_desc":"<p>吳楚帆生於天津，在一九二六年肄業於聖保羅書院。於電影業享負盛名，早期於一九三七年憑《人生曲》獲譽爲 「華南影帝」。</p> <p>吳氏不忘祖國，在一九三五年出演關文淸執導的《生命線》，於一九三八年拍攝《最後關頭》爲抗戰籌款義演。香港淪陷後，有傳日軍將利用香港人員攝製宣傳片，吳氏不屑被用，隨多人赴廣州灣（今湛江）成立劇團，旨在服務社會及堅定宣傳抗日。</p> <p>戰後初時，粵語片偏重暴力畫面，粗製濫造，吳氏與各影人發起「第三次粵語片淸潔運動」，旨於改善粵語片劇本質素，着重文化倫理，得社會及業界支持，從而改寫戰後香港電影發展。</p> <p>身爲著名演員，吳氏坐有倆金句爲人所知，其一來自一九五三年《危樓春曉》之「人人爲我，我爲人人」及一九六四年《香港屋簷下》之「食碗面，反碗底」。</p> <p>吳氏逝世後，第十二屆香港電影金像獎於同年頒授特別紀念獎，以表揚其對電影業的貢獻。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Tianjin in 1911, Ng Cho Fan studied at St. Paul’s College until 1926 and went on to become one of Hong Kong’s biggest movie stars; his 1937 performance in <i>Song of Life </i>saw him crowned as the ‘King of Southern Chinese film’.</p> <p>As the conflict between China and Japan worsened, Ng starred in movies that supported China. This included <i>Lifeline</i> (1935) and <i>At the Critical Juncture</i> (1938), the latter raising funds for Chinese troops. Ng was one of many who fled to French-controlled Guangzhouwan (now Zhanjiang) as Hong Kong fell, amid rumours that the Japanese were planning to use Hong Kong’s talent for imperialist propaganda. There, he founded a troupe with fellow actors, performing regularly to boost Chinese morale.</p> <p>Shortly after the war ended, Ng and his colleagues started the ‘third Cantonese film cleansing movement’, which aimed to improve screenwriting quality and get rid of cheap, unnecessary violence in contemporary movies. Receiving broad support, the movement changed the course of post-war Hong Kong cinema.</p> <p>Ng also produced two of Hong Kong cinema’s most memorable quotes: 人人爲我，我爲人人 (‘all for each and each for all’), from <i>In the Face of Demolition</i> (1953) and 食碗面，反碗底 (roughly, ‘standing behind someone’s back only to stab them’) from <i>Under Hong Kong’s Roof</i> (1964).</p> <p>Ng was posthumously awarded the Special Commemoration Award at the 12th Hong Kong Film Awards (1993).</p>","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/38.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":39,"alumni_name":"Franklin Wong Wah Kay","alumni_name_chi":"黃華麒","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1961","chi_alias":"《獅子山下》之父","eng_alias":"Founder of ‘Below the Lion Rock’  Series","chi_desc":"<p>黃華麒在一九五六年入讀聖保羅書院，在一九六三年高六畢業，爲香港導演、監製，曾策劃及執導《獅子山下》，又爲香港電台前廣播處長。</p> <p>黃氏年少家住牛頭角，來往聖保羅需時長久，要乘巴士及渡輪，對黃氏而言卻爲温習良機，起初不慣英語敎育的他後來對英語文學深感興趣，爲未來編劇工作奠下基礎。</p> <p>畢業後一九六六年，黃氏加入港台，協助一九七〇年創辦電視部，創作寫實《獅子山下》系列，節目風格是直言不諱，探究當時社會問題，反映香港基層各種抱怨，結果收視極佳，系列成爲香港人集體回憶。</p> <p>一九七四年廉政公署成立，黃氏調至新署，主管媒體宣傳，助廉署於香港坊間立下良好口碑。在一九七七年投身電影業，擔任監製和執導電影十餘齣。在二〇〇八年，黃氏獲委香港電台廣播處長，期間製作高質節目，至二〇一一年退休，二〇一三年重操舊業，策劃兼監製無綫電視寫實劇《我們的天空》。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Franklin Wong entered St. Paul’s College in 1956 and graduated from sixth form in 1963. He is a director, producer, and former broadcasting director of Radio Television Hong Kong (RTHK). Wong is mostly known for creating the drama series <I>Below the Lion Rock</I>.</p> <p>Living in Ngau Tau Kok, in an era before harbour tunnels, the commute took a lengthy two hours. This, however, gave him a chance to catch up with his studies. Reading English classics proved helpful for his marks and inspired his future career.</p> <p>Wong joined RTHK (then known as Radio Hong Kong) in 1966 and helped create its television division. By the 1970s, he was a respected figure, having created the drama series <I>Below the Lion Rock</I>. Portraying the hardships ordinary citizens faced, the series did not shy away from airing public grievances and was widely praised for its social commentary.</p> <p>From 1974 to 1977, Wong also worked for the Independent Commission Against Corruption (ICAC), which had just started up. He helped build the agency’s reputation. Wong later set his sights on directing and producing movies, with over a dozen to his name. He was appointed RTHK director in 2008, during which he continued to produce quality programmes. Wong retired in 2011, but later that year planned and produced the TVB documentary drama <I>Shades of Life</I>, which aired in 2014.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/39.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":40,"alumni_name":"Alfredo So Hau Leung","alumni_name_chi":"蘇孝良","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1964","chi_alias":"音樂金牌經理人","eng_alias":"The Triumphant Manager in Cantonese Pop Musi","chi_desc":"<p>蘇孝良於一九五八年入讀聖保羅書院，在一九六六年於聖保羅書院預科畢業，於音樂界成就過人，曾任香港管弦樂團首任總經理，在一九八〇年代以華星娛樂總經理身份見證香港音樂進入黃金時期，曾爲梅艷芳、林憶蓮、倫永亮、李國祥、梁朝偉、劉喜玲、呂方等藝人之經理人，爲香港文娛發展之要臣。</p> <p>蘇氏於幼年時已展現音樂天賦和興趣，早於中一已獲召爲學校琴師，高六以文學聯會委員的身份籌辦書院音樂會。</p> <p>蘇氏在大學畢業後和藝術中心創辦人盧景文多次於歌劇合作，後獲提名領導香港管弦樂團。任華星總經理時，蘇氏革新經理人制度，又將紅磡體育館打造成樂壇演唱會成就指標。同時，蘇氏亦協助港府制定文化政策相關的工作，曾出任西九龍核心文化藝術設施諮詢委員會財務小組成員。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>So Hau Leung studied at St. Paul’s College between 1958 and 1966. So was the first director of the Hong Kong Philharmonic and a music industry titan. In the 1980s, as general manager of Capital Artists, So witnessed Hong Kong’s entertainment industry enter its golden era; he himself was a manager for Anita Mui, Sandy Lam, Anthony Lun, K.C. Lee, Tony Leung Chiu Wai, Carina Lau, Lui Fong, and other talents. He remains a key figure in Hong Kong’s evolving cultural industry.</p> <p>So demonstrated an ear for music at a young age. He became a College pianist in Form 1 and assisted in organising the College concert while in sixth form, as part of the College’s Arts Association.</p> <p>After graduating from university, So frequently collaborated with Lo King Man, the founder of the Hong Kong Arts Centre, in opera performances, before going on to lead the city’s philharmonic. So’s success at Capital can also be traced back to how he changed the way talent agencies were run in Hong Kong. Under So, the Hong Kong Coliseum became a benchmark of success in Hong Kong’s music industry. So had also helped the government by working as a consultant on the project for the West Kowloon Cultural District.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/40.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":41,"alumni_name":"Dominic Tsim Tak Lung JP","alumni_name_chi":"詹德隆","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1965","chi_alias":"文化傳播之子","eng_alias":"Anchor of English-language Broadcasting Programmes","chi_desc":"<p>詹德隆於聖保羅書院修讀預科，在一九六五年高六畢業，以主持英語廣播節目最廣爲人知，曾就職香港總商會、新鴻基集團，曾任香港中文大學出版社社長，現爲全職顧問。</p>\r\n<p>詹氏就讀聖保羅書院時，老師燃點了他對英語文學的興趣，本校開放氣氛與辯論賽讓他日後選擇英語文學爲大學主修科，奠定了英文寫作的基礎。</p>\r\n<p>赴英肄業，詹德隆先加入英國廣播公司，在一九七六年回流返港，曾任無綫電視新聞報導員及新聞編輯，主持《聽歌學英文》（香港電台）、《Newsline》（亞洲電視）等節目，又於《南華早報》及《信報》撰文。他緊貼社會脈搏，後受政府賞識，曾任中央政策組顧問，於一九九一年獲委太平紳士。在一九九四年成立詹德隆顧問公司，擔任總裁至今，曾受僱於金融業、地產業各大集團。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Dominic Tsim read sixth form at St. Paul’s College, graduating in 1965. He is most known for hosting English-language broadcasting programmes and has also worked for the Hong Kong General Chamber of Commerce, Sun Hung Kai, and is a former director of the Chinese University of Hong Kong Press. He now works as a full-time consultant.</p>\r\n<p>Tsim’s time at St. Paul’s helped him decide that he wanted to work in English media. He said that the staff’s willingness to listen to different points of view and hold school debates helped him become interested in writing and English literature, which he majored in at university.</p>\r\n<p>After furthering his studies in Britain, Tsim first joined the BBC before returning to Hong Kong in 1976. He has worked as a newscaster and news editor for TVB and hosted such programmes as <i>Learning English Through Music</i> (Radio Television Hong Kong) and <i>Newsline</i> (Asia Television), and has written for the <i>South China Morning Post</i> and the <i>Hong Kong Economic Journal</i>. Tsim has been hired by the government as a consultant, and has worked in the Central Policy Unit. He was appointed JP in 1991 and founded his consulting firm, T.L. Tsim & Associates, in 1994, with clients including financial and real estate conglomerates.</p>","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/41.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":42,"alumni_name":"Jimmy Chan Foon Sheung","alumni_name_chi":"陳晃相","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1966","chi_alias":"指揮聲樂家","eng_alias":"Vocal Instructor and Chorus Conductor","chi_desc":"<p>陳晃相在一九六六年畢業於聖保羅書院，爲香港男低中音歌唱家及指揮家，現爲香港中文大學及香港浸會大學聲樂導師。</p>\r\n<p>一九七〇年代起，陳氏常與本港及海外樂團及合唱團合作指揮演出。於一九七三至八三年間，任聖保羅男女中學音樂系系主任。在一九七八成立香港銀禧合唱團，任創團指揮曁音樂總監。赴美考獲碩士學位後，曾於香港大學及香港演藝學院任敎聲樂及指揮，曾任香港兒童合唱團藝術總監、香港聖樂團指揮等，於二〇〇四至二〇一三年間任香港歌劇院合唱團總監兼指揮，又於多項音樂比賽擔當評委，帶領香港合唱界成長，發掘靑年的音樂才華。</p>\r\n<p>致一生於音樂，可由聖保羅日子說起。陳氏自幼熱衷音樂，惟家境欠佳，入讀聖保羅後由吳東老師親自免費敎他小提琴、指揮和唱歌，望陳氏能傳承事業。書院時值一三五週年（一九八六年）時，陳氏應邀指揮校慶音樂會。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Jimmy Chan Foon Sheung graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1966. Chan, a bass-baritone conductor, is currently a vocal instructor at the Chinese University of Hong Kong and the Hong Kong Baptist University.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Since the 1970s, Chan has often performed with and conducted many local and foreign choruses. Between 1973 and 1983, he headed the St. Paul’s Co-educational College music department. He was the Hong Kong Silver Jubilee Singers’ founding conductor and music director. After postgraduate studies in the USA, Chan has taught vocals and conducting at the University of Hong Kong and the Academy for Performing Arts, and has been (among others) the Hong Kong Children’s Choir’s artistic director and the Hong Kong Oratorio Society’s conductor. From 2004 to 2013, he was Opera Hong Kong Chorus’ director and conductor. Chan has also adjudicated many choral/singing competitions, continuously advancing the city’s choral music scene.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This lifelong dedication to music stems from his College days. Chan’s interest in music was apparent at a young age, but he could not afford music lessons. It was Moses Wu at St. Paul’s who gave him free lessons on the violin, chorus, and conducting as a means of passing the torch. Chan returned to his alma mater in 1986 to conduct the College’s 135th anniversary concert.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/42.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":43,"alumni_name":"Koo Tak Ming","alumni_name_chi":"古德明","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1971","chi_alias":"中西文化評論家 ","eng_alias":"Cultural Critic","chi_desc":"<p>古德明生於中山，在一九七二年於聖保羅書院完成初六，爲作家、翻譯、編輯及評論家。曾於香港大學、香港中文大學及香港科技大學任文員及翻譯，在一九八九至一九九一年間曾任《明報月刊》總編輯，及後亦於《指標》雜誌和《讀者文摘》中文版任編輯，又曾於《明報》及《ａｍ７３０》撰寫專欄。</p>\r\n<p>古氏之文章涵蓋政治及言語文化評論，觀察入微，精於疏理中英文常見訛誤，望改善大眾日常用語。</p>\r\n<p>就讀聖保羅時，古氏曾任《文苑》編輯。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Zhongshan, Koo Tak Ming left St. Paul’s after his sixth year in 1972. A writer, translator, and editor, Koo had been part of the administrative/translation staff at the University of Hong Kong, the Chinese University of Hong Kong, and the Hong Kong University of Science and Technology. He was the editor-in-chief of <I>Ming Pao Monthly</I> (1989–91), and has edited <I>Benchmark and Readers’ Digest</I> (Chinese edition). He has also been a columnist for <I>Ming Pao</I> and <I>am730</I>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Koo’s commentary covers language, culture, and politics. Koo is known for having a direct and observant style. He has often pointed out mistakes in both Chinese and English, paying special attention to how language is used and written.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>During his years at St. Paul’s, Koo was an editor for <I>Scope</I>.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/43.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":44,"alumni_name":"Professor Gabriel Kwok Ka Tak MH ","alumni_name_chi":"郭嘉特敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1972","chi_alias":"鋼琴大師","eng_alias":"Phenomenal Pianist","chi_desc":"<p>郭嘉特敎授是鋼琴家，於一九六一年入讀聖保羅書院，至一九七二年畢業，其後獲發獎學金到英國皇家音樂學院深造，師從佳鍾遜（Guy Jonson），後隨路易士堅拿（Louis Kentner）在倫敦學習，在一九八四年加入香港演藝學院任敎，自一九八九年起任鍵盤學系主任，又於西安音樂學院、武漢音樂學院、中國音樂學院等任客席敎授。</p>\r\n<p>郭氏多次於世界各地舉行大師班，於本港、中國與全球各比賽中擔任評審，曾在多國音樂節、夏季音樂營等活動任敎。二〇〇三年，郭氏獲推選爲英國皇家音樂學院院士，港府又於二〇一四年頒予榮譽勛章，表揚郭氏對培育靑年音樂家的貢獻。</p>\r\n<p>郭氏認爲音樂上之成就建基於個人才華及努力。就讀聖保羅書院時，郭氏爲琴師，除自身頻頻練習外亦要爲合唱團伴奏。吳東老師對排練講究認眞與嚴肅，在談笑風生間令許多同學對音樂產生獨特的歸屬感，至今郭氏仍言猶在耳。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Gabriel Kwok entered St. Paul’s College in 1961 and graduated in 1972. A pianist, Kwok won a scholarship to the Royal Academy of Music and studied under Guy Jonson and Louis Kentner. He joined the Hong Kong Academy for Performing Arts in 1984 and has been its head of Keyboard Studies since 1989. He has also been a visiting professor at numerous conservatories, including those in Xi’an, Wuhan, and the China Conservatory.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Kwok has given masterclasses at over twenty-five institutions across the globe and adjudicated local, national, and international music competitions. He has taught at numerous musical festivals and summer schools, as well as collaborated with numerous artists. He was conferred Fellow status by the Royal Academy of Music (FRAM) in 2003. In 2014, the Hong Kong government awarded Kwok the Medal of Honour for services to piano education.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Kwok attributes such high achievement in music to talent and hard work. Growing up in Hong Kong in the 1960s, young pianists were scarce; in addition to his own daily practice, he frequently provided accompaniment for the college choirs. Between Moses Wu’s sternness and the shared laughs during rehearsal, Kwok and his peers found joy and fulfilment in their musical pursuits, leaving an unforgettable impression to this day.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/44.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":45,"alumni_name":"Cheung Man Sun","alumni_name_chi":"張文新","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1971","chi_alias":"十大中文金曲始創父","eng_alias":"Founder of the Top Ten Chinese Gold Songs Award","chi_desc":"<p>張文新在一九七三年於聖保羅書院預科畢業，後入香港電台，成爲知名唱片騎師，始創中文歌曲龍虎榜及十大中文金曲頒獎禮，又爲張學友、劉德華、郭富城、黎明冠以「四大天王」稱號，於本港樂壇名垂靑史。</p>\r\n<p>張氏一家務農，居於上水，在一九六六年小學畢業後憑佳績入讀本校，惟每日來往西環單程需三小時，素來習慣之中文敎育於英文中學難派用場，張氏初時壓力甚大。幸好校內氣氛開放，張氏拾起自信，迅速適應，更考獲奬學金，於中三時因家庭狀況險而輟學，時任班主任甫聞即爲張氏代買課本，還親赴張宅，說服張父三思。憶述當時，張氏直言此事對他一生影響至深。</p>\r\n<p>張氏加入港台後起初任時事節目記者，乃初代烽煙節目助理，爲基層市民表達心聲。自一九七五年起任唱片騎師，之間任入境事務處入境事務主任，在一九八〇年代起轉至節目管理，曾任港台中文台台長及電視部助理廣播處長，至二〇一三年退休。期間港台團隊所製作節目屢獲國際殊榮，奪多個最高收視、收聽及欣賞指數大獎。</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Cheung Man Sun graduated from sixth form at St. Paul’s College in 1973. He is a respected broadcaster for Radio Television Hong Kong (RTHK). Cheung created the Chinese Pop Chart and the Top Ten Chinese Gold Songs Award, and came up with the name ‘Four Heavenly Kings’ for Jacky Cheung, Andy Lau, Aaron Kwok, and Leon Lai. He left an indelible mark on the city’s music scene.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Hailing from Sheung Shui, Cheung entered St. Paul’s in 1966. It was not easy at first: the three-hour commute to Bonham Road plus switching to an English-language school was a recipe for stress, but Cheung eventually found within the College’s permissive culture the confidence to adapt; he even won a scholarship. However, by his third year, his family finances almost forced him to drop out. Upon hearing of this, Cheung’s class teacher bought his textbooks post-haste and went to Sheung Shui herself, convincing Cheung’s father to reconsider. In retrospect, Cheung says this changed his life forever.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Cheung initially joined the RTHK as a reporter and assistant for its early phone-in programmes, lending a voice to the city’s grassroots. He began disc jockeying in 1975 and was also briefly an immigration officer. From the 1980s, Cheung moved to programme management, eventually heading RTHK’s Chinese radio service and serving as assistant broadcasting director before retiring in 2013. His programmes have consistently led ratings and have won awards at home and abroad.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/45.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":46,"alumni_name":"Raymond Fu Yun Kwong","alumni_name_chi":"符潤光","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1971","chi_alias":"音樂敎育家","eng_alias":"Facilitator in Music Education","chi_desc":"<p>符潤光一九七一年畢業於聖保羅書院，在一九八二年重返聖保羅任音樂科主任，至二〇一三年退休。另外曾任聖約翰座堂詩班長、香港歌劇社主席及香港中文大學兼職敎師等職。</p>\r\n<p>符氏爲香港歌劇界的中流砥柱，曾參與多達四十埸音樂劇和歌劇的指揮，曾任香港管弦樂團合唱團、The Hong Kong Singers，以及香港合唱團等指揮。過去二十年，每當籌備及上演大型歌劇時，符氏亦不忘招募聖保羅學生和校友參加活動。另曾於二〇一一年首屆越南國際合唱音樂節擔任評判，現時任世界合唱比賽理事會成員。</p>\r\n<p>就讀聖保羅書院時，當時由吳東老師帶領，一直提供良好的音樂環境，使符氏有機會發揮。符氏曾形容自己角色爲「facilitator（輔翼）」，相信學生在自主的氣氛下參與音樂能得到更大肯定。符氏回母校授敎時，沿襲既有傳統，如讓學生擔當指揮，又擴大樂團數量，涉足中樂，鼓勵學生參加各音樂團體及大型比賽。符氏亦鼓勵學生放眼國際，汲取多國音樂文化，在一六〇週年校慶之際率領合唱團、弦樂團與中樂小組到英格蘭表演。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Raymond Fu graduated from St. Paul’s in 1971, returning to the College in 1982, where he headed our music department until retiring in 2013. Fu was also a former choirmaster at St. John’s Cathedral, the chairman of Hong Kong’s Opera Society, and taught at the Chinese University of Hong Kong. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Fu is a mainstay of the choral music scene in Hong Kong. He has led more than forty musicals and operas, and has collaborated with the Hong Kong Philharmonic Orchestra Choir, the Hong Kong Singers, and the Hong Kong Choir. Over the past two decades, Fu has recruited talent from St. Paul’s for major events, giving students invaluable experience. Beyond Hong Kong, he was an adjudicator at the inaugural Vietnam International Choir Festival (2011). He currently serves on the World Choir Council.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Music in St. Paul’s was already thriving under Moses Wu, where Fu found his lifelong passion. Heading the music department, Fu describes himself as a ‘facilitator’, believing students to thrive best on their own initiative. He continued existing traditions such as having student conductors while continuing to expand the department, notably shining a light on Chinese music. Fu also values learning from other cultures; during our 160th anniversary, Fu led our choir, instrumentalists, and Chinese ensemble to perform in England.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/46.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":47,"alumni_name":"Lawrence Cheng Tan Shui","alumni_name_chi":"鄭丹瑞","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1972","chi_alias":"跨媒體文化人","eng_alias":"Cross-media maestro","chi_desc":"<p>鄭丹瑞，暱稱阿旦，在一九六七年入讀聖保羅書院中一，於一九七四年高六畢業，爲主持、唱片騎師、電影監製、導演、編劇、演員及作家，於娛樂界打滾逾四十載，以構思《小男人週記》系列成名。</p>\r\n<p>大學畢業後加入麗的電視（後來爲亞洲電視，二〇一六年停播），曾參與《天蠶變》、《靑春三重奏》節目製作，在一九八三年任亞洲電視助理創作主任，及後曾於香港電台（《靑春交響曲》、《三個小神仙》）、無綫電視（《十大勁歌金曲頒獎典禮》、香港小姐司儀）和商業電台工作（二台節目總監、後任營運總監、創作總監），自二〇〇〇年代以監製身份再度活躍影壇，當中例子包括《六樓后座》、《見鬼》系列等。其後又開設「健康．旦」網站及影片頻道，內容以健康養生爲主。</p>\r\n<p>鄭氏從小對寫作有興趣，就讀聖保羅書院時期受中文老師啓蒙，曾任《文苑》編輯委員會成員。談起在校園踢西瓜波時仍津津樂道，當時淸晨、小休、午休皆用盡機會踢球。他曾言在人生旅途上所遇的聖保羅學弟皆有一種「得意」的天真，易於辨認。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Lawrence Cheng (nicknamed ‘Ah Tan’) studied at St. Paul’s from 1967 to 1974. A host, disc jockey, film producer, director, screenwriter, actor, and writer, Cheng is a veteran in Hong Kong’s entertainment industry and is known, among other things, as the creator of <i>The Yuppie Fantasia</i> franchise.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Cheng joined Rediffusion Television (RTV, which became Asia Television (ATV), which stopped broadcasting in 2016) after he graduated from college. He worked on such television series as <i>Reincarnated</i> and <i>The Trio</i>, and in 1983, became ATV’s assistant creative director. He has gone on to work at Radio Television Hong Kong (RTHK), TVB (hosting the Jade Solid Gold Best 10 Awards, the Miss Hong Kong Pageant, and others), and Commercial Radio (CR) (first as CR2 director and later as CR’s COO and creative director). Since the 2000s, Cheng has been an active film producer, including 2003’s <i>Truth or Dare: 6th Floor Rear Flat</i> and the horror movie franchise <i>The Eye</i>. He has also created HiEggo, an website / online video channel focusing on health and lifestyle.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>A creative mind since his youth, Cheng found inspiration from his Chinese teachers at St. Paul’s; he once edited the College bulletin Scope. He fondly remembers the College’s watermelon balls, reminiscing on how he would go to the playground whenever possible. Though he has met thousands of people since graduation, Cheng says all of St. Paul’s alumni have an easily-recognisable trait: a ‘charming’ innocence.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/47.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":48,"alumni_name":"Chan Hing Kai (Lip Wang Fung)","alumni_name_chi":"陳慶嘉（聶宏風）","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1977","chi_alias":"影視編製奇才","eng_alias":"Creative Hong Kong Film Producer and Screenwriter","chi_desc":"<p>陳慶嘉（又用筆名聶宏風）在一九七六至一九七七年於聖保羅書院就讀預科，爲編劇、監製及導演，共編劇電影逾四十齣，包括《小男人週記》系列（一九八六年與同爲聖保羅舊生鄭丹瑞合製）、《英雄本色》（一九八六）、《三人世界》（一九八八）、《風塵三俠》（一九九三）、《江湖告急》（二〇〇〇）等。陳氏為現任寰亞電影製作總監，並於浸會大學電影學院任客席講師。</p> <p>一九八一年大學畢業後，先後加入商業電台、無綫電視、香港電台、亞洲電視從事幕後製作，在一九九〇年聯同鄭丹瑞及陳嘉上成立仝人製作社，在二〇〇〇年成立尚品電影。影視廣播以外，在一九九三至一九九五年間曾任文化傳信創作總監，期間決定購入《龍珠》漫畫本港代理出版權。</p> <p>本來個性偏內向的陳氏，適逢書院一二五週年校慶入讀，可謂大開眼界。師生之間坦率、直接，同學之間又團結，或多或少啓發陳氏日後發展方向。雖僅於書院不足兩年，陳氏已活躍於校慶活動，爲當時《文苑》中文部編輯及校刊攝影師。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Chan Hing Kai studied sixth form at St. Paul’s College from 1976 to 1977, and is a screenwriter, producer, and director. He has written/co-written over forty movies, including <i>The Yuppie Fantasia</i> franchise (co-written with fellow Pauline, Lawrence Cheng, since 1986), <i>A Better Tomorrow</i> (1986), <i>Heart to Hearts</i> (1988), <i>Tom, Dick and Hairy</i> (1993), and <i>Jiang Hu: The Triad Zone</i> (2000). He currently heads the film division in the Media Asia Group and is a guest lecturer at the Hong Kong Baptist University Academy of Film.</p>\r\n<p>Since graduating from university in 1981, Chan has worked for Commercial Radio, TVB, Radio Television Hong Kong, and Asia Television, mostly in a creative and production capacity. He co-founded People’s Productions in 1990 with Lawrence Cheng and film director Gordon Chan and founded Icon Pictures in 2000. Outside of film and radio, Chan was also creative director for Culturecom from 1993 to 1995, during which the company bought the local rights to the <i>Dragon Ball</i> manga.</p>\r\n<p>A quiet, bookish character, Chan was in for a shock when he entered St. Paul’s in its 125th anniversary year. Frank, casual, yet united, the College’s atmosphere made Chan more open to new opportunities — despite his short stay, he was a Chinese editor for <i>Scope</i> and a student photographer for the year’s festivities.</p>","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/48.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":49,"alumni_name":"Stephen Ng Chee Sing","alumni_name_chi":"吳智誠","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1990","chi_alias":"國際聲樂敎育大師","eng_alias":"International Vocal Master","chi_desc":"<p>吳智誠在一九九〇年畢業於聖保羅書院。就讀時曾任書院音樂聯會會長，參與合唱團及管弦樂團，現於本港及美國賓州辦聲樂大師班。</p>\r\n<p>自二〇〇二年起，吳氏於美國各大學敎授聲樂，亦於每年暑假赴歐洲演出，在德國及意大利任教聲樂大師班。個人方面，尤以演出巴哈、韓德爾《彌賽亞》及多套神劇聞名。</p>\r\n<p>二〇〇七年，吳氏返回母校，出任書院首位駐校藝術家，指導學生，尤其重視學生能自由試驗，展露熱情及創意。及後在二〇一〇年再度轉至美國敎授聲樂。於二〇一九年曾任嶺南大學駐校音樂家。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Stephen Ng graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1990. His passion for music is evident in his College years. He joined the College choir and college orchestra and was a former chairman of the Music Union. He currently shuttles between Hong Kong and Pennsylvania for his vocal masterclasses.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ng has taught voice in American universities since 2002 and frequently performs in Europe while on leave, opening vocal masterclasses in Germany and Italy. Ng’s solo work encompasses all of Bach’s major works, Handel’s <I>Messiah</I>, and major oratorios.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ng returned to Hong Kong in 2007 and served as the College’s first artist-in-residence until 2010. He taught our students by encouraging them to try new things and follow their creative passions. He passed the torch to the next generation. He resumed his teaching work stateside in 2010. Ng also worked as a resident musician at Lingnan University in 2019.</p>\r\n","category":"artists","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/49.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"}],"craftspeople":[{"id":18,"alumni_name":"Tam Shiu Hong (aka Tam Sze Nin) ","alumni_name_chi":" 譚肇康（譚時秊）","lifespan":"(1875–1961)","grad_year":"1899","chi_alias":"二十世紀香港基建泰斗","eng_alias":"Titan of Hong Kong’s Early-20th-Century Infrastructure Projects ","chi_desc":"<p>譚肇康爲廣東新會人，在一八九八至九九年間就讀聖保羅書院。譚氏一生主領香港多項二十世紀初基建計劃，於一九二〇年創辦香港建造商會（一九二二年曾任主席），又於一九〇六年加入同盟會，曾效力革命。</p> <p>書院畢業後，譚氏決定投身工程界，北赴大連學習。返港後，譚氏於一九〇六年加入同盟會，爲籌餉委員，負責會計稽核等。一九一一年四月黃花崗起義失敗後，譚氏爲一眾收殮者之一，冒死爲七十二烈士合葬。民初時曾獲推舉從政，譚氏婉拒並決定獻生於建築業。</p> <p>譚氏在一九一一年及一九二二年分別創立永利建築公司及裕利建築公司，包辦多項大型發展項目，包括：</p> <ul> <li><p>中環牛奶公司倉庫擴建（一九一〇年代施工，二〇〇九年獲評爲一級歷史建築）</p></li> <li><p>香港電燈北角發電廠（一九一九年竣工，一九八〇年代重建爲城市花園）</p></li> <li><p>中華電力鶴園發電廠（一九二一年竣工，一九九一年拆卸，重建爲海逸豪園）</p></li> <li><p>置地告羅士打行大廈（一九三二年竣工，一九七七年拆卸，今爲置地廣塲一部份）</p></li> <li><p>掃桿埔孔聖堂（一九三五年竣工，二〇二〇年獲評爲一級歷史建築）</p></li> <li><p>香港官立高級工業學院（一九三六年竣工，位於活道，爲「灣仔紅磚屋」之一，一九八八年拆卸，學院爲理工大學前身）。</p></li> </ul> <p>譚氏同時熱心公益，在壬申年（一九三二至三三年）曾任東華三院首總理，於甲戌年（一九三四至三五年）曾任保良局首總理。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Tam Shiu Hong was born in Xinhui, and studied at St. Paul’s College between 1898 and 1899. Tam founded the Hong Kong Construction Association in 1920 (which he chaired in 1922) and led many of the city’s early–twentieth-century infrastructure projects. Having joined the Tongmenghui in 1906, Tam also left a mark in modern Chinese history.</p> <p>After graduating from the College, Tam chose to enter construction, first honing his craft in Russian-held Dalniy (today Dalian). Returning to Hong Kong, he joined the Tongmenghui in 1906, and was assigned to internal bookkeeping work. Following the failed Second Guangzhou Uprising (April 1911), Tam was one of the people who risked life and limb to rebury the uprising’s seventy-two martyrs. Although Tam was offered a position in Sun’s new government, he declined, preferring instead to focus on construction projects.</p> <p>With his two companies, Wing Lee & Co. and Yee Lee & Co., Tam was awarded multiple large-scale building contracts, including:</p> <ul> <li><p>Extensions to the Dairy Farm Depot (1910s; the building was awarded Grade 1 status in 2009);</p></li> <li><p>The North Point power station (Hongkong Electric, completed 1919, rebuilt as City Gardens in the 1980s);</p></li> <li><p>The Hok Un power station (CLP, completed 1921, demolished 1991, rebuilt as Laguna Verde);</p></li> <li><p>The Gloucester Building in Central (completed 1932, demolished 1977, now part of Landmark);</p></li> <li><p>The Confucius Hall in So Kon Po (completed 1935, awarded Grade 1 status in 2020);</p></li> <li><p>The Government Trade School on Wood Road, in Wan Chai (completed 1936, the forerunner to the Polytechnic University; popularly known as the ‘red brick house’, demolished 1988); and so on.</p></li> </ul> <p>Tam was also a philanthropist, having served on the boards of Tung Wah Hospital (1932–33 lunar year) and Po Leung Kuk (1934–35 lunar year).</p>","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/18.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":19,"alumni_name":"Wang Jimin (aka Wang K. Chi-min)","alumni_name_chi":"王吉民","lifespan":"(1889–1972)","grad_year":"1900","chi_alias":"濟世醫學院士","eng_alias":"China’s First Medical Historian","chi_desc":"<p>王吉民生於東莞，爲醫師及醫史學者。在一八九六至一九〇〇年間於聖保羅書院就讀，後於香港西醫學院升學，並於一九一〇年畢業。一九一一年在上海出任中國防疫醫院創院院長，於一九三〇年自設診所。一九三八年，王氏任國立上海醫學院醫學士副敎授，其後獲選國際醫學史學會會員及國際科學史硏究院院士。</p>\r\n<p>王氏於一九一五年加入中華醫學會，爲早期會員之一。五十年來，王氏著有中英論文或文章逾百篇，並在一九二八年出版《中國歷代醫學之發明》。一九三二年，王氏與好友及中國近代醫學家伍連德出版英籍《中國醫史》，爲中國最早之醫學通史硏究專著。一九三七年，王氏獲選副會長，翌年，王氏在上海推動中華醫學會醫史博物館的成立，成爲中國首所醫學博物館，擔任館長至一九六六年該館臨時關閉爲止。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Dongguan, Wang Jimin was a physician and one of modern China’s first medical historians. He studied at St. Paul’s College between 1896 and 1900, later graduating from the Hong Kong College of Medicine in 1910. In 1911, he was the founding director of one of China's first quarantine hospitals, and in 1930, he established his own practice. In 1938, Wang was appointed assistant professor of medicine at the National Shanghai Medical College and was later inducted into the International Society for the History of Medicine and the International Academy of the History of Science.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Wang joined the Chinese Medical Association (CMA) in 1915, becoming one of its earliest members. He was a prolific academic, authoring over 120 articles and works over his five-decade career, including 1928’s <i>Zhongguo Lidai Yixue zhi Faming </i>(‘Medical innovations throughout Chinese history’) and 1932’s <i>History of Chinese Medicine</i>, the latter of which was co-written with his friend, Wu Lien-teh, one of China’s first modern health scientists, and marked China’s English-language entry into the medical history field. He was nominated a CMA vice president in 1937, and in 1938 he founded China’s first medical history museum in Shanghai, serving as its curator until its temporary closure in 1966.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/19.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":20,"alumni_name":"​Professor Lamb Ping Yin","alumni_name_chi":"林炳賢敎授","lifespan":" (1901–1986)","grad_year":"1918","chi_alias":"現代建築敎育家","eng_alias":"Modernist Architecture Educator ","chi_desc":"<p>林炳賢在一九一八年畢業於聖保羅書院，爲建築師及建築敎授。自一九二九年起，林氏於交通大學唐山工學院（簡稱唐院，今西南交通大學）敎授建築，直至一九四九年返港，林氏仍繼續建築工作。</p>\r\n<p>林氏於美國北俄亥俄大學受訓，後又考獲英國皇家建築師學會會員資格。一九二〇年代，林氏於天津任工程師，開設林泰工程公司。一九二九年，林氏加入唐院任副敎授，在抗戰時學院曾搬至貴州，及後獲擢升爲建築系主任，合共授敎近二十載。據學生憶述，林氏授課淸晰易明，爲人公正，和藹可親，孕育一代中國建築師，其學生包括城市規劃家及淸華敎授鄭孝燮、中國工程院資深院士佘峻南等人，後者讚譽林氏「帶我進入現代主義建築世界中去，爲我奠下牢固的基礎」。</p>\r\n<p>林氏返港後獲註冊建築師資格，加入Hazeland & Co.建築師事務所。香港戰後經濟蓬勃，林氏曾設計學校、唐樓、工廠等。一九六一年，林氏設計的重慶大廈於尖沙咀落成，見證林氏對香港建築之雄心。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>P.Y. Lamb graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1918. A US-trained architect, from 1929, Lamb taught architecture at Tangshan Engineering College, Chiao-tung University, then known as the ‘Cornell of the East’. Lamb returned to Hong Kong in 1949, where he continued his architectural work. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lamb trained in the USA, obtaining his degree from Ohio Northern University, and later qualified as a Royal Institute of British Architects (RIBA) member. Originally practising in Tianjin, Lamb joined Tangshan Engineering College in 1929 as an assistant professor, where he taught for nearly twenty years, rising to lead the architecture department. The school moved to Guizhou during the Second Sino-Japanese War. In teaching, Lamb was known for his clarity, fairness, and generosity, allowing a generation of Chinese architects to find their footing. Students included Tsinghua lecturer and urban planner Zheng Xiaoxie and senior Chinese Academy of Engineering member She Junnan, who credited Lamb with ‘bringing me into the world of modernist architecture’.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> Lamb returned to Hong Kong in 1949, where he joined Hazeland & Co. During Hong Kong's post-war boom, the company built schools, homes, and factories, helping to shape the city’s look. Among his works was Chungking Mansions (1961) in Tsim Sha Tsui, a reflection of the city’s unfettered ambition and optimism.</p>\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/20.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":21,"alumni_name":"Ieoh Ming Pei (I.M. Pei)","alumni_name_chi":"貝聿銘","lifespan":"(1917–2019)","grad_year":"1927","chi_alias":"現代主義建築末代大師","eng_alias":"Master of Late-modernist Architecture ","chi_desc":"<p> 貝聿銘於聖保羅書院就讀至一九二七年，爲美藉華裔建築師，著作有一九八〇年代法國巴黎羅浮宮金字塔、一九七〇年代美國波士頓甘迺迪總統圖書館、美國華盛頓國家藝廊東館、一九八〇年代香港中銀大廈【註】、一九八二年北京香山飯店等，以結合現代主義根底、幾何圖案、天然採光、迎合項目本身場景爲特色。一九八三年，貝氏贏得普立兹克獎。</p>\r\n<p>貝氏於十歲時離開香港，一家遷居上海，暑假時則回蘇州祖家。貝氏曾言一生作品受其少時在中國的經驗而啓發：香港賦予他認識文藝的根基，上海的國際氣氛釀成他赴美的決定，與自然融和的設計來自蘇州的園林，而華夏文化使他設計時不忘以人爲本。</p>\r\n<p>一九三五年，貝氏赴美，在一九五五年創辦自身建築事務所。貝氏以一九六一年設計的美國國家大氣硏究中心科羅拉多州梅薩實驗室成名。一九八三年，貝氏受時任法國總統米特朗委託，任羅浮宮博物館翻新工程建築師。起初公佈廣場將築起玻璃金字塔時引起譁然，但隨時日過去設計漸得接受，在一九八九年落成後廣受褒揚。後期多數以設計博物館爲主，包括蘇州博物館新館，及於澳門科學館擔任設計顧問。</p>\r\n<p>雖然貝氏作品沒有單一風格，但亦可見共通之處。一如梅薩實驗室設計具迷宮式氛圍，又如華盛頓東館、羅浮宮、香山飯店均設大型中庭，可見作品着重人與人之間的互動。後三者又用玻璃天窗引入天然光，開放設計有助疏導訪客。同時，各建築亦反映本身功能：東館富現代主義色彩，反映館內展覽以現代藝術爲主；羅浮宮金字塔襯托宮庭固有宏偉；而香山飯店爲貝氏較保守的設計，不致蓋過附近香山景色。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>【註】　中銀大廈指一九九〇年落成之摩天大廈，並非指滙豐總行旁，一九五〇年落成之中國銀行大廈。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p> I.M. Pei studied at St. Paul’s College until 1927. A Chinese-American architect, Pei’s works include the Louvre Pyramid (Paris, 1983–89), the JFK Library (Boston, 1976–79), the National Gallery of Art’s East Building (Washington, DC, 1968–78), the Bank of China Tower (Hong Kong, 1982–90), and the Fragrant Hills Hotel (Beijing, 1982). He is known for his striking blend of modernist sensibilities with geometric motifs and use of natural light, while paying close attention to the unique context of each project. He won the Pritzker Prize in 1983. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> Raised in Hong Kong, Pei’s family moved to Shanghai when he was ten, and he also spent time in his native Suzhou. Later, he credited his Chinese upbringing as key inspiration: Hong Kong gave him his literary foundation, Shanghai’s internationalism influenced his decision to travel to the United States in 1935, Suzhou’s gardens inspired his emphasis on nature, and Chinese culture informed his human-centric approach. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> Pei first gained recognition for his 1961 design of the National Center for Atmospheric Research’s Mesa Laboratory. In 1983, Pei was approached by French President François Mitterand to plan the Louvre’s renovation. Despite initial controversy over the proposed glass pyramid, it garnered praise upon completion in 1989. Later works include Suzhou Museum’s new building and as a consultant for the Macau Science Center.\r\n </p>\r\n\r\n<p> While Pei’s output is difficult to categorise, there are shared characteristics. Human interactions are encouraged, be it via the Mesa Laboratory’s maze-like atmosphere or the large atriums of the East Building, the Louvre, and the Fragrant Hills Hotel. Form follows context: the East Building’s modernism stems from its service as a modern art gallery; the Louvre Pyramid complements the palace’s grandeur, and the Fragrant Hills Hotel was decidedly traditional, so as not to overshadow the former imperial garden. </p>\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/21.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":22,"alumni_name":"Szeto Wai CBE","alumni_name_chi":"司徒惠","lifespan":"(1913–1991)","grad_year":"1931","chi_alias":"香港近代建築工程之父 ","eng_alias":"1931屆畢業 — 香港近代建築工程之父 ","chi_desc":"<p>司徒惠在一九三一年畢業於聖保羅書院。身爲建築師及工程師，司徒氏是香港近代建築設計之先驅，代表作包括一九六〇年代香港中文大學校舍及於一九六六年重建的皇后像廣場。</p>\r\n<p>司徒氏在聖保羅書院求學期間，在一九三〇年任宿生會主席，畢業後赴上海聖約翰大學硏修。於一九三八年赴英國深造，專攻工程學，考取特許工程師資格。在一九四五年回國，曾參與長江三峽工程勘察與計劃。於一九四八年返港，成立司徒惠建築師事務所。</p>\r\n<p>司徒氏的建築多具現代主義風格，設計方面着重實用，建築物以混凝土爲主，多見長型，故具空間感。司徒氏擅長配合香港陡峭地勢，他於中大校園的規劃中最能體現其優點。除中大校舍外，司徒氏亦參與建築香港大學柏立基學院、西環觀龍樓、筲箕灣明華大廈、京士柏循道衞理聯合敎會九龍堂及循道學校（今循道中學所用）。</p>\r\n<p>從書院畢業多年後，司徒氏仍心繫母校。本校一九六〇年代重建計劃中，史伊尹及史璧琦校長與司徒氏合作，新校舍便出自他手筆，落成後大樓大多屹立至今。</p>\r\n<p>除土木工程及建築設計方面之成就，司徒氏亦熱心政治，希望盡自己一份綿力，回饋社會。他曾任立法局議員（一九六五至七二年）及行政局議員（一九七二至七六年），並擔任政府交通諮詢委員會主席、工務小組委員會委員、香港大學校董等。在一九七四年獲授CBE勛章。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p> Szeto Wai graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1931. Szeto was a pioneer in Hong Kong's modern urban landscape. He was a skilled architect and chartered engineer. Representative works include the Chinese University of Hong Kong (CUHK) campus (1963–69) and Statue Square (1966). </p>\r\n\r\n<p>While at St. Paul’s, Szeto was president of the Boarders’ Union in 1930. He furthered his studies in Shanghai and Britain, qualifying as a chartered engineer. Returning to China in 1945, Szeto took part in surveying and planning a potential dam for the Three Gorges. He returned to Hong Kong in 1948 and founded his own firm, W. Szeto & Partners. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>As exemplified on CUHK’s campus, Szeto’s output is characteristically modernist — with functional design, long, straight structures, and heavy use of concrete — while deftly accommodating Hong Kong’s mountainous terrain, thanks to his engineering background. Other works include the University of Hong Kong’s (HKU) Robert Black College, Kwun Lung Lau in Kennedy Town, the Kowloon Methodist Church, and Methodist College in King’s Park.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Another client of Szeto’s was his own alma mater. Working with principals Evan Stewart and Geoffrey Speak, Szeto was the architect behind the Campus Reconstruction Project. Completed throughout the 1960s, most of the buildings stand to this day. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>In addition to his engineering and architectural legacies, Szeto also left a mark in politics. He once chaired the government transport advisory committee, sat on the HKU council, and on both the Legislative Council (1965–74) and the Executive Council (1972–76); he was appointed CBE in 1974.</p>\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/22.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":23,"alumni_name":"Victor Chang Yam Him AC","alumni_name_chi":"張任謙","lifespan":" (1936–1991)","grad_year":"1952","chi_alias":"心臟移植醫師","eng_alias":"Cardiothoracic Surgeon","chi_desc":"<p>張任謙生於上海，在一九五〇至五二年間就讀聖保羅書院，於一九五三年移居澳洲，爲心肺外科醫生，致一生推動亞太地區心臟手術發展。</p>\r\n<p>由悉尼大學畢業後，張氏先後於英美受訓，後返悉尼聖文森特醫院行醫。在一九八四年，張氏成立澳洲首個心臟移植中心，至去世時張氏團隊已完成超過二百五十次心臟移植手術。見及心臟捐贈者不敷，張氏致力硏發低成本人工心瓣膜與人工心臟。</p>\r\n<p>張氏同時淸楚國際醫術交流之重要，在一九七八年起頻與中國醫生合作，贊助中國醫生赴澳訓練。張氏曾為設立印尼及日本國家心臟手術中心擔任顧問。於一九八二年，張氏獲委澳中理事會成員，致力促進兩國外交關係，在一九八六年獲澳洲政府頒授ＡＣ（澳洲同伴）勛章。</p>\r\n<p>一九九一年，張氏被匪徒意圖綁架勒索，期間不幸遇害。新南威爾士州爲他擧行州葬，至今洋名Victor Chang仍於澳洲家傳戶曉。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Shanghai, Victor Chang studied at St. Paul’s College between 1950 and 1952. A Chinese-born Australian heart and lung (cardiothoracic) surgeon, Chang moved to Australia in 1953 and dedicated his life to advancing heart surgery across the Asia-Pacific.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Graduating from the University of Sydney, Chang trained across the UK and at the USA’s Mayo Clinic, though it was his work at Sydney’s St. Vincent’s Hospital that remains the most remembered. In 1984, he founded the National Heart Transplant Programme, an Australian first. Until his death, Chang’s team performed over 250 heart transplants. Recognising the need to reduce reliance on donors, Chang researched and developed low-cost artificial heart valves and artificial hearts. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chang also knew the importance of international medical exchange. From 1978, he worked closely with Chinese doctors, sponsoring them to train in Australia. He has additionally advised Indonesia and Japan on setting up their heart transplant programmes. He was appointed to the Australia–China Council in 1982, improving Sino-Australian ties. In 1986, he was appointed a Companion of the Order of Australia.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chang was murdered in 1991, the victim of a failed extortion attempt. He was given a state funeral from the New South Wales government and remains a household name in Australia today.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/23.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":24,"alumni_name":"Anthony Lee Kai Yiu","alumni_name_chi":"李繼堯","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1958","chi_alias":"醫學敎育專才","eng_alias":"Expert in rheumatology ","chi_desc":"<p>李繼堯在一九五一年入讀聖保羅書院，於一九五八年畢業，現任仁安醫院院長及醫務總監，專門研究風濕病學。在二〇一二至一八年間曾出任香港私家醫院聯會主席。</p>\r\n<p>李氏畢業聖保羅書院後考獲政府獎學金，就讀於香港大學醫學院，大學畢業後任教於港大。後考獲英聯邦獎學金前往澳洲深造，一九七四至七五年間在美國硏究免疫細胞和癌症。回港後，李氏於瑪麗醫院擔任內科顧問，在一九七八年起於香港及墨爾本私人執業，最後自一九九六年起出任現職。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Anthony Lee graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1958. He is currently the manager and director of Union Hospital and is a practitioner himself, specialising in rheumatology. Between 2012 and 2018, Lee chaired the Hong Kong Private Hospitals Association. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>After graduating from the College, Lee was awarded a government scholarship to study medicine at the University of Hong Kong. After graduating, Lee lectured at his alma mater. Lee later went to Australia on a Commonwealth scholarship and took part in immunocyte and cancer research in the USA from 1974 to 1975, returning to Hong Kong thereafter. Lee has since served as a consultant for Queen Mary Hospital and opened his private practice in 1978, with offices in Hong Kong and Melbourne. He was appointed to his current position in 1996. </p>\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/24.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":25,"alumni_name":"Patrick Lau Sau Shing SBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"劉秀成敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1961","chi_alias":"城規建築大師","eng_alias":"城規建築大師","chi_desc":"<p>劉秀成生於澳門，在一九五八年入讀聖保羅書院，於一九六三年高六畢業，爲建築師、建築學家及前立法會議員（建築、測量及都市規劃界，二〇〇四至一二年），一九七三年加入香港大學建築系，在一九九六至二〇〇〇年間任系主任，二〇〇六年獲頒名譽大學院士銜，作品以校舍爲主。</p>\r\n<p>一九六九年，劉氏在加拿大考獲建築學士後，曾短暫留加，返港後承辦多項建築項目，涉足古蹟保育、市區重建、城市規劃、學校及社區建設，當中包括法國國際學校、香港國際學校（大潭校舍）、三棟屋博物館、西島中學、香港澳洲國際學校、香港知專設計學院曾獲獎。於二〇〇〇年獲特區政府頒銀紫荊星章、及二〇〇四年獲委太平紳士。</p>\r\n<p>劉氏亦一直關注書院發展，身為現任校董，曾操手設計書院二〇〇六年啓用之新南翼大廈及二〇一三年啓用之小學部薄扶林道新校舍。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Macau, Patrick Lau attended St. Paul’s College in 1958 and graduated from sixth form in 1963. Lau is an architect, a professor of architecture, and has sat on the Legislative Council from 2004 to 2012 (Architectural, Surveying, and Planning functional constituency). He began teaching architecture at the University of Hong Kong in 1973 and headed the department from 1996 to 2000. He was conferred an honorary fellowship in 2006. He is most known for his school campus designs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After completing his undergraduate studies in Canada, Lau returned to Hong Kong, where he worked on a diverse range of projects, including heritage conservation, urban rebuilding, urban planning, schools, and community projects. In particular, his work on the Lycée Français International de Hong Kong, Hong Kong International School (Tai Tam), Sam Tung Uk Museum, West Island School, and the Australian International School Hong Kong have garnered awards. Lau was additionally awarded the SBS in 2000 and appointed JP in 2004.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lau has also been active in College affairs and is a current council member. He played a part in designing the College’s South Wing (opened in 2006) and the Primary School’s Pokfulam Road campus (opened in 2013). </p>\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/25.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":26,"alumni_name":"Ho Chung Ping MH JP","alumni_name_chi":"何仲平","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1967","chi_alias":"香港醫學會前會長","eng_alias":"The Former President of the Hong Kong Medical Association","chi_desc":"<p>何仲平生於一九五〇年，在一九六七年畢業於聖保羅書院，爲腎科專科醫生，於二〇一八至二〇年間曾任香港醫學會會長。</p>\r\n<p>何氏在瑪麗醫院任實習外科醫生時，受操刀泌尿科的上司梁智鴻啓發，從此踏上腎科專科之路。行醫四十年間，何氏緊跟科技發展，望醫療與資訊科技相輔相成，曾參與政府醫健通設定及推廣，讓病人紀錄互通公私營醫護機構，又曾任器官捐贈推廣委員會委員。此外，何氏又開設「腎臟敎室」網站，提供參考資料裨益大眾，增進社會對腎臟健康認知；近年則着手硏究多囊腎診症及治療方法（多囊腎爲基因變異病，現今尚未有公認療法）。二〇〇七年獲頒榮譽勳章，二〇一〇年獲委太平紳士。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>除腎科外，何氏亦有硏究護理及物理治療，事緣女兒二〇〇三年中風，倖存後嘗試用新方式助她康復。有見醫療科系隨時間發展逐漸專科化，何氏寄語下一代醫科生除鑽硏專門以外，亦要留意兼具備別科之基礎知識。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Ho Chung Ping graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1967. A kidney (nephrology) specialist, Ho is a former president of the Hong Kong Medical Association (2018–20). </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The inspiration to enter nephrology came while Ho was a houseman at Queen Mary Hospital, shadowing urologist Edward Leong. Over the past four decades, Ho has striven to better his field with technological developments. He is an early adopter and advocate for eHealth, an electronic medical record system that aids record sharing between public and private practices, and has previously served on the government organ donation promotion committee. He has also run ‘The Kidney Classroom’, a kidney health website, allowing easier access to medical information. More recently, Ho has been doing research on how to diagnose and possibly treat polycystic kidney disease, a genetic disease for which there is no known treatment yet. He was awarded the Medal of Honour in 2007 and appointed JP in 2010. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Outside of nephrology, Ho has retained an interest in patient care and physiotherapy after his daughter suffered a life-threatening stroke in 2003. As medical education in Hong Kong matures, Ho observes that medical schools have become increasingly compartmentalised; he hopes the next generation of medics can not only further their own fields but also study broadly and learn from neighbouring fields.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/26.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":27,"alumni_name":"Freddie Fu Ho Keung","alumni_name_chi":"傅浩強","lifespan":"(1950–2021)","grad_year":"1968","chi_alias":"骨科運動醫學賢才","eng_alias":"Orthopaedics and Sports Medicine Specialist","chi_desc":"<p>傅浩強在一九六八年畢業於聖保羅書院，後赴美升學，爲骨科醫生，專門運動醫學，一九八六年於賓夕凡尼亞州匹兹堡大學醫學院創辦運動醫學中心，於大學田徑系任首席隊醫卅二年，團隊曾醫治過萬人，由業餘運動員至芭蕾舞者至奧林匹克選手不等，其中在二〇一七年曾主理醫治瑞典國腳伊巴謙莫域右膝。其兄傅浩堅同爲本校舊生。\r\n<p>傅氏尤擅膝部手術，專門硏究修復前十字韌帶，畢生合著／著作逾六百五十篇經同儕評閱之論文，帶領匹兹堡大學於此領域領先全球，出版物廣受引用。</p>\r\n<p>傅氏居匹兹堡逾卅年，爲人親善、關懷備至，一生培訓數千名來自全球各地的醫生，不論族裔與性別，其部門均爲全美最多元化之一。</p>\r\n<p>傅氏一生熱心體育可由少時說起，就讀聖保羅時曾領甲組籃球隊於一九六九至七〇年度奪全港校際冠軍。離校多年，傅氏仍以「聖保羅」身份爲榮，畢生珍重良師及美好回憶。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Freddie Fu graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1968 and later furthered his studies in the USA. An orthopaedics and sports medicine specialist, Fu founded and chaired the University of Pittsburgh’s sports medicine programme since 1986, and was the university’s athletics department’s head team physician for thirty-two years. His team has served tens of thousands of patients, from amateur athletes to ballet dancers to Olympians alike; one high-profile patient of Fu’s was Swedish striker Zlatan Ibrahimović, after his 2017 right knee injury. His older brother, Frank, also studied at St. Paul’s. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Fu’s own research focuses on the knee, specifically anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) reconstruction. Throughout his lifetime, he wrote/co-wrote over 650 peer-reviewed articles, and led the University of Pittsburgh to become the world’s most prolific and widely-cited institution in ACL research. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Over three decades in Pittsburgh, Fu was known for his devotion and initiative, having trained thousands of students from all over the globe. His department is one of the most diverse in the US, both in terms of ethnicity and gender. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Fu’s love of sport began at a young age. While at St. Paul’s, he led the A grade basketball team to victory in the 1969–70 inter-school championship. Many years later, Fu remains proud of his College years, treasuring good teachers and good memories. </p>\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/27.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":28,"alumni_name":"Professor Jim Chi Yung","alumni_name_chi":"詹志勇敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1970","chi_alias":"綠化環境敎育專才","eng_alias":"Leader in Urban Biogeographical Research","chi_desc":"<p>詹志勇在一九七〇年畢業於聖保羅書院，爲世界領先城市生物地理學硏究人員之一。他的硏究涉及城市林業和城市綠化、城市設計和景觀規劃、土壤科學以及休閒和旅遊之環境影響，地理重點爲香港和華南地區。詹氏現於香港敎育大學任敎。</p>\r\n<p>除涉獵硏究和講學外，詹教授亦積極推廣古樹保育，以及培育城市綠化及樹木保育理念的需要。對城市綠化過程的不懈奉獻，爲他贏得「樹醫」的美譽。</p>\r\n<p>詹氏喜歡回憶他在聖保羅書院的時光，猶記得那些給他未來職業生涯靈感的老師，昔時書院的花園萌發了他對環境保護的興趣。</p>\r\n<p>詹氏敦促青少年選擇未來的道路時，要追隨自己的內心，而非盲目追求名利。以己爲例，他回憶自己當時陷入選擇行醫或地理之間的困境，他最終選擇了追隨自己的志向。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Jim Chi Yung graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1970. He is one of the world’s leading urban biogeographical researchers. His research spans multiple disciplines, including urban forestry and greening, urban design and landscape planning, soil science, and the environmental impacts of recreation and tourism, with a geographical focus on Hong Kong and the southern China region. Jim currently lectures at the Education University of Hong Kong. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jim is an arborist in addition to doing research and giving lectures. He is a strong supporter of Hong Kong's historic trees, their preservation and conservation, and greening cities for the future. His relentless devotion to the city’s greening process earned him the sobriquet ‘tree whisperer’.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jim fondly reminisces of his time at St. Paul’s College. He vividly remembers the teachers who gave him inspiration for his future career. The former college gardens may have sown the seeds for his environmental conservation work.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Jim tells teenagers that when it comes to their futures, they should follow their hearts instead of blindly going after fame. Using himself as an example, he recalls his dilemma between practising medicine or geography. In the end, he chose to follow his passion and choose the latter.</p>\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/28.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":29,"alumni_name":"Professor Raymond Liang Hin Suen SBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"梁憲孫敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1972","chi_alias":"血液腫瘤專家","eng_alias":"Haematological Oncologist ","chi_desc":"<p>梁憲孫於一九六一年入讀聖保羅書院小一，一九七二年畢業，曾任香港大學血液及腫瘤科講座敎授，在二〇〇七至〇八年間任港大醫學院院長，自二〇一一年起任養和醫院副院長。一九九一年創立香港骨髓捐贈基金，在二〇〇一年參與成立香港血癌基金，其後於二〇一三年獲委太平紳士、二〇一五年獲頒銀紫荊星章。</p>\r\n<p>梁氏憶述校園生活讓他有充足課餘時間閱讀，當時又結識良朋益友，同學之間相互扶持，縱然興趣不同卻造成契機。受同儕郭嘉特啓發而一起參加當時新辦的香港藝術節，培育了他對文學和古典音樂的興趣。</p>\r\n<p>雖曾獲海外大學取錄，又曾於一九八〇年代赴倫敦深造，梁氏仍心繫香港，見香港缺乏人手，始返香港，親手培育下一代本地醫生之餘亦發起基金，支援有需要病人。</p>\r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Raymond Liang entered our Primary School in 1961 and graduated in 1972. Liang is a former chair professor in haematology and oncology at the University of Hong Kong (HKU) and was the medical faculty’s dean during 2007–08. Since 2011, Liang has been an assistant medical superintendent at the Hong Kong Sanatorium and Hospital. Liang has helped found the Hong Kong Marrow Match Foundation in 1991 and the Hong Kong Blood Cancer Foundation in 2001. He was appointed JP in 2013 and awarded the SBS in 2015. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Liang remembers academic stress being manageable at St. Paul’s, meaning he had plenty of time to read and make friends. While students had vastly different interests, there was a strong sense of camaraderie and support. At the time, Liang was encouraged to go to the Hong Kong Arts Festival by his classmate Gabriel Kwok. This was the start of his lifelong interest in classical music.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Though Liang had an offer from overseas, he ultimately decided to study medicine in Hong Kong. While furthering his studies in London, Liang saw Hong Kong in need of medical personnel. He thus headed back in 1985, joining HKU’s teaching staff to guide the next generation and founding trusts and foundations to help patients in need.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/29.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":30,"alumni_name":"Lo Wing Lok JP","alumni_name_chi":"勞永樂 ","lifespan":"(1954–2015) ","grad_year":"1972","chi_alias":"香港首名傳染病明醫","eng_alias":"The First Expert on Infectious Diseases of Hong Kong","chi_desc":"<p>勞永樂在一九六一年入讀聖保羅書院小一，於一九七二年畢業。勞氏爲香港少數傳染病科醫生之一，以一九九七年禽流感爆發時獻策殺雞爲人所知，實行後疫情得以遏止。勞氏後於二〇〇〇至二〇〇四年間任香港醫學會會長，兼任立法會醫學界功能組別議員。</p>\r\n<p>受書院服務精神感動，勞氏選擇攻讀冷門難學的傳染病科。赴英深造後，返港時成爲唯一一名傳染病醫生。勞氏決心無私奉獻，除一九九七年外亦於二〇〇三年沙士疫情與二〇〇九年豬流感疫情積極提出意見，用以回饋社會。在二〇〇三年獲委太平紳士。</p>\r\n<p>勞氏從政後曾任多個政府委員會委員，偶於《東方日報》撰寫專欄。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Lo Wing Lok entered St. Paul’s College Primary School in 1961 and graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1972. As an expert on infectious diseases, Lo came to the public's attention during the 1997 avian flu outbreak. He suggested culling chickens to stop the spread of the disease, which helped stop it from getting worse. From 2000 to 2004, Lo was president of the Hong Kong Medical Association and served in the Legislative Council (Medical Functional Constituency).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Inspired by the College’s spirit of service, Lo made the decision to branch out into infectious diseases, a rare specialty; when he returned to Hong Kong from Britain, he was the city’s only such specialist. Lo’s service extended beyond the 1997 outbreak; he also gave advice during the 2003 Sars outbreak and the 2009 swine flu outbreak. He was appointed JP in 2003.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lo had also served on multiple government committees and was a columnist for <I>Oriental Daily</I>. <p>\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/30.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":31,"alumni_name":"Professor David Hui Shu Cheong BBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"​許樹昌敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1978","chi_alias":"公共衞生要臣","eng_alias":"Prime Public Health Consultant","chi_desc":"<p>許樹昌於一九七四年轉讀聖保羅書院，在一九七八年畢業，現任香港中文大學醫學院內科及藥物治療系系主任，香港沙士疫情爆發期間於沙田威爾斯親王醫院任呼吸科主管，在前線工作。二〇一九年起新冠肺炎疫情期間任政府專家顧問，爲香港公共衞生及控疫工作要臣。其弟許樹源同爲本校舊生。</p>\r\n<p>許氏主要從事臨牀硏究工作，其硏究主要圍繞呼吸疾病，包括描述症狀及診治方法。至今合著／著作論文逾三百五十篇，爲呼吸學科最受引用作者之一。二〇一七年，許氏獲特區政府頒授銅紫荊星章，於二〇二二年獲委太平紳士。</p>\r\n<p>許氏踏上從醫之路，由家說起。其祖母患哮喘，祖父早逝，後推測因肺結核亡故，又見此病依舊常見，激發許氏懸壺濟世。</p>\r\n<p>當年決定轉至聖保羅，許氏坦言只想盡情踢球，但四年間所得遠不止足球，還結識後來一同從醫的朋友。許氏至今亦盡量撥時間參與本校活動，先前一七〇週年校慶亦曾出席活動。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>David Hui transferred to St. Paul’s College in 1974 and graduated in 1978. He is in charge of the medicine and therapeutics department in the Faculty of Medicine at the Chinese University of Hong Kong. During the 2002–03 Sars outbreak, Hui was on the front lines, heading the respiratory medicine department in Sha Tin’s Prince of Wales Hospital. Throughout the Covid-19 pandemic, Hui has served as a government expert adviser, shaping the city’s public health policy and virus response efforts. His younger brother, Ron, also studied at St. Paul’s.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Hui’s research is primarily clinical and centres around respiratory illnesses, including their effects and treatment. Having written/co-written over 350 publications, he is among the most-cited researchers in the field. He was awarded the BBS in 2017 and appointed JP in 2022.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>The decision to practise medicine came because of family. Hui’s grandmother suffered from asthma, and his grandfather died young; after determining it was likely to have been tuberculosis and seeing the disease remain a common affliction, Hui was determined to do what he could to help.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>As for the decision to transfer, Hui says it was primarily because he liked playing football. Yet Hui got more than football in his student years — lifelong friendships with Pauline were another gain. Hui retains close ties with the College, and has attended our 170th anniversary celebrations.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/31.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":32,"alumni_name":"Professor Ron Hui Shu Yuen ​","alumni_name_chi":"許樹源敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1979","chi_alias":"電子工程科硏大匠","eng_alias":"Pioneer in Electronics Engineering","chi_desc":"<p>許樹源在一九七九年畢業於聖保羅書院，爲電子工程師，以改進無線​充電技術成名，硏究成果爲全球首個無線充電標準Qi之基礎，現今大多智能電話所用。此外，許氏又着手改善發光二極管（ＬＥＤ）照明系統，延長電燈壽命，令其成爲眾多照明系統中最具效益，最環保之選項。其兄許樹昌同爲本校舊生。</p>\r\n<p>許氏曾任香港大學和香港城市大學電機及電子系講座敎授，現任倫敦帝國學院講座敎授及新加坡南洋理工大學敎授，又爲電機電子工程師學會成員以及英國皇家工程院院士等。許氏硏究成果豐碩，至今已合著／著作硏究論文逾五百篇，逾一百二十項專利廣受業界應用。</p>\r\n<p>硏究方面，許氏以實用性及長遠發展爲本。無線充電科技早已存在，許氏硏發的方式令裝置之間能自由擺放，又改善安全，使之通用；前文提及改善ＬＥＤ應用又爲另一例子。</p>\r\n<p>許氏認爲科硏應多重視人與創意，讓硏究人員有充足自由探索新槪念，不受短期目標所限，慨歎香港既缺乏資金，又不願意投資較高風險、較高潛能之硏究計劃。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Ron Hui graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1979. As an electronics engineer, Hui is best known for making progress in the field of wireless power transmission. His work led to Qi, the world’s first wireless power standard, which is widely used in smartphones today. Another area of Hui’s research is in LED systems, where he is improving their longevity to make them the most efficient and environmentally-friendly lighting option available today. His older brother, David, is also an old Pauline. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Hui is a former chair professor at the University of Hong Kong and the City University of Hong Kong, and is currently a chair professor at Imperial College London and a professor at Nanyang Technological University, Singapore. Among other fellowships, Hui is a fellow of the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE) and the Royal Academy of Engineers. A prolific researcher, Hui has authored/co-authored over 500 papers and has over 120 widely adopted patents to his name.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Practicability and sustainability are a constant focus in Hui’s research: although wireless charging is not new, Hui allowed free positioning of devices while keeping them safe to use. As mentioned earlier, improving LED efficacy is another example.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Hui considers ideas and creativity to be the bedrock of scientific innovation and laments the city’s lack of investment and aversion to risk. To Hui, researchers should be allowed more room to explore concepts instead of being constrained by short-term targets.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/32.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":33,"alumni_name":"Shun Chi Ming SBS","alumni_name_chi":"岑智明","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1980","chi_alias":"天文台前台長","eng_alias":"Former Director of the Hong Kong Observatory","chi_desc":"<p>岑智明於一九六九年入讀聖保羅書院小學，一九八〇年畢業書院中學部。一九八六年加入皇家香港天文台，出任科學主任。他在二〇一一年至二〇年間出任香港天文台台長。</p>\r\n<p>一九九三年起，岑氏專門從事航空氣象工作，曾負責安裝及引入赤鱲角機場（一九九八年啓用）多普勒天氣雷達及激光雷達，爲機場探測風切變。他亦進一步硏究風切變成因和改進預警方法，二〇〇五年於赤鱲角機場建立全球首個激光雷達風切變預警系統。</p>\r\n<p>岑智明擔任台長後，強調「科學」、「創新」和「多元合作」為天文台發展的三個支柱，並接連推出新服務，包括加增顯示分區閃電次數、九天天氣預報、特別天氣提示，推出定點降雨預報，並將新服務加入智能電話電話應用程式「我的天文台」，又設立香港天文台個人版網站，自動分區天氣預報，成立天氣廣播站團隊並統一電視天氣節目等。</p>\r\n<p>岑智明在擔任台長期間帶領香港天文台走向現代化，強調氣象服務是要積極面對改變的重要性，並指在面對挑戰時，必須更具彈性、更有創意及更具成本效益。岑氏於二〇二〇年獲頒銀紫荊星章。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Shun Chi Ming entered St. Paul’s College Primary School in 1969 and graduated from the College in 1980. Shun entered the Royal Observatory, Hong Kong, as a scientific officer in 1986. He served as director of the Hong Kong Observatory (HKO) between 2011 and 2020. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Shun began specialising in aviation meteorology in 1993, dedicating himself to improving wind shear detection and forecasting. He was responsible for introducing the Chek Lap Kok airport’s lidar-based wind shear alert system in 2005, the first such system in the world.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As HKO director, Shun identified ‘science’, ‘innovation’ and ‘diversified cooperation’ as the three pillars towards HKO’s future development. This manifested most visibly in more comprehensive forecasting, including the lightning location information service, extending the seven-day forecast into a nine-day forecast, providing regional forecasts, personalised websites, and incorporating such improvements in the MyObservatory mobile app. Shun also unified the HKO’s broadcasting across television stations by setting up the Weather On-Air team.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Shun’s tenure saw the HKO’s further modernisation. He underscores the importance of a positive attitude, flexibility, creativity, and adaptability in weathering all manners of change. He was awarded the SBS in 2020.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/33.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":93,"alumni_name":"Professor Monica Lam Sin Ling","alumni_name_chi":"林倩玲敎授 ","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1977","chi_alias":"中六畢業 — 史丹福大學虛擬助理硏究總監","eng_alias":"Director of Stanford’s Virtual Assistant Programme ","chi_desc":"<p>林倩玲敎授於一九七六至七七年度於聖保羅書院修讀預科班。一九八八年起，林氏任史丹福大學電腦科學系敎授，現時兼任該校公開虛擬助理實驗室（Open Virtual Assistant Lab，簡稱OVAL）總監。</p>\r\n<p>林氏以研究電腦系統與語言為專長，包括程式語言及自然語言。她的早期硏究圍繞編譯器——即將原始碼（程式本身編碼）譯至機械碼（電腦硬件可以直接處理的編碼）之程式。一九九〇年代，隨着平行運算開始於硏究用之超級電腦普及（至二〇〇〇年代中開始於個人電腦普及），林氏着手優化編譯器中的平行化處理（即將某項工序分工予多個處理系統的程序）【註㊀】，開創仿射劃分（affine partitioning）理論【註㊁】，改善電腦系統的運算效率。其硏究團隊曾開發硏究用編譯器「SUIF」，至今仍於大專敎學廣泛應用。她亦為《編譯系統設計》（簡體版譯《編譯原理》，俗稱「龍書」）第二版（二〇〇六年）的合著者之一，乃電腦科學學系經典敎材。</p>\r\n<p>近年，她領導OVAL團隊硏發開源虛擬助理程式，涉及透過語言模型與機器學習以解構、分析及處理自然語言。林氏憂慮虛擬助理將被大型企業寡頭壟斷（如Amazon之Alexa、Google助理、Apple之Siri等），而OVAL的開發過程則着重開源及開放取用（包括原始碼、資料庫、文件等）。由於取用OVAL各工具不受商業牽制，內裏構造又公開透明，換言之，人人可以自行編製虛擬助理，亦可知道程式每一步如何運作，從而保障個人資料及私隱。</p>\r\n<p>在二〇〇四年，於回顧SUIF成就的一篇文章中，林氏曾言自己的硏究工作深受實用性驅動：「事實上，我認為此項硏究得以成功，當中有賴每一步皆為解決現實問題而起。這讓我們可以集中於重要難題，同時確保我們基礎上採取正確的方法。」</p>\r\n<p>本展另有幸與林氏訪談，可按此連結觀賞。</p>\r\n<p>【註㊀】「平行運算」與「循序運算」相對，循序運算則由單一系統順序處理程式所有運算。雖然平行運算更具效率，循序式程式較容易編寫，故早期電腦程式（至一九八〇年代末）以循序運算為主。平行化的難處之一正是探究何以有效地兼顧處理器／系統構造而分工：理想的平行化會用盡系統所有，同時減少因為工序進展不斷更新而系統與系統之間所需要之同步。</p>\r\n<p>【註㊁】「仿射劃分」理論運用幾何學「仿射轉換」的概念。圖形經仿射轉換後會保留線與線之比例，例如透過旋轉、鏡像、放大縮小等，相似三角形為一例子。仿射劃分以同樣原理轉換並劃分程式編碼，以作平行化，應用於迴圈轉換演算法。</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Monica S. Lam studied lower sixth at St. Paul’s College during the 1976–77 academic year. Since 1988, she has been a professor at Stanford University’s Computer Science Department. Presently, she is also the faculty director of Stanford’s Open Virtual Assistant Lab (Oval).</p>\r\n<p>Lam’s expertise lies in computer systems and languages, both computing languages and natural language (i.e. human language). Her early work was on compilers, which translate software code into a format understandable by hardware. As parallel processing became more common among research supercomputers in the 1990s (and later in consumer systems by the mid-2000s), Lam specialised in optimising parallelisation (dividing up and distributing a workload to multiple systems).* Her pioneering theory on affine partitioning has enabled more efficient running of code.† She was part of the team behind SUIF, a research compiler widely used in academia. Lam also co-authored <i>Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools</i> (second edition, 2006), aka the ‘Dragon Book’, a highly popular computer science textbook.</p>\r\n<p>More recently, Lam has been leading and championing an open-source virtual assistant initiative through Oval, which involves interpreting and processing natural language via language models and machine learning. Anxious about an emerging corporate oligopoly among current picks such as Alexa (Amazon), Google Assistant, and Siri (Apple), her work on Oval prioritises open access (to code, databases, and documentation). By extension, since everyone can know exactly what the assistant is doing, and are free to build their own virtual assistant using Oval’s tools, there is an extra emphasis on personal control and privacy.</p>\r\n<p>As she wrote in a 2004 retrospective on SUIF, practical application is a core theme in her work: ‘<i>In fact, I think that the success of this research rested partly on the fact that each step along the way addressed some real-life problem. This helped us focus on important issues and ensured that the basic approach was correct.</i>’</p>\r\n<p>Click here for an interview with Lam, specially prepared as part of this exhibition.</p>\r\n<p>* Contrast parallel processing with serial processing, where a workload is handled by a single system in sequence. While parallel processing is more efficient, serial processing is considerably easier to program for. This made serial processing prevalent for early computer programs (up to the late 1980s). Part of the difficulty in coding for parallel processing comes from figuring out how to divide up the work optimally — the ideal scenario would be to make use of all available processors/systems while minimising synchronisation (every individual system needing to update task progression for all other systems).</p>\r\n<p>† Affine partitioning theory makes use of the geometric concept of affine transformations. An affine transformation preserves ratios of distances by means of rotation, reflection, scaling, etc.; an example would be similar triangles. Affine partitioning analyses software code and performs such transformations where appropriate, partitioning (dividing) it into parallel processes. It is used in loop transformation algorithms.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"craftspeople","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/93.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"}],"learnedmen":[{"id":50,"alumni_name":"Wu Ting Fang JP (aka Ng Choy / Wu Chih-yung)","alumni_name_chi":"伍廷芳（伍才／伍秩庸）","lifespan":"(1842–1922)","grad_year":"1861","chi_alias":"首位華人大律師","eng_alias":"The First Chinese Barrister","chi_desc":"<p>伍廷芳在一八五六至六一年就讀於聖保羅書院，於一八七四年自費留學英國倫敦林肯法律學院，回港後成爲首位華人大律師，並獲委任爲首位華人太平紳士與定例局（今立法會）首位華人議員，後爲淸末民初傑出外交家、法學家及政治家。</p>\r\n<p>一八八二年，時値中國多事之秋，伍氏遂應淸廷北洋大臣李鴻章邀請，入其幕府，推動新政，倡辦鐵路，擔任中國首家鐵路公司——開平鐵路公司總辦。於外交貢獻尤爲卓著，協商國際條約，兩度奉職中國駐美大臣，力保華僑權益，抗議排華法案，爲世稱頌。伍氏又參與改革中國法律，制定中國最早之商業法，提出廢除凌遲等酷刑，並建議採取陪審團制度，開中國刑法新紀元，官至正二品。</p>\r\n<p>一九一一年辛亥革命後，伍氏支持共和，任南方軍政府代表，參與南北議和，促成淸帝遜位。歷任中華民國臨時政府、北洋政府及廣州護法軍政府要職，如司法總長、外交總長、財政總長、廣東省省長、代總理及代行非常大總統等職務。逝後孫中山親撰《伍廷芳墓表》，譽伍氏爲人知識淵博、正直不阿及盡心國事。</p>\r\n<p>伍氏感念母校敎育之恩，多次捐助鉅款予聖保羅書院校舍擴建基金。書院爲表彰其貢獻，於一九一一年建成「伍廷芳堂」（今中環己連拿利聖保羅堂副堂），般咸道校舍圖書館亦於一九七四​年以公諱命名。在二〇一六年時，書院適逢一百六十五周年，故於館內立伍氏銅像，以表深仰，激勵後​學踵武前賢。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Wu Ting Fang studied at St. Paul’s College from 1855 to 1861. In 1874, Wu furthered his studies at Lincoln’s Inn and became Hong Kong’s first Chinese barrister (1877), Chinese JP (1878), and Chinese Legislative Council member (1880). Later in his career, Wu rose to prominence as a leading jurist, diplomat, and statesman.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In 1882, Wu was invited by Li Hongzhang to join his reform efforts, thus beginning his career in China. Initially, Wu was a diplomatic negotiator, then moved on to manage various railway companies springing up across China, including its very first, the Kaiping Coal Delivery and Railway Company. From the late 1890s, Wu served as the Qing ambassador to the Americas (1897–1902 and 1907–09), during which he strove to protect the Chinese diaspora and pushed against anti-Chinese measures. Wu also modernised China’s laws, including drafting China’s first commercial code and revising its criminal code, recommending trial by jury, and eliminating barbaric practices such as <i>lingchi</i> (death by a thousand cuts) and posthumous execution. By 1911, Wu was a second-rank official in imperial China’s nine-rank system (the first rank being the highest).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Following the Wuchang Uprising in 1911, Wu was the chief representative for the revolutionaries in negotiations with the Qing court. Wu later served in key positions in the fledgling republic, including as justice minister and foreign minister, briefly serving as acting premier and acting president. His epitaph was penned by none other than Sun Yat-sen, who praised Wu’s intellect, sense of justice, and service to the nation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Wu had never forgotten his alma mater over all these years. He donated generously to St. Paul’s College in 1911 and 1919 to help with expansion projects, and the Wu Ting Fang Hall (in Glenealy, built in 1911) was named in his honour. The current College library was renamed the Wu Ting Fang Library in 1974 in his memory. In 2016, for our 165th anniversary, the College installed a bronze bust of Wu’s likeness as a commemoration of our past and inspiration for our future.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/50.jpg","exhibition_video":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni_video/50.mp4","status":"A"},{"id":51,"alumni_name":"Chan Ayin (aka Chan Oi Ting / Chen Shan Yen)","alumni_name_chi":"陳言 （陳靄庭／陳藹亭／陳善言）","lifespan":"(1846–1905)","grad_year":"1864","chi_alias":"首代中國外交官 ","eng_alias":"One of China’s First Diplomats","chi_desc":"<p>陳言生於廣東新會，在一八五六至六四年間就讀聖保羅書院，爲翻譯官、編輯、外交官，曾創辦《華字日報》。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>畢業後，陳氏出任巡理府翻譯及書記，在一八七一年轉投香港《德臣西報》任副編輯，爲華人任英文報紙編輯之始，同年創辦並主編香港第二份中文報紙《中外新聞七日報》，翌年易名《華字日報》，出任總編輯。在一八七三年與一眾華人創辦中華印務總局，曾英譯王韜《普法戰紀》，在一八七四年參與創辦首份華資報章《循環日報》，任總司理。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>陳氏視中文報紙爲協助各華商及知識份子增廣見聞、集思廣益之渠道，除翻譯與報導西方政治、社會、軍事狀況等新聞，亦報中國時事，就淸廷改革、保護海外華工及港府施政提出意見，並歡迎讀者投稿以表評論。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>陳氏在港力說淸廷宜對外派使，其後獲薦並效力淸廷，在一八七八年遠赴美洲，派駐古巴馬丹薩領事，於一八八六年任總領事，期間改善當地華工待遇，至一八八九年返國。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>回國後，陳氏曾任一八九七至九九年任開平礦務局會辦，後任淞滬及滬寧鐵路督辦。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Chan Ayin was born in Xinhui, Guangdong. He studied at St. Paul’s College from 1856 to 1864. A translator, editor, and diplomat, Chan founded the <i>Chinese Mail</i> (aka <i>Wah Tsz Yat Po</i>), Hong Kong’s second-ever Chinese-language newspaper.</p>\r\n<p>After graduating from the College, Chan first worked as an interpreter and secretary at the Magistrate’s Court. He joined the <I>China Mail</i> in 1871 as a sub-editor, one of the first Chinese to do so. The following year, he founded and edited the <i>Chinese Mail</i>. In 1873, he co-founded the General Chinese Printing Office and translated Wang Tao’s <i>Report on the Franco-Prussian War</i> into English. In 1874, he co-founded the <i>Universal Circulating Herald</i>, the first newspaper fully financed by Chinese capital, and was its chief manager.</p>\r\n<p>Chan saw Chinese newspapers as a platform for Chinese merchants and intellectuals to broaden and articulate their thoughts. His newspapers frequently reported news in China and commented on local and national governance.</p>\r\n<p>Chan advocated China setting up diplomatic missions and later served the Qing government. He sailed to the Americas in 1878 and began working at the Chinese Legation in Matanzas, Cuba, in 1879. He was promoted to consul-general in 1886. During his time there, Chan worked to improve the treatment of Cuban-Chinese workers. He returned to China in 1889.</p> <p>Chan later managed the Chinese Engineering and Mining Company at Kaiping (1897–99), and supervised the Shanghai–Woosung and the Shanghai–Ningbo railways.</p>","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/51.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":52,"alumni_name":"Yeung Ku Wan","alumni_name_chi":"楊衢雲","lifespan":" (1861–1901)","grad_year":"1881","chi_alias":"中國近代革命家","eng_alias":"Chinese Revolutionary of the late Qing Dynasty","chi_desc":"<p>楊衢雲在一八八一年畢業於聖保羅書院。及後於一八九〇年合創輔仁文社，在一八九五年與孫中山合作成立香港興中會，獲推會長。同年發動廣州乙未起義，惟因泄機未果，被迫離港，及後數年輾轉於全球，其後創立興中會分會，在一九〇〇年再發動惠州起義，又敗，留港善後，翌年遭淸廷所派刺客殺害。</p> <p>見中國十九世紀屢次被列強入侵，楊氏堅信欲振興中華則要推翻帝制本身，比當時主張改革保皇之輿論更爲激進。自身熟讀外語政論，楊氏爲輔仁文社領袖，常論及反淸思想。一八九一年，楊氏與孫中山相遇，兩人一見如故。</p> <p>楊氏遇刺，興中會元氣大傷，對孫中山影響尤甚，曾言「衢雲死矣，衢雲死矣，予承其志，誓成民國，帝制自爲，予必討之」。楊氏歿葬於跑馬地香港墳場無名碑，政府近年亦爲墓碑加上官方介紹，紀念此名中華革命先鋒。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Yeung Ku Wan graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1881. He co-founded the Foo Yan Man Ser (Furen Literary Society) in 1890 and later the Revive China Society in Hong Kong in 1895 with Sun Yat-sen. The society planned the First Guangzhou Uprising the same year, but it failed due to leaked plans. Forced out of Hong Kong, Yeung spent the following years founding global branches of the society. He plotted the Huizhou Uprising in 1900, but without success. Yeung remained in Hong Kong until his 1901 assassination by a Qing agent.</p> <p>Embittered by China’s repeated defeats throughout the nineteenth century, Yeung believed monarchy itself to be the barrier to China’s rejuvenation — a significantly more radical stance compared to his reformist contemporaries in the 1880s. Well-read in western treatises, Yeung led the Foo Yan Man Ser, giving frequent speeches. He met Sun Yat-sen in 1891, and the two immediately became lifelong friends.</p> <p>Yeung’s death came as a shock to the Revive China Society, profoundly impacting Sun, who vowed to carry on his will of overthrowing the monarchy and establishing a republic. Yeung was buried in the Hong Kong Cemetery in Happy Valley under a nameless grave, though the government has since marked its location in commemoration of his revolutionary contributions.</p>","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/52.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":53,"alumni_name":"Wang Ch‘ung-hui","alumni_name_chi":"王寵惠 ","lifespan":"(1881–1958)","grad_year":"1891","chi_alias":"法學國際外交官","eng_alias":"Jurist and Foreign Minister","chi_desc":"<p>王寵惠在一八八七至一八九一年間就讀聖保羅書院。王氏學術精湛，爲民國政府法學家及外交官，在一九二二年曾短暫擔任國務總理，後任南京政府訓政時期及行憲後首任司法院院長，於一九三七至四一年間任外交總長，在一九四一至四七年間任國防最高委員會祕書長，民國以外亦曾於一九三一至三六年間任海牙國際法院法官。</p>\r\n<p>王氏父母爲傳敎士，幼時居於荷李活道道濟會堂，自此與當時硏醫的孫中山相識。後來王氏於東京硏修時，在一九〇一年曾編輯反淸報章《國民報》外文版。</p>\r\n<p>中華民國成立時，王氏在孫中山臨時內閣獲提名委任外交總長。後來民初動盪時期，王氏先效力北京政府，於一九二〇年任大理院（最高法院）院長。後於一九二七年，國民革命軍北伐，王氏轉赴南京，助國民政府制憲，參與撰寫刑法及政府組織法，訂立孫中山所倡之五院制。</p>\r\n<p>王氏一九三七年上任外交部後，適逢抗日戰爭爆發，期間力爭國際支持。王氏以中國使團成員出席一九四三年開羅會議及一九四五年三藩市會議，前者最終讓戰後中國得以收復東北及臺灣，後者奠定中國成爲新成立聯合國安全理事會中常任理事國之一。</p>\r\n<p>一九四九年，王氏曾短暫留港，期間與聖保羅舊生重逢，出席活動作演講。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Wang Ch‘ung-hui studied at St. Paul’s College between 1887 and 1891. A gifted scholar, Wang was one of the Republic of China’s foremost jurists and diplomats. He briefly served as premier (1922), twice served as the Judicial Yuan’s first president (1928–31 and 1948–58), the foreign minister (1937–41), and secretary general of China’s war cabinet (1941–47). Wang was also a judge on the Permanent Court of International Justice (1931–36), the direct predecessor to the International Court of Justice in The Hague.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Born to a Protestant missionary family, Wang lived at the To Tsai Church, where he first met Sun Yat-sen and then studied medicine. While studying in Tokyo in 1901, Wang edited the English section of the <I>Chinese National</I>, an anti-Qing, anti-imperialist journal.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Wang was named foreign minister in Sun Yat-sen’s short-lived provisional cabinet. Wang initially served the Beiyang government before becoming the Supreme Court’s chief justice in 1920. As the Kuomintang swept northward, Wang headed south to Nanjing. Helming the Judicial Yuan, Wang helped draft the Republic’s constitution, in particular its criminal code and governmental outline, giving a legal framework to Sun Yat-sen’s ‘five branches of government’ model.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Soon after Wang’s appointment as foreign minister, Japan launched its invasion of China. At the foreign ministry and the defence council, Wang courted international support, negotiating terms favourable to China in the post-war order — he was a key aide in the 1943 Cairo Conference and the 1945 San Francisco Conference, which respectively ensured China retook the North East and Taiwan and guaranteed China’s permanent seat on the United Nations Security Council.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In 1949, Wang made a brief detour to Hong Kong, where he reunited with College alumni.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/53.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":54,"alumni_name":"General Yee Shiu Kee CBE","alumni_name_chi":" 余兆麒將軍 ","lifespan":" (1904–1993)","grad_year":"1920","chi_alias":"軍事領導敎育長","eng_alias":"Military Academy Instructor","chi_desc":"<p>余兆麒生於廣東新會，在一九二〇年畢業於聖保羅書院，兩年後赴美升學，曾於密芝根大學、維珍尼亞軍事學院就讀，後獲芝加哥大學法律博士。</p>\r\n<p>學成歸國後，先後出任黃埔軍校敎官及廣東空軍訓練學校敎育長等職位。在一九三八年，廣州淪陷後，余氏赴港擔任軍令部駐港高級參謀，負責蒐集情報及維持紀律。在一九四一年隨香港淪陷，余氏協助盟軍殘部突圍，後獲英國頒贈名譽CBE勛章。其後在一九四二年轉赴重慶，爲戰時情報機關服務，負責與英美盟軍協調。一九四五年，余氏領中將銜，日本投降後代表中方參與盟軍接收香港儀式。</p>\r\n<p>戰後余氏退役營商，在一九四七年創立中國聯合銀行。畢業後多年，余氏仍關注書院事務，曾支持書院一九四〇年代末之復校運動。余氏亦熱心公益，一九八三年設立余兆麒醫療基金，用以改善貧苦大眾的醫療設施，逝後，其聯合銀行股份及利潤一倂贈予基金，遺愛人間。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Xinhui, Guangdong, S.K. Yee graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1920. Two years later, he furthered his studies in the United States. He studied at the University of Michigan, the Virginia Military Institute, and obtained a JD from the University of Chicago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yee returned to China and served as an instructor at the Whampoa Military Academy, where at one point he directed the aviation school. During the Second Sino-Japanese War, after Guangzhou fell, Yee served as a senior officer of China’s General Staff stationed in Hong Kong and was placed in charge of intelligence and maintaining morale. As Hong Kong fell, Yee assisted Allied troops in their escape while continuing intelligence services in Chongqing; in 1942, he was awarded an honorary CBE for his bravery. Yee was promoted to lieutenant general in 1945. After Japan surrendered to the Allies, he was part of the Chinese delegation at the Hong Kong’s handover ceremony.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yee retired from military service after the war. In 1947, Yee established the United Chinese Bank. He remained committed to St. Paul’s and was one of many who supported its re-establishment. Yee was additionally a philanthropist: he established the S.K. Yee Medical Foundation in 1983 to improve facilities for the poor, bequeathing his shares and profits from the bank to the foundation posthumously.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/54.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":55,"alumni_name":"Xu Li (aka Xu Jingcheng)","alumni_name_chi":"許立（許敬誠）","lifespan":" (1905–1971)","grad_year":"1920s","chi_alias":"工人運動領袖","eng_alias":"Labour Activist ","chi_desc":"<p>許立生於福建晉江，在一九二〇年代初曾於聖保羅書院就讀，爲共產革命家、工人運動領袖、游擊隊領袖。在一九四九年建國後曾任中共中央對外聯絡部首任副部長之一。</p>\r\n<p>許氏在港期間受海員大罷工啓發，遂自學馬克斯主義，立志從事工人運動。在一九二五年赴菲律賓，同年上海發生五卅慘案，許氏決心聲援上海工人及學生，在一九二七年成立菲律賓華僑工人協會，於一九三一年成爲共產黨員，領導中共菲律賓支部。</p>\r\n<p>隨抗日戰爭爆發，國共再度合作，許氏在菲律賓組織華僑靑年赴華入新四軍。菲律賓日佔時期，許氏聯同李炳祥等在菲華僑成立菲律賓華僑抗日遊擊支隊（「華支」），打擾日軍補給，配合美軍反攻，太平洋戰爭爲盟軍出力。</p>\r\n<p>一九四六年，許氏回國，第二次國共內戰於華南動員支持中共解放。中共建政後，曾出任中聯部首任副部長之一，中國人民政治協商會議全國委員會委員等。文化大革命爆發時，許氏被康生誣爲「三反分子」。四人幫下臺後，國家領導層於一九七八年的追悼會上獻了花圈，對其貢獻作出肯定，許氏終獲平反。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Jinjiang, Fujian, Xu Li studied at St. Paul’s College in the early 1920s. A communist revolutionary, labour activist, and guerrilla leader, Xu primarily worked at the Communist Party (CPC)’s International Department (ILD) after the People’s Republic (PRC) was established.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Inspired by the Seamen’s Strike of 1922, Xu began reading Marxist literature, entering a life of labour activism. In 1925, Xu left Hong Kong for the Philippines and was part of the anti-imperialist May Thirtieth Movement. He founded the Chinese General Labour Union in the Philippines in 1927, became a full CPC member in 1931, and led the party’s Philippine branch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>With the outbreak of the Second Sino-Japanese War, Xu mobilised his compatriots to join the mainland’s New Fourth Army. When Japan occupied the Philippines, Xu organised the Philippine-Chinese Anti-Japanese Guerrilla Forces (aka the Wha-Chi) together with Li Bingxiang and other Chinese Filipinos, disrupting Japanese supply lines and assisting the American-led counterattack.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In 1946, Xu went back to southern China, where he mobilised support for the Communists in the Chinese Civil War. Following the PRC’s founding, Xu was the ILD’s first deputy director and also served on the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference National Committee. During the Cultural Revolution, Xu was framed as a counter-revolutionary by Mao Zedong’s ally, Kang Sheng. His rehabilitation came posthumously, following Deng Xiaoping’s accession to power in 1978.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/55.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":56,"alumni_name":"Li Xin (aka Li Hongmo)","alumni_name_chi":"李欣（李鴻模） ","lifespan":"(1917–2017)","grad_year":"1932","chi_alias":"中共軍政之才","eng_alias":"Communist Party Diplomat","chi_desc":"<p>李欣，本名李鴻模，在一九三一年入讀聖保羅書院，翌年退學，後爲中國共產黨政治家、翻譯，爲中華人民共和國首批外交官之一。</p>\r\n<p>李氏一九二〇年代長大，見證國共分裂，受反帝國主義及革命思潮影響。在一九三一年隨父赴港，於聖保羅書院寄宿，惟就讀一個學期後便離港。</p>\r\n<p>一九三三年，李氏在廣州加入左翼讀書會，鑽硏馬列主義，在一九三六年於靑島加入共產黨，帶領山東大學內黨的活動，抗戰爆發後獲委靑島特別支部書記，曾參與徐州、武漢會戰，在山東參與游擊戰。及後第二次國共內戰，李氏於解放軍各軍隊中任政委和政治部主任，參與遼瀋、平津、廣西等戰役。</p>\r\n<p>共和國成立後急需外交官，李氏諳德語和英語，在一九五〇年獲派駐東德參贊，於一九五四年出任駐英首任參贊，至一九五七年回國，於解放軍學院繼續硏究，後出任軍事科學院外國軍事研究部副部長，主持學院翻譯克勞塞維茨著作《戰爭論》，被譽爲眾多譯本中最佳。文革中，李氏被下放勞動約五年，於一九七七年獲平反，至一九八五年退休。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Li Xin was born in 1917. He studied at St. Paul’s College between 1931 and 1932. Li was a Communist Party (CPC) organiser, translator, and one of the People’s Republic’s first diplomats. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Li grew up in the 1920s, at the peak of Chinese anti-imperialism and during the first Kuomintang–Communist split. He was sent to St. Paul’s as a boarder in 1931, but left Hong Kong after one term.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Li joined a leftist reading society in 1933 in Guangzhou, further familiarising himself with Marxism-Leninism. He joined the CPC in 1936, where he led party activities within Qingdao’s Shantung University. In the Second Sino-Japanese War, he saw battle at Xuzhou, Wuhan, and guerrilla operations in Shandong. Li was a political commissar in different People’s Liberation Army (PLA) armies during the Chinese Civil War. He took part in the Liaoshen, Pingjin, and Guangxi campaigns.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Within the early People’s Republic, there was a sore need for diplomats. Fluent in German and English, Li was a political counsellor in China’s East German (1950–54) and British (1954–57) missions. Li returned to China thereafter, studying at the PLA’s military school. He later became the PLA Academy of Military Science’s Department of World Military Research’s deputy director. There, he oversaw the translation of von Clausewitz’s <I>On War</I> into Chinese, which is now thought to be the best Chinese version of the book. The Cultural Revolution saw Li exiled to labour; he was rehabilitated in 1977. Li retired in 1985.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/56.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":57,"alumni_name":"The Honourable Sir Sze-yuen Chung GBM GBE JP","alumni_name_chi":"鍾士元爵士 ","lifespan":"(1917–2018)","grad_year":"1936","chi_alias":"香港政壇元老","eng_alias":"The Elder Figure in Hong Kong politics","chi_desc":"<p> 鍾士元在一九​三六年畢業於聖保羅書院,曾任本校校董多年。鍾氏於香港工商界及政壇成就非凡,爲香港和平回歸貢獻良多。 </p> <p> 鍾氏自書院畢業後主攻工程學,在一九四一年加入黃埔船塢,爲當時兩位華人工程師之一。香港淪陷後赴江西工作,抗戰後返港,後赴英國深造。返港後創辦崇佳實業,至一九五八獲邀加入工業總會籌委會,自此踏上公職生涯。 </p> <p> 鍾氏政壇地位崇高,獲委立法局和行政局非官守議員後,又於一九七四年和一九八〇年分別出任立法局和行政局首席非官守議員。期間,他參與中英有關香港主權的談判,並多次向兩國官員反映港人意見,至一九八八年退休。於一九九〇年代,他任職港事顧問,就將來特區運作向中國政府提出建議。及後,他先後成為特區預備委員會及其推選委員會成員,回歸後獲推舉爲第一屆行政會議召集人。 </p> <p> 另外,鍾氏熱心敎育及公益事業,曾先後參與籌辦理工學院(今理工大學)、城市理工學院(今城市大學)及香港科技大學,在一九九〇年籌辦醫院管理局,管理全港公立醫院。鍾氏先後於一九七八年封爵,在一九八九年獲頒GBE勛章,以及一九九七年獲頒特區大紫荊勳章。 </p>","eng_desc":"<p> Chung Sze-yuen graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1936 and had served for many years on the College Council. Known as the ‘godfather of Hong Kong politics’, Chung was a key figure in ensuring Hong Kong’s peaceful handover. </p> <p> Chung focused on engineering after his time at St. Paul’s. In 1941, he was one of only two Chinese engineers at the Whampoa Docks. During the war, Chung worked in China-controlled Jiangxi and furthered his studies in the UK after the war. In the 1950s, having founded Sonca Industries, Chung became a successful entrepreneur; he was invited to join the organising committee of the Federation of Hong Kong Industries, marking the beginning of his public career. </p> <p> Chung rose to the most senior positions in both the Legislative Council (1974–80) and the Executive Council (1980–88). In the 1980s, he led groups of Hongkongers to Britain and China to talk about their concerns about the future of the city. In the 1990s, he served as a Hong Kong Affairs Adviser to the Chinese government and was part of the Preparatory Committee of the HKSAR and the Selection Committee. After the handover, he was selected as the SAR’s first Executive Council Convenor. </p> <p> Outside of politics, Chung was a key figure in establishing the Hong Kong Polytechnic (now Polytechnic University), City Polytechnic (now City University), and the Hong Kong University of Science and Technology. In 1990, he unified the management of Hong Kong’s public hospitals under the Hospital Authority, leaving an indelible mark on Hong Kong’s infrastructure. He was knighted in 1978, was conferred the GBE in 1989, and received the HKSAR’s GBM in 1997. </p>","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/57.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":58,"alumni_name":"Tse Ping Fu","alumni_name_chi":"謝炳奎 ","lifespan":" (1923–2018)","grad_year":"1941","chi_alias":"香港童軍主領袖","eng_alias":" (The Chief) of Hong Kong’s Scouts ","chi_desc":"<p>謝炳奎生於汕頭，在一九三四年入讀聖保羅書院，至一九四一年畢業，期間於一九三五年宣誓加入童軍第十旅。太平洋戰爭期間，謝氏效力美國戰時新聞處。謝氏爲香港童軍戰後發展之功臣，童軍內稱爲「老總」。</p>\r\n<p>謝氏在書院求學首年爲書院寄宿生，當年寄宿生多爲只懂潮汕話的泰籍華裔，謝氏懂國語、粵語、潮語，成爲寄宿舍內的小翻譯。</p>\r\n<p>一九三七年抗戰爆發，港府加快在港佈防，當中涉及民防方面與童軍合作。謝氏擔任防空傳訊員，日本侵港時帶領駐己連拿利校舍的聖約翰救傷隊員救傷扶危，至淪陷一刻緊守崗位。日佔時謝氏被迫遷徙，後於一九四四年加入美國新聞處，翻譯惠州、潮汕一帶軍事情報。抗戰勝利後，於一九四七年返港。</p>\r\n<p>謝氏戰後獲史伊尹校長邀請重建童軍第十旅，後於一九五〇至五四年間擔任旅長，一九五五至六一年任維多利亞區區總監，在一九六一至六八年間任首任港島地區總監，又曾任大潭營地總主任，之間常率團往海外參與大露營等交流活動，在一九七五年起爲香港童軍總會榮譽總監。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Shantou (Swatow), Tse Ping Fui studied at St. Paul’s College from 1934 to 1941, joining the Scouts in 1935. During the Pacific War, Tse worked as an interpreter for the Americans. A key figure in the Hong Kong Scouts’ post-war development, he is known as <i>lo chung </i>(‘the chief’) within the association.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tse was a boarder in his first year; most college boarders at the time were Thai Chinese who only spoke Teochew. Knowing Cantonese, Mandarin, and Teochew, Tse found himself an interpreter within the hostel.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As the Second Sino-Japanese War broke out, the local administration strengthened defences in preparation; this included civil training in collaboration with the Scouts. Tse was a dispatch rider and served on the air raid precaution patrol. In the Battle of Hong Kong, Tse helped St. John Ambulance medics (stationed at the College campus) navigate Central’s streets to treat casualties. Among the many displaced following the fall of Hong Kong, Tse joined the US Office of War Information in 1944, translating intelligence from the Huizhou–Chaoshan region. He returned to Hong Kong in 1947.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After the war, at the behest of principal Evan Stewart, Tse helped to rebuild the 10th Hong Kong Scout Group. He successively served as the 10th group’s scoutmaster (1950–54), the Victoria District commissioner (1955–61), Hong Kong Island Area’s first commissioner (1961–68), and Tai Tam Scout Camp’s chief warden. Tse frequently led his scouts to international jamborees. He was appointed the Scout Association of Hong Kong’s honorary commissioner in 1975.</p>","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/58.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":59,"alumni_name":"Preston Wong Shiu Pun","alumni_name_chi":"黃韶本 ","lifespan":" (1890–1943)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"爲港捐軀之童軍創旅愨士","eng_alias":"A Faithful Founding Scoutmaster Who Gave His Life for Hong Kong","chi_desc":"<p>黃韶本曾任聖保羅書院校務主任及司庫，在一九二二年創辦香港第十旅童軍，出任首任旅長。香港日佔時期爲地下運動效力，在一九四三年十月廿九日遭日軍處死。</p>\r\n<p>黃氏一九一二年任職本校敎師。一戰時黃氏加入香港後備警察隊，後晉升至副督察。在一九一九年遠赴海外進修，於伯明翰、劍橋及芝加哥修讀心理學及神學，至一九二二年返港，在書院創立第十旅。除敎授中文及領導第十旅外，黃氏亦爲書院舍監，照顧本校眾寄宿生。</p>\r\n<p>香港淪陷後，黃氏加入英軍服務團，當中協助英方人員逃離香港，並負責蒐集情報，包括日軍士兵與船隻動向，助盟軍空襲。黃氏在一九四三年四月被日方拘捕，同年十月於赤柱被斬首。黃氏身爲虔誠敎徒，曾領一眾受刑人祈禱，直至最後一刻依然原諒處刑人。香港重光後，黃氏之遺骸移葬赤柱國殤紀念墳場。</p>\r\n<p>本校第十旅童軍於二〇一三（黃氏七十週年忌日）及二〇二一年起每年在赤柱辦悼念儀式致敬。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Preston Wong was the College’s senior Chinese master, bursar, and the founding scoutmaster of our resident 10th Hong Kong Scout Group. During the Japanese occupation, Wong was part of Hong Kong’s underground resistance. He was executed by the Japanese on 29 October 1943.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Wong joined the College staff in 1912, having studied at Queen’s College and having taught briefly at the Diocesan Boys’ School. Wong joined the police reserve during the First World War, where he eventually rose to the rank of sub-inspector. In 1919, he set off for further studies in psychology and theology at Birmingham, Cambridge, and Chicago, returning in 1922. In addition to his teaching and scouting duties, Wong was also in charge of the College’s hostel.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Following the fall of Hong Kong in 1941, Wong joined the British Army Aid Group (BAAG). BAAG agents aided Britons in their escape and collected military intelligence, including Japanese troop and ship movements, which aided Allied air raids. Wong was arrested by Japanese authorities in April 1943 and beheaded in Stanley. A devout Christian, Wong led his fellow condemned men in prayer, forgiving his executioners until the very end.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Following Allied victory, his remains were reinterred at Stanley Military Cemetery. The 10th scout group held a memorial service at the cemetery in 2013, marking the seventieth anniversary of his death, and annual memorial services have been held from 2021 onwards.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/59.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":60,"alumni_name":"Gallant Ho Yiu Tai","alumni_name_chi":"何耀棣","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1960","chi_alias":"公益律師","eng_alias":"Charitable Lawyer","chi_desc":"<p>何耀棣在一九六二年由聖保羅書院高六畢業，於一九六九年考獲執業律師資格，在一九七七年創辦何耀棣律師事務所。何氏尤其專注地產法，於一九八一年成爲首批中國司法部委託公證人，助內地分割地產所有權及地產使用權，爲中國經濟發展打開新一頁。之後在一九九二年獲批在廣州設辦事處，爲內地境外首批，至一九九七年退休。</p>\r\n<p>何氏又熱心社會公益，甲寅年（一九七四至七五年）曾任保良局主席，又曾於一九八〇至八四年間任消費者委員會主席。何氏又曾資助聖公會成立兩所小學，皆以先翁何澤芸命名，亦曾任本校校董。</p>\r\n<p>何氏心繫祖國，曾任第八屆（一九九三至九八年）中國人民代表大會代表，退休後亦與貴州省春暉行動合作，捐贈鉅額予當地學童，助鄕村孩童得平等敎育機會。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Gallant Ho graduated from sixth form at St. Paul’s College in 1962. Ho qualified as a lawyer in 1969 and founded his own practice, Gallant, in 1977. Ho specialises in property law and has been a China-appointed attestation officer since 1981, helping to separate the ideas of land ownership and land use, which partly enabled China’s economic boom. Ho’s firm was one of the first non-mainland Chinese practices to open offices in Guangzhou, opening in 1992. He retired in 1997.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ho’s passion for charity began when he was young. He chaired the Po Leung Kuk during the 1974–75 lunar year and chaired the Consumer Council (1980–84). He sponsored two Sheng Kung Hui primary schools, both bearing his father’s name, Ho Chak Wan. Ho was additionally a former College council member.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>This spirit of service extended beyond Hong Kong. Outside of his legal work, Ho served on the eighth National People’s Congress (1993–98). After retirement, Ho donated to Guizhou’s charity programme ‘Chunhui Action’, improving education access to local schoolchildren in the mountainous, rural province.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/60.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":61,"alumni_name":"The Honourable Jasper Tsang Yok Sing GBM GBS JP ","alumni_name_chi":"曾鈺成","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1963","chi_alias":"政治敎育明才","eng_alias":"Scholarly Politician","chi_desc":"<p>曾鈺成於一九五六年入讀聖保羅書院，於一九六三年畢業，爲政治家及敎育家，在一九九八至二〇〇八年間任直選立法會議員，在二〇〇八至一六年間任立法會主席，在一九九二至二〇〇三年間任民建聯創黨主席，又曾於一九九三年起任四屆全國政協委員，其後於二〇一五年獲頒大紫荊勳章。在一九八五至九七年間任培僑中學校長，現任校監。其弟曾德成同爲本校舊生。</p>\r\n<p>曾氏又於政壇以外活躍，曾主持廣播節目、撰寫專欄及著書，傳媒著作包括《埋班》（ViuTV）、《鈺成其事》（《am730》）、《英語．人生　曾鈺成》（訪問錄，錢景亮著）及回憶錄《主席八年》兩冊等。</p>\r\n<p>曾氏以就讀聖保羅書院日子爲榮，當時有份參與成立學生會，又與同窗創辦「木鐸」文社，初嘗自編及自行出版。他尤其欣賞時任校長史璧琦牧師，講話淸晰，富魅力、幽默感，領導作風非凡，承傳史伊尹上校之精神，同時對書院作出革新。曾氏至今與母校有聯繫，不時參與聚舊及校慶活動。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Jasper Tsang entered St. Paul’s College in 1956 and graduated in 1963. A politician and educator, Tsang is a former directly-elected Legislative Council member (1998–2008), former Legislative Council president (2008–16), founding chairman of the Democratic Alliance for the Betterment of Hong Kong (DAB) (1992–2003). He served on the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference from 1993 to 2012. He is a former principal of Pui Kiu Middle School (1985–97), now serving as the school’s supervisor. He was awarded the GBM in 2015. He is an elder brother to Tsang Tak Sing, who is also an old Pauline.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Outside of politics, Tsang has hosted broadcast programmes such as The Recruitment, was an <I>am730</I>  columnist, and has penned several books and memoirs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Studying at St. Paul’s is a time Tsang looks back on with pride. He had a hand in forming the Students’ Association and formed his own literary society with his classmates, making a foray into editing and self-publishing. Taking over from Col Evan Stewart, then Principal the Revd Geoffrey Speak had very large shoes to fill, but proved a worthy successor through his charm, humour, and leadership, carrying the torch for Stewart’s legacy while making key reforms. To this day, he retains cordial ties with the College, participating in reunions and celebratory events.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/61.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":62,"alumni_name":"Tsang Tak Sing GBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"曾德成","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1966","chi_alias":" 政治獻策家","eng_alias":"Political Journalist ","chi_desc":"<p>曾德成在一九六六年畢業於聖保羅書院，後從事《新晚報》及《大公報》記者工作逾卅年，曾任《大公報》總編輯，又曾任各公職，於一九八八至二〇〇八年爲中國人民代表大會代表，在一九九八至二〇〇七年獲特區政府聘爲中央政策組顧問，於二〇〇七至一五年間任民政事務局局長。其兄曾鈺成同爲本校舊生。</p>\r\n<p>書院校風自由開放，曾氏飽讀課外書籍​，又曾任天文學會創會祕書。曾氏任記者及編輯時，支持中華人民共和國政府對香港恢復行使主權。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Tsang Tak Sing graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1966. He was a journalist for over thirty years for the <I>New Evening Post</I> and the <I>Ta Kung Pao</I>, once serving as the latter’s chief editor. He has also held public office, including as a deputy to the National People’s Congress (1988–2008), a consultant for the HKSAR’s Central Policy Unit (1998–2007), and home affairs secretary from 2007 to 2015. His older brother, Jasper, also studied at St. Paul’s. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Within St. Paul’s open environment, Tsang read broadly and had been the founding secretary of the Astronomical Society. While working as a journalist, Tsang supported Hong Kong’s return to the motherland.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/62.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":63,"alumni_name":"Joseph Yam Chi Kwong GBM GBS CBE JP","alumni_name_chi":"任志剛","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1967","chi_alias":"預科畢業 — 金融謀略大帥","eng_alias":"Form 7 - Analytical Economist","chi_desc":"<p>任志剛於一九六五年於聖保羅書院修讀預科，在一九六七年畢業，一九七一年加入政府任統計師，在一九八五年任副金融司，於一九九三年起任金融管理局首任總裁，至二〇〇九年退休，在二〇一七年起爲行政會議成員。</p> <p>任職官員期間，在一九八〇年代見證香港加入聯繫匯率制度，渡過多次股災，在一九九〇年代帶香港過渡回歸、亞洲金融風暴及科網熱潮，在二〇〇〇年代讓香港持續國際金融中心領先地位，渡過二〇〇八年金融海嘯。前特首曾蔭權曾形容任氏爲「親密戰友」。於一九九五年獲贈ＣＢＥ勛章，在二〇〇九年獲頒大紫荊勳章。</p> <p>在學僅兩年，聖保羅書院卻對任氏影響深遠。任氏期間活躍於合唱團，又曾任劍擊學會副會長。眼見身邊同學已選定志向，任氏亦決定不再渾渾噩噩，立志奉獻社會。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Joseph Yam studied sixth form at St. Paul’s College from 1965 to 1967. A statistician and economist, Yam joined the civil service in 1971 and was appointed deputy secretary for monetary affairs by 1985. In 1993, he became the first chief executive of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority, the city’s central bank, a position he held until his retirement in 2009. He has been sitting on the Executive Council since 2017.</p> <p>Across his career, Yam presided over many ups and downs in Hong Kong’s financial history: multiple crashes throughout the 1980s, the Hong Kong dollar entering a peg with the US dollar in 1983, the 1997 Asian financial crisis, the dot-com bubble, the 2008 crash — all while maintaining Hong Kong’s position as an international financial hub. Former chief executive Donald Tsang once described Yam as a ‘comrade-in-arms’. He received the CBE in 1995 and the GBM in 2009.</p> <p>Within two years, St. Paul’s had left a lasting impact on Yam. He was an active chorister, and had been honorary secretary of the Fencing Club. Yam remembers seeing his peers already decided on their future paths; it was under this atmosphere that he chose to devote his life towards bettering society.</p>","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/63.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":64,"alumni_name":"Alan Hoo Hon Ching SBS SC","alumni_name_chi":"胡漢淸","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1968","chi_alias":"政治推導大律師","eng_alias":"Political Consultant","chi_desc":"<p>胡漢淸在一九六八年畢業聖保羅書院，後赴英進修，現爲香港資深大律師，自一九九九年起任基本法硏究中心主席，在二〇〇三年起任中國人民政治協商會議全國委員會委員。</p>\r\n<p>胡氏政治啓蒙由聖保羅說起，當時書院思想開放，胡氏聽畢張奧偉御用大律師在書院演講，已確定日後想從事法律工作，在學時經常到最高法院（今終審法院）旁聽法官審理案件。啓發胡氏的還有常談論國情的同班同學。胡氏不忿當時香港政治社會缺乏漢人領導，立志日後爲港爲國貢獻。一九九〇年，胡氏獲委任御用大律師。現今除法律事務外，經常對政制與公共政策發表評論。胡氏又推動中意民間文化交流，並成立中意友好協進會，在二〇〇四年與二〇〇九年分別獲頒銀紫荊星章和意大利星級司令勛章。</p>\r\n<p>當時課餘除常赴法庭，胡氏亦曾活躍於扶輪少年服務團，組織社區活動服務鄰舍，又活躍於書院籃球部和合唱團。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Alan Hoo graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1968, later furthering his studies in Britain. Hoo is a barrister and one of Hong Kong’s Senior Counsels. He has chaired the Basic Law Institute since 1999 and been a member of the National Committee of the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference from 2003 to 2023.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Hoo had his political awakening within the College’s open environment. After listening to a speech by Queen’s Counsel Sir Oswald Victor Cheung, Hoo often attended sessions in the Supreme Court. Another source of inspiration was some of his classmates’ unbridled passion for discussing China’s politics. Hoo decried a lack of ethnic Chinese leadership in Hong Kong and was determined to serve his country in the future. He was appointed Queen’s Counsel in 1990. In addition to his legal work, he frequently comments on politics and policy. Hoo has promoted cultural exchange between China and Italy by founding the Marco Polo Society. He was awarded the SBS in 2004 and the Grande Officiale of the Order of the Star of Italy in 2009.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Aside from going to court hearings then, Hoo was also active in the Interact Club, which organised community service activities, and was active in the choir and basketball team.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/64.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":65,"alumni_name":"Emeritus Professor Lau Siu Kai GBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"劉兆佳榮休敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1966","chi_alias":"政務社會學家","eng_alias":"Distinguished Sociologist","chi_desc":"<p>劉兆佳在一九六八年於聖保羅書院高六畢業，爲社會學家，曾長年於香港中文大學任敎，曾任社會學系主任，於二〇〇七年退休，曾爲全國港澳硏究會副會長。</p>\r\n<p>劉氏硏究興趣主要圍繞社會及政治發展。在一九七五年由明尼蘇達大學考獲博士學位後返中大任敎，期間著作《Society and Politics in Hong Kong》（「香港社會與政治」，一九八二年英文初版），《一國兩制在香港的實踐》（二〇一五年中文初版），主題不離施政及在港華人兩者之關聯。</p>\r\n<p>劉氏在一九九三年出任港事顧問，此後爲特區及中央政府輔政，包括由二〇〇二至一二年間任香港中央政策組首席顧問，在二〇〇三至一八年爲中國人民政治協商會議全國委員會委員。於一九九九年獲港府委太平紳士，在二〇一二年獲頒金紫荊星章。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Lau Siu Kai graduated from sixth form at St. Paul’s College in 1968. A sociologist, Lau taught sociology at the Chinese University of Hong Kong (CUHK), once heading the university’s sociology department. He retired in 2007 and was most known for his work as vice-president of the Chinese Association of Hong Kong and Macau Studies. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lau’s interests lie in socio-political development. After attaining his doctorate from the University of Minnesota in 1975, Lau began teaching at the CUHK. Selected works include <i>Society and Politics in Hong Kong</i> (1982) and <i>The Practice of ‘One Country, Two Systems’ Policy in Hong Kong</i> (2017); common themes include governance and its connection with Hong Kong’s Chinese population. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lau was appointed a Hong Kong Affairs Adviser to Beijing in 1993, prior to the handover, and has served city and country ever since: he headed Hong Kong’s Central Policy Unit from 2002 to 2012 and was a member of the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference (national committee, 2003–18). He was appointed JP in 1999 and awarded the GBS in 2012.</p> \r\n\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/65.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":66,"alumni_name":"Stephen Ip Shu Kwan GBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"葉澍堃","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1968","chi_alias":"金紫荊局長","eng_alias":"Government Secretary with GBS","chi_desc":"<p>葉澍堃在一九六八年畢業於聖保羅書院，曾在政府中擔任多項要職，如二〇〇〇至〇二年間任財經事務局局長，在二〇〇二至〇七年間任經濟發展及勞工局局長，促進香港經濟與民生發展。於二〇〇一年獲頒金紫荊星章，在二〇〇七年獲委任非官守太平紳士。</p>\r\n<p>退休後，葉氏於各刊物不時撰寫美食評論，在二〇一〇年代起於不同飲食節目擔任主持。</p>\r\n<p>葉氏自立自強，不僅以自修的方式成為執業律師，還主動尋找學習的機會，增廣見聞。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Stephen Ip graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1968. With over thirty years in the civil service, his portfolio is vast and varied. Ip specialised in growing the city’s economy, having served as financial services secretary from 2000 to 2002 and economic development and labour secretary between 2002 and 2007. He was awarded the GBS in 2001 and was appointed a non-official JP in 2007.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After retirement, Ip explored his other passion — food. Today, he is known for his culinary reviews in various publications and as an occasional food show host.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A committed autodidact, Ip has always sought learning opportunities beyond the classroom and is a self-taught solicitor.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/66.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":67,"alumni_name":"Ma Ho Fai GBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"馬豪輝","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1968","chi_alias":"經驗豐富房務律師","eng_alias":"Veteran Solicitor","chi_desc":"<p>馬豪輝在一九六三年入讀聖保羅書院，於一九七〇年高六畢業，後赴海外進修，考獲執業律師資格，專門處理內地房地產法律事宜，助中港交往逾四十年，在二〇〇〇年起獲中國司法部委任委託公證人，於二〇〇八至二〇二三年間任中國人民代表大會代表。</p>\r\n<p>就讀聖保羅書院時，馬氏活躍課外活動，曾任學生長、中文學會會長，​尤嗜書法；亦參與合唱團。後回饋母校，在一九九〇年代曾任本校校董。除中港工作外，馬氏亦擔任多項公職，如自置居所津貼上訴委員團主席及旅遊業監管局主席等，又曾任香港旅遊業議會獨立理事十六年。在二〇〇五年獲港府委任太平紳士，於二〇一七年獲頒金紫荊星章。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Ma Ho Fai enrolled at St. Paul’s College in 1963 and graduated from sixth form in 1970. A veteran solicitor, Ma specialises in conveyancing work between Hong Kong and the mainland and has worked in the profession for over four decades. He has been a China-Appointed attestation officer since 2000 and a National People’s Congress delegate from 2008 to 2023. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ma’s energy can be seen as early as his College days. He was a prefect and a former chairman for the College’s Chinese Association, with a special interest in Chinese calligraphy; Ma was also a chorister. Ma has served the College as a former College council member in the 1990s. In addition to his legal and political work, Ma is also active in the city’s public sphere, and has chaired the Home Purchase Allowance Appeals Committee Panel, the Travel Industry Authority, and for sixteen years served as an independent director of the Travel Industry Council of Hong Kong. He was appointed JP in 2005 and awarded the GBS in 2017. . </p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/67.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":68,"alumni_name":"Chang Ka Mun","alumni_name_chi":"張家敏","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1977","chi_alias":"國務政治專才","eng_alias":"Political Consultant","chi_desc":"<p>張家敏在一九七七年畢業於聖保羅書院，專長國務硏究，曾著近代國史本《建國以來》（一九九七年初版）。曾任觀塘區議員、國務院港事顧問、在一九九八至二〇一三年間任中國人民政治協商會議全國委員會委員，現任全國港澳硏究會理事及馮氏集團利豐硏究中心董事總經理。</p>\r\n<p>就讀書院時，中三一次社會探訪讓他開始關注民生，高中時活躍於各學會，曾任乒乓球學會主席，初六任書院扶輪少年服務團主席，籌辦活動萌生其社會服務意識，爲日後硏究發展鋪路。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Chang Ka Mun graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1977. He specialises in social studies, having authored the historical anthology <I>Jian Guo Yi Lai </I>(1997). He was a former Kwun Tong District councillor, a Hong Kong Affairs Adviser to Beijing, and was on the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference (national committee) from 1998 to 2013. He is currently a member of the Chinese Association of Hong Kong and Macau Studies and a senior adviser to the conglomerate, Fung Group.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>While he was studying in Form 3, a particular outing inspired Chang’s interest in service; he became active in extracurriculars in his senior secondary years, once chairing the Table Tennis Club, and led the Interact Club in his lower sixth year. Through organising activities and visits, Chang developed his interest in serving society, foreshadowing his current duties.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/68.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":69,"alumni_name":"Raymond So Wai Man BBS JP ","alumni_name_chi":"蘇偉文","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1982","chi_alias":"商業管理之才","eng_alias":"Business Management Expert","chi_desc":"<p>蘇偉文在一九八二年畢業於聖保羅書院，曾於香港中文大學任敎金融學，亦曾任恒生管理學院（今恒生大學）商學院院長及浸會大學持續敎育學院院長，曾任職運輸及房屋局副局長。</p>\r\n<p>走上官場非蘇氏本來預想，轉捩點可說是一九九七年的亞洲金融風暴。危機中，民怨四起，蘇氏遂希望服務社會，不時發文評論及釐淸常見誤解，曾爲多個政府諮詢委員會委員，於二〇一三年獲委太平紳士，在二〇一六年獲頒銅紫荊星章。</p>\r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Raymond So graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1982. So has taught finance at the Chinese University of Hong Kong, was a dean of Hang Seng Management College’s (now Hang Seng University) School of Business and a former dean of continuing education at Baptist University, and was formerly one of Hong Kong’s transport and housing undersecretaries. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Entering the civil service was not So’s original career path; the 1997 Asian financial crisis can be described as a personal turning point. So saw discontent among Hong Kong’s citizens, and has since been an active commentator, clearing up common misconceptions the public may have, and has served on many government consultative committees. He was appointed JP in 2013 and awarded the BBS in 2016.</p>\r\n","category":"learnedmen","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/69.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"}],"teachers":[{"id":70,"alumni_name":"The Reverend Vincent John Stanton","alumni_name_chi":"史丹頓牧師","lifespan":"(1817–1891)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"聖保羅書院創辦牧師","eng_alias":"Founder of St. Paul’s College","chi_desc":"<p>史丹頓生於英國布里斯托，爲英格蘭聖公會首名派遣本港之牧師，期間創辦現今聖約翰座堂及聖保羅書院。史丹頓年少時已熱衷傳道，一八三八年不惜從劍橋輟學遠赴澳門，當了三年家庭敎師後歸英。一八四三年獲委香港牧師一職，遂與妻子偕行，同年抵港。</p> <p>香港開埠，各敎會迅速在港建立常駐設施，史丹頓作爲聖公宗代表，一面需要從速募資覓才興建敎堂與學塾，同時兼顧居民需要。 由於當時政府只著重洋人的敎育發展，故史丹頓欲爲當地華人設校惜遲遲不獲批，儘管史丹頓來港前，政府已為聖公會預留用地，惟於一八四五年才得己連拿利地皮可作設校，及後隨即動土。</p> <p>一八四九年五月，施美夫牧師獲祝聖爲維多利亞敎區首任會督。同年秋季，史丹頓所設學塾（臨時命名爲「英格蘭敎會英華學塾」）及聖約翰敎堂（今聖約翰座堂）正式開幕。學塾首年有學童卅四名，由史丹頓本人與岑馬士（James Summers）帶領。翌年三月，施美夫抵港，當時史丹頓察覺身健康轉差，遂將管轄權轉予施美夫後功成身退，四月啓航返英。</p>\r\n<p>及後，身體抱恙的史丹頓未能再臨香港，而他對自身海外的功績仍畢生難忘。此後史丹頓繼續在英格蘭佈道，至一八八八年退休。</p>\r\n\r\n<p>史丹頓在港短短的七年間，為聖公會(維多利亞教區)紮下穩實基石，歷百七載繼續服務市民，實在功不可沒。如今，本校的班際足球賽以「史丹頓盃」為名致以紀念。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Bristol, Vincent Stanton was Hong Kong’s first colonial chaplain (1843–50). During his mission, he founded what would become St. John’s Cathedral and St. Paul’s College.</p> <p>Stanton’s missionary zeal was apparent in his youth: he interrupted his studies at Cambridge to work as a family tutor in Macau in 1838, returning in 1841. His appointment for his new mission came in 1843, and he immediately set sail with his wife.</p> <p>Stanton had the unenviable task of establishing the Church of England’s presence on the island. This meant finding funds, materials, and people to build and run both a church and a school, all while tending to the local settlers’ needs. To complicate matters, the administration prioritised schools for Europeans, whereas Stanton was determined to build a school for local Chinese. Land was already set aside for the church before he arrived, but it took until 1845 to find a place for the school on Glenealy. Construction began soon after.</p> <p>In May 1849, in England, George Smith was consecrated as the first Bishop of Victoria. That same autumn, Stanton’s school (tentatively named the ‘Church of England Anglo-Chinese School’) and St. John’s Church finally opened. The school had thirty-four boys and one headmaster, James Summers. Bishop Smith arrived in Hong Kong in March 1850. Seeing his work at a close and with deteriorating health, Stanton transferred authority and ownership to Smith and left for England in April.</p> <p>Although poor health never permitted his return, Stanton fondly remembered Hong Kong, proud of his brief yet fruitful stay. He continued his ministry until retiring in 1888.</p> <p>Within seven years, Stanton laid the foundations for the Anglican Church’s presence in Hong Kong, foundations that still stand 170 years later. Currently, the College’s inter-class football competition is named the Stanton Cup in his memory.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/70.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":71,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend George Smith ","alumni_name_chi":"施美夫會督（宋美）","lifespan":"(1815–1871)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"聖保羅書院首任監院","eng_alias":"The First Warden of St. Paul’s College ","chi_desc":"<p>施美夫一八四九至六五年間爲維多利亞敎區首任會督，兼任聖保羅書院首任監院。</p> <p>施美夫於東亞致力推動敎會發展，於一八四四至四六年間考察中國各商埠，至一八四九年獲祝聖會督，旋攜英皇制誥及聖保羅書院憲規起航。</p> <p>維多利亞敎區起初涵蓋中日所有商埠，故施美夫長期不在港，經常四處奔波以助英國海外傳道會（Church Missionary Society）於他處設佈道團。</p> <p>至於史丹頓牧師所設學塾，英格蘭敎會在一八四九年決定將其命名爲「聖保羅書院」。施美夫一八五〇年抵港後，隨即擴建校舍，並於一八五一年九月五日以同一名義重開。</p> <p>香港開埠首二十年間，敎育方面十分寬鬆。政府雖與各辦學團體合作，但不會插手制定課程或設官立學校。於是，施美夫須從敎會培育華人傳道士及政府急需翻譯人材之間平衡，爲此而四處招聘敎師。所聘人材既有傳道士，又有非信徒，皆讓書院早年得以穩長。在施美夫及其領導的聖保羅書院規模下，影響後來一八六〇年代敎育司署初期成立的運行模式。</p> <p>惟香港此時仍缺重要基建配合，施美夫也因用心勞碌而致身心疲勞，於一八六五年退休。本校班際籃球賽命名「施美夫盃」，記念此名爲香港及聖保羅發展貢獻良多的首名監院。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>George Smith was the first Bishop of Victoria (1849–65) and the first warden of St. Paul’s College <I>ex officio</I>.</p> <p>Smith prominently advocated the church’s expansion in East Asia. From 1844 to 1846, he surveyed China’s treaty ports as part of his mission. Shortly following his consecration in 1849, Smith set sail for Hong Kong, letters patent and the statutes for St. Paul’s College in hand.</p> <p>The diocese initially covered all Chinese and Japanese treaty ports. This meant Smith spent most of his tenure outside Hong Kong, helping the Church Missionary Society build local missions.</p> <p>As for the Anglo-Chinese school Stanton built, it was already decided in 1849 that it would become known as ‘St. Paul’s College’. Smith worked to expand the campus soon after arriving in 1850, with the school reopening under its current name on 5 September 1851.</p> <p>Between the 1840s and the 1860s, Hong Kong was marked by a <I>laissez-faire</I> educational policy, with the government working closely with, but not directly intervening in, educational organisations. As such, Smith’s direction for St. Paul’s balanced the church’s goal of training native clergymen with the government’s goal of providing interpreters; to this end, Smith sought tutors — missionaries and laymen alike — who steered the College toward stable growth. In turn, Smith and St. Paul’s College helped shape the early direction of what would become the Education Department by giving advice and ideas. </p> <p>Education aside, life in Hong Kong remained physically taxing, and Smith resigned in 1865. Our College’s inter-class basketball competition is named the Smith Cup in his memory.</p> ","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/71.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":72,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend Charles Richard Alford","alumni_name_chi":"柯爾福會督","lifespan":"(1816–1898)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"維多利亞敎區第二任會督及聖保羅書院監院","eng_alias":"The Second Bishop of Victoria and warden of St. Paul’s College","chi_desc":"<p>柯爾福一八六七年至七二年間爲維多利亞敎區第二任會督，兼任聖保羅書院監院。</p>\r\n<p>在任期間，柯爾福經歷多重困難。他一如前任施美夫主敎，四訪中日商埠，助敎區成長，但當時各佈道會規模已難以單人應付。同時，英格蘭敎會內對海外會督於敎區內的角色意見不一；後來又計劃將敎區分割，上述分歧使柯爾福短短五年後請辭。</p>\r\n<p>聖保羅書院此時面臨重重挑戰。一方面港、英多方停止補貼；另一方面，受一八六六年金融風暴波及，書院失去既有存款陷於財困，於一八六七年被迫停辦，至一八六八年才能重開。多年來，柯爾福要爲書院親自供款以保運作，可惜及至一八七〇年書院終須臨時停辦。</p>\r\n<p>柯爾福辭任後，一生繼續在英格蘭四處服務敎會。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Charles Alford was the second Bishop of Victoria (1867–72) and warden of St. Paul’s College <I>ex officio</I>.</p> <p>Alford’s tenure was characterised by the bishopric’s growing pains. Like Bishop Smith, Alford sailed across China and Japan, fulfilling duties for the episcopal see. However, unlike Smith’s time, the mission’s scope was expanding. The Church of England was mired in debates on what role an overseas bishop should take; this was compounded by proposals to partition the diocese. These disagreements played a significant part in Alford’s resignation, only five years after his consecration.</p> <p>St. Paul’s College, too, was going through growing pains as funding from the British and Hong Kong governments dried up. This was worsened by the panic of 1866, resulting in the College losing its savings and closing temporarily in 1867. From 1868, Alford kept the College afloat partly with his private purse, yet this was not enough to avoid its temporary closure from 1870 onwards.</p> <p>After resigning, Alford continued his parish duties across England until his death.</p>\r\n","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/72.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":73,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend John Shaw Burdon","alumni_name_chi":"包爾騰會督","lifespan":"(1826–1907)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"漢譯經文的聖保羅書院監院","eng_alias":"Warden of St. Paul’s College and Translator of Scripture","chi_desc":"<p>包爾騰生於蘇格蘭格拉斯哥，爲傳敎士及翻譯，一八七四至九七年間任維多利亞敎區第三任會督，兼任聖保羅書院監院。</p> <p>包爾騰一八五三年起赴海外佈道，先在蘇浙滬傳敎，後調派北京，期間於京師同文館敎授英語，爲中國首間現代洋式學校首批敎師之一。包爾騰駐京時亦開始聯同其他傳敎士漢譯經文，後來成爲畢生事業，譯有《公禱書》及淺文理和合本（淺白文言）聖經，亦參與國語和合本（白話文）部份早期譯本，後者於一九一九年面世，至今仍爲華人新敎徒常用之聖經本。</p> <p>獲祝聖維多利亞會督後，包爾騰遂爲華南和日本信眾服務。經營聖保羅書院方面，包爾騰獲悉前任會督曾面對的困難後，在一八七五年聖保羅書院重開後決定將書院分拆成神學班（寄宿）與普通班（走讀／非寄宿）。在一八八〇年又增辦專爲洋人而設的Hongkong Public School 校舍【註】，以補助敎區收入。以致一八九四年爆發鼠疫，聖保羅書院也得以復原，到一八九六年有逾百名書生就讀。</p> <p>退任後，包爾騰繼續於中國多處傳敎，至一八九九年返英。</p>\r\n<p>包爾騰一生致力讓華人易於接受基督敎，當中曾建議聖餐禮換用米餠和綠茶代替餠和葡萄酒。而華人書寫仍用文言文之時，包爾騰推廣簡易文體，助基督敎在中國快速散佈，間接促使白話文普及，部分更漸漸塑成我們現今的日用語。</p>\r\n<p>【註】　Hongkong Public School（「香港公校」）雖爲座落己連拿利校舍之英格蘭敎會學校，惟辦學方面與聖保羅書院本身毫無關聯。「Public School」乃跟隨英格蘭之傳統意義，「public」指開放予公眾報讀，與私人授課作對比，並非由公帑資助，實際較接近私立學校的定義；Hongkong Public School亦有徵收學費，不應與義學混淆。該校後於一八九一年停辦。政府曾於憲報暫譯「港眾男義學」，惟該譯名僅供當時參考，無任何正式地位。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Glasgow, John Burdon was a missionary, translator, the third Bishop of Victoria (1874–97), and warden of St. Paul’s College <i>ex officio</i>.</p> <p>Burdon’s ministry in China began in 1853, in Shanghai and eastern China. Later, he was assigned to Beijing, where he was the first English teacher at the Tongwen Guan, which was one of China’s first schools for learning foreign languages. There, Burdon began his other lifelong mission of translating scripture into Chinese. Collaborating with other missionaries, he co-translated the <i>Book of Common Prayer</i> and the Easy Wenli [literary Chinese] Union Version of the Bible, while indirectly contributing to the Chinese Union Version, which remains the most used Chinese Protestant Bible today.</p> <p>After his consecration, Burdon reopened St. Paul’s in 1875 while tending to his flock across southern China and Japan. Aware of Alford’s previous difficulties, he split the College into two programmes: a general education stream and a theological school. He additionally founded the Hongkong Public School in 1880,* which catered to European residents as an additional revenue source. Even though the bubonic plague in 1894 hurt enrolment, St. Paul’s recovered, and by 1896, it had more than 100 students.</p> <p>Post-retirement, Burdon continued missionary duties in China until 1899.</p> <p>Burdon paid much attention to localising Christianity for the Chinese. One of his more interesting ideas was to replace bread and wine in the Eucharist with rice cakes and green tea. In an age before vernacular written Chinese was the norm, Burdon’s advocacy of using easier Chinese forms aided Christianity’s spread across China, which in turn played a key role in shaping standard written Chinese as we know it today.</p> \r\n<p>* While Hongkong Public School was an Anglican school resident in the Glenealy campus, it was institutionally unaffiliated with St. Paul’s College, and had a separate administration. Burdon explicitly modelled the school after public schools in England, which are privately-funded institutions, not publicly-funded. The school folded in 1891.</p> ","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/73.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":74,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend Joseph Charles Hoare","alumni_name_chi":"霍約瑟會督","lifespan":"(1851–1906)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"傳敎士敎育之父","eng_alias":"Innovator in Missionary Training","chi_desc":"<p>霍約瑟在聖公會家庭成長，一八七八至九八年間爲寧波三一書院監院，後於一八九八年獲祝聖爲第四任維多利亞敎區會督，兼任聖保羅書院監院，一九〇六年歿於靑山灣。</p> <p>在寧波授課二十餘年間，霍約瑟學懂寧波話，並習得自身獨特的敎育模式，稱之爲「evangelical theory through ambulando」（略譯「修行佈道習神學」），主要圍繞出外鍛煉：霍約瑟曾親自帶領學徒到各偏僻村莊佈道，除了自身示範之餘又向學徒說敎，佈道團每次遠行可長達十週。三一書院在霍約瑟帶領期間，成功培育數十名華人傳敎士，於眾多地區中首屈一指。</p> <p>獲祝聖抵港後，霍約瑟見敎區急需本地傳敎士，遂中止聖保羅書院實行的普通班，依三一書院模式轉型爲神學院，鼓勵華人效力聖公會佈道。期間與英國海外傳道會達成協議，敎學由會督親自管理，傳道會則提供額外人手及資源援助。除聖保羅書院外，霍約瑟亦有份於二十世紀初籌建聖士提反書院。</p> <p>可惜，霍約瑟不幸遇難，結束短暫的留港旅程。於一九〇六年九月十八日，當時珠江口受颱風襲擊，霍約瑟會督正與四名學生在佈道團完結之際，由屯門啓航返校時，船隻遭風浪打沉，所有人不幸遇溺。颱風終致逾萬人喪命，史稱丙午風災。</p> <p>及至二〇二一年爲霍約瑟一一五週年忌日，本校於靑山灣舉辦悼念儀式。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Joseph Hoare was born into a family of clergymen. He ran Trinity College in Ningbo (1878–98) and was the fourth Bishop of Victoria and warden of St. Paul’s College <i>ex officio</i> from 1898 until his death in 1906.</p> <p>In Ningbo, Hoare mastered the local tongue and honed his teaching methods, termed ‘evangelical theology through <i>ambulando</i>’. Field missions were front and centre: Hoare led students on preaching tours through rural villages, teaching directly by example; tours sometimes lasted up to ten weeks. His leadership of Trinity College bore fruit, producing dozens of Chinese clergymen by the 1890s, all determined to spread the gospel.</p> <p>Following his consecration and arrival in Hong Kong, Hoare set about transforming St. Paul’s College into a school similar to Trinity: a dedicated seminary that would provide much-needed native clergymen to the bishopric. To this end, he ended the College’s general education programme in 1901 and cooperated with the Church Missionary Society (CMS), where the Bishop taught directly and the CMS provided additional personnel and resources. Outside of St. Paul’s, Hoare was part of a coalition that organised the founding of St. Stephen’s College at the turn of the century.</p> <p>Sadly, Hoare’s work came to an abrupt end. While returning from an <i>ambulando</i> preaching tour, Hoare and four of his students perished at sea in Castle Peak Bay on 18 September 1906, among the many thousands who died in the 1906 Hong Kong typhoon.</p> <p>In September 2021, the College held a commemorative service at Castle Peak Bay, marking 115 years since his death.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/74.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":75,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend Gerard Heath Lander","alumni_name_chi":"倫義華會督","lifespan":"(1861–1934)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"聖公會敎育改革領袖","eng_alias":"Transformer of SKH Education","chi_desc":"<p>倫義華一九〇七至二〇年間爲維多利亞敎區第五任會督，兼任聖保羅書院監院。</p>\r\n<p>倫義華早年在利物浦服務敎會近二十年。於霍約瑟遇難後敎會急需繼任人，倫義華獲祝聖後立即起航。當時香港敎育環境正迅速轉變，倫義華尤其煩惱在缺乏霍約瑟獨特敎學模式及領導下，聖保羅書院的前路：</p>\r\n<p>英國海外傳道會欲見聖保羅重歸創校時的敎學模式，爲華人提供西式敎育，亦認爲將現有神學院遷往內地能有助募資收生。</p>\r\n<p>其時港府正籌建大學，書院如可恢復初時全面的敎學模式，力助學生進修，於收生方面將大有受惠，更甚可平衡收支。</p>\r\n<p>倫義華本人則希望任內能釐淸維多利亞會督、英格蘭敎會、英國海外傳道會和位於中國內地各會督之間的關係。同時，他希望能善用己連拿利地皮。</p> <p>抵港後，倫義華會督臨時延長霍約瑟與英國海外傳道會之協定，自己繼續爲書院尋新定向。聖保羅書院終於一九〇九年以舊有模式重開，敎學方面由英國海外傳道會全面接管，會督的角色則與現今之校監相似。</p>\r\n<p>書院以外，倫義華見證敎會於香港急速成長：一九一二年中華聖公會創立，後成爲自身敎省，獨立於英格蘭敎會；聖保羅女書院（今聖保羅男女中學）於一九一五年創立；一九一〇年代，拔萃女書院與男書院雙雙購獲現有校址，而聖保羅書院本身亦於同期擴建校園，收生量倍增。</p>\r\n<p>倫義華退任後，他回到英國倫敦北部新巴尼特管理敎區敎堂，至一九三三年榮休。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Gerard Lander was the fifth Bishop of Victoria (1907–20) and warden of St. Paul’s College, <i>ex officio</i>.</p>\r\n<p>Lander’s early ministry was in the port city of Liverpool, where he preached for nearly twenty years. St. Paul’s College weighed on Lander’s mind as he set sail for his new post. In the absence of Hoare’s hands-on leadership, the College once again found itself on the cusp of change.</p>\r\n<p>The Church Missionary Society (CMS) wanted St. Paul’s to return to its Anglo-Chinese roots; additionally, it believed moving the existing seminary to the mainland would attract more students and support.</p>\r\n<p>The Hong Kong administration was planning a university; were St. Paul’s to revert to a general programme and prepare its students for further study, enrolment would likely benefit — perhaps it would even balance the books!</p>\r\n<p>Lander himself wanted to see the Glenealy site used better, and to clarify the bishop’s role <i>vis-&#224;-vis</i> the Church of England, the CMS, and his colleagues across China.</p>\r\n<p>Once in Hong Kong, Lander extended his late predecessor’s agreement while labouring to find a new direction. St. Paul’s College eventually reopened in 1909 as an Anglo-Chinese school, now under full CMS management. Lander continued as the College’s warden, albeit with a reduced supervisory role.</p>\r\n<p>Lander also oversaw the church’s further maturation: the Chung Hua Sheng Kung Hui was established in 1912, St. Paul’s Girls’ College (now St. Paul’s Co-educational College) was founded in 1915, the Diocesan Girls’ and Boys’ schools each acquired new sites, and St. Paul’s College itself expanded throughout the 1910s.</p>\r\n<p>After Lander resigned, he returned to England, where he served in a parish church in New Barnet (north London) until 1933.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/75.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":76,"alumni_name":"The Reverend Arthur Dudley Stewart","alumni_name_chi":"史超域牧師","lifespan":"(1877–1953)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"聖保羅書院革新要臣","eng_alias":"Reformer of St. Paul’s College","chi_desc":"<p>史超域生於福州，一家爲傳敎士，一九〇九至三〇年間爲聖保羅書院第七任校長【註】。而其弟為第八任校長史伊尹上校。</p>\r\n<p>一九〇五年末，史超域赴港在聖保羅書院任敎，當時學校由霍約瑟會督領導。霍約瑟遭難後，倫義華會督接替數年間，史超域轉至聖士提反書院任敎，亦在廣東話上學有所成。一九〇九年，聖保羅書院重開，史超域獲委校長。</p>\r\n<p>身作校長，史超域負責重新制定書院敎育方針。他保留固有傳統，繼續採用全面英語敎學，又保留駐校傳敎士，同時致力讓眾華人能負擔英式基督敎敎育。此時書院身心並重，授敎之餘亦着重體能訓練。到一九二〇年代，本校常於校際運動賽事名列前茅，書生漸漸得蒙受洗，舊生亦於社會各界建功。</p>\r\n<p>在倫義華會督與英國海外傳道會支持，書院收生持續上升，校園漸變擁擠。史超域爲書院募款，監督多項擴展工程，包括一九一一年聖保羅堂／伍廷芳堂、一九一九年寄宿舍（今敎堂禮賓樓）及一九二五年之實驗室。史超域上任時，聖保羅書院僅有十八名師生，離任時則有超過五百名學生。</p>\r\n<p>離任校長後，史超域繼續擔任三年校牧，之後赴英，接替倫義華在倫敦北部新巴尼特管理的敎堂，又曾短暫返港於聖士提反任敎。一九六四年史超域樓落成，以記念兩兄弟半世紀以來夙夜不懈領導書院。</p>\r\n<p>【註】　本校現今視史丹頓爲首任校長，施美夫爲第二任校長（但爲首任監院），柯爾福爲第三任校長云云。</p>\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Fuzhou to a missionary family, Arthur Stewart was the seventh principal of St. Paul’s College,* serving from 1909 to 1930. He was an older brother to his successor, Col Evan Stewart.</p>\r\n<p>Stewart joined St. Paul’s in late 1905 during Bishop Hoare’s direction. While Bishop Lander tended to St. Paul’s College after Hoare’s death, Stewart briefly taught at St. Stephen’s College and learnt Cantonese. In 1909, Stewart was appointed headmaster at the newly-reopened Anglo-Chinese St. Paul’s College.</p>\r\n<p>Responsible for the College’s educational direction, Stewart continued long-standing policies such as teaching in English and having a resident missionary, while prioritising affordable access to a Christian education. In addition to the spiritual element, much emphasis was placed on physical exercise. The 1920s saw St. Paul’s as a frequent top contender in inter-school athletics; student baptisms were a regular sight, and graduates found success across many fields.</p>\r\n<p>With both Bishop Lander and the Church Missionary Society’s backing, enrolment rose steadily, which strained the College campus. Stewart raised funds and oversaw multiple extensions being built, including St. Paul’s Church / Wu Ting Fang Hall (1911), the College Hostel (1919) (now the Church Guest House), and the College laboratories (1925). When Stewart took over, St. Paul’s College only had eighteen students and staff; by the time of his retirement, the College had over 500 boys.</p>\r\n<p>Stewart remained as College chaplain for three more years, before succeeding Bishop Lander’s vicarage at New Barnet. He returned to Hong Kong briefly to teach at St. Stephen’s. The Stewart Building at St. Paul’s, opened in 1964, honours the two brothers who had given so much to the College over half a century.</p>\r\n<p>* Retroactively, the Revd Vincent Stanton is considered the first principal of the College; Bishop Smith the second (but the first warden), Bishop Alford the third, and so on.</p>\r\n\r\n","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/76.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":77,"alumni_name":"Colonel Evan George Stewart DSO OBE ED","alumni_name_chi":"史伊尹上校","lifespan":"(1892–1958)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"聖保羅復光之父","eng_alias":"Architect of St. Paul’s College’s Revival","chi_desc":"<p>史伊尹上校從一九三〇年始擔任聖保羅書院第八任校長，爲第七任校長史超域牧師弟輩。</p> <p>一九一〇年，史伊尹應兄輩邀請，赴聖保羅書院授課，一九一三年返都柏林繼續就學。一戰爆發，史伊尹決心保家衞國，於一九一五至一九一九年效力英國陸軍，曾於法國前線作戰，一九二〇年再赴港。</p> <p>史伊尹軍人背景於書院體育發展大派用場，在港時亦活躍於義勇防衞軍。一九三〇年承繼兄業，使聖保羅持續穩長，本校舊生三〇年代於香港多處又以聖保羅書院名義設立免費夜校。</p> <p>一九四一年末香港保衞戰，史伊尹再度踏入戰火，與聖保羅一眾師生死守我城，惟終究敗北，香港日佔時期在戰俘營苦渡。一九四五年香港重光，史伊尹須立刻返英療傷，要到一九四七年才得回港。</p> <p>史伊尹離港期間，聖公會決定臨時將聖保羅書院與聖保羅女書院合倂成男女中學。史伊尹返港後與胡素貞博士攜手帶領新校，但男校同學會反對兩校永久合倂，要求獨立復辦聖保羅書院，最終一九五〇年兩校分離，惟未能復用己連拿利校舍，要遷址般咸道（香港大學對面聖約翰堂舊址）。</p> <p>復校後多靠舊生、戰前老師及史伊尹本人重視自律的治校方式，聖保羅書院得以快速復元。一九五三年，黃明謙堂落成；一九五五年，圖書館重置等……</p> <p>一九六四年落成之史超域樓以兩兄弟命名，記念兩人半世紀以來夙夜不懈領導書院。</p> <p>本展另有幸與史伊尹獨子史犖文上校訪談，可按此<a href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLgcaQ3_wRoR1zZSFiEKWeFstZ8lppIVZa\">連結</a>觀賞。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Evan Stewart was the eighth principal of St. Paul’s College, serving from 1930 until his death in 1958. He is a younger brother to the Revd Arthur Stewart, the College’s seventh principal.</p> <p>In 1910, Evan joined his older brother to teach at St. Paul’s before going to Dublin for further studies in 1913. As war broke out, Stewart joined the army, serving from 1915 to 1919, during which he fought on the French front. He returned to Hong Kong in 1920.</p> <p>Stewart’s military background played an important part in the College’s physical education drive, and he was additionally active in the Hong Kong Volunteer Defence Corps. Upon taking the reins, Evan continued Arthur Stewart’s success in growing the College. Throughout the 1930s, College alumni ran free night schools on campus and in locations across Hong Kong.</p> <p>The Battle of Hong Kong in December 1941 saw Stewart on the frontlines once again. Together with Pauline teachers and students, they risked their lives in defence, but to no avail. Stewart was imprisoned in a prisoner-of-war camp throughout the Japanese occupation. Upon the liberation of Hong Kong, Stewart had to return to England to treat his sustained injuries. He would not return until 1947.</p> <p>In Stewart’s absence, St. Paul’s Girls’ College was temporarily merged with St. Paul’s College to form one of Hong Kong’s first co-educational secondary schools. Stewart and Catherine F. Woo became co-principals of the merged college. However, a group of alumni started a campaign to bring back the boys' school. St. Paul’s eventually separated from the co-educational school in 1950 but no longer occupied the Glenealy campus; instead, the College took over the site of St. John’s Hall, opposite the University of Hong Kong.</p> <p>With help from alumni and pre-war College staff, Stewart’s discipline enabled the College’s rapid rebuilding. The Wong Ming Him Hall was completed in 1953, the College library was restored in 1955, etc. </p> <p>The Stewart Building, which opened in 1964, pays tribute to the two brothers who have given so much to the college over the past half-century.</p> <p>Click <a href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLgcaQ3_wRoR1zZSFiEKWeFstZ8lppIVZa\">here</a> for an interview with Evan’s only son, Col Michael Stewart, specially prepared as part of this exhibition.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/77.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":78,"alumni_name":"The Reverend Geoffrey Lowrey Speak","alumni_name_chi":"史璧琦牧師","lifespan":"(1924–2000)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"聖保羅重建領袖","eng_alias":"Architect of St. Paul’s College’s Revitalisation","chi_desc":"<p>史璧琦在一九五四年赴聖保羅書院任敎，於一九五九至六七年間爲聖保羅書院第九任校長，後任港島中學（Island School）創校校長及英基學校協會創辦人。</p> <p>於英國約克郡當助理牧師時，史璧琦偶遇何明華會督，向其表示有意赴海外服務，後受何會督邀請，在一九五四年抵港，開始在本校任敎，期間制定書院預科課程。史璧琦爲人親靄，平易近人，校內暱稱「Speak佬」。</p> <p>史伊尹上校亡故，史璧琦在一九五九年奉委校長一職，遂備校園重建計劃，所建樓羣至今大多依然屹立。校政方面，史璧琦就任期間書院之敎育管理職責由英國海外傳道會轉至校董會，見證書院進一步自主。及後，史璧琦協助學生會於一九六三年成立，爲香港中學最早出現的學生組織之一，六、七〇年代附屬學會數量倍增。學生會後來成爲學生探索課外活動之平臺，供學生認識班級以外志同道合之朋輩。自遷址般咸道後，聖保羅於史璧琦任內得以擴展、現代化、校園活動更趨蓬勃。</p> <p>一九六〇年代中，在港洋人敎育需求急增，史璧琦在一九六七年辭任，赴港島中學任首任校長。一九七〇年，史璧琦成爲英基學校協會祕書，助英基成爲全港最大國際學校辦學團體。</p> <p>史璧琦在一九八四年返英，啓程前重返聖保羅書院，作主禮嘉賓出席畢業禮，於舊校致辭。總結一生，史璧琦對戰後的香港英語敎育貢獻不菲。</p> <p>本展另有幸與史璧琦遺孀訪談，可按此<a href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLgcaQ3_wRoR0ygITwp0UT8oOZJtyAA6l8\">連結</a>觀賞。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Geoffrey Speak was the ninth principal of St. Paul’s College, serving from 1959 to 1967. Afterwards, he became the founding principal of Island School and a founder of the English Schools Foundation (ESF).</p> <p>While a curate in Yorkshire, Speak had a chance meeting with Bishop R.O. Hall and expressed his interest in teaching abroad. He travelled to Hong Kong on the Bishop’s invitation and joined St. Paul’s in 1954, where he gave form to the College’s matriculation course. His affable demeanour earned him the sobriquet <I>Speak-loh</I> (‘Ol’ Speak’).</p> <p>Col Stewart’s death sparked the search for a successor. Speak was appointed in 1959 and oversaw the Campus Reconstruction Project, most of which still stands to this day. Administratively, teaching responsibilities were transferred from the Church Missionary Society to the College Council, an affirmation of the College’s ability to manage itself. Speak also gave his blessing to the College’s Student Association. Founded in 1963, it is among the first such organisations in Hong Kong secondary schools, providing a conduit for students to explore hobbies and meet like-minded peers; the number of clubs climbed quickly in the following decades. St. Paul’s was an expanded, modernised, and vibrant presence on Bonham Road under Speak.</p> <p>By the mid-1960s, demand for educating native English speakers in Hong Kong was rising. Speak left St. Paul’s in 1967 to become the first principal of Island School. In 1970, he became the ESF’s secretary, aiding its growth. Today, the ESF is Hong Kong’s largest English language international school organisation.</p> <p>Speak returned to England in 1984, but not before appearing as the guest speaker for our speech day that year. He left an indelible mark on Hong Kong’s post-war English education.</p> <p>Click <a href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLgcaQ3_wRoR0ygITwp0UT8oOZJtyAA6l8\">here</a> for an interview with Mrs Speak, who also taught at St. Paul’s, specially prepared as part of this exhibition.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/78.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":79,"alumni_name":"Timothy Ha Wing Ho MBE JP ","alumni_name_chi":"夏永豪","lifespan":null,"grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"前衞敎育家","eng_alias":"Ingenious Educator","chi_desc":"<p>夏永豪爲香港敎育家，前任立法局議員（一九九一至九五年，委任議席），在一九六八至二〇〇六年間任聖保羅書院第十任校長。</p> <p>任內一九七〇年於預科班引入男女敎育，同年起與心光學校合作，錄取視障學生。一九七八年，書院新增泳池；退休後同年，本校新南翼大廈啓用。</p> <p>夏氏常爲聖保羅書院瞻念，着重確保師生有適妥器具面對未來：在一九八〇年代初爲學校購入電腦，於一九八五年成爲本港最早一批引入普通話班之中學。一九九〇年代全球化加速，夏氏推行旅行學習團，在二〇〇二年成立「環球敎室」，至今每年校內不同部門也會擧辦國內海外考察團（全球疫情大流行時除外），讓學生增廣見聞。</p> <p>同時，夏氏致力鞏固學生「聖保羅」身份，在一九七一年起逢五年辦校慶。一九七五年又重推社制，旨在凝聚學生歸屬感。</p> <p>聖保羅書院以外，夏氏亦一生效力聖公會，主攻敎會敎育事務，退休後至今仍爲聖公會敎育顧問。</p> \r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Timothy Ha is an educator and former legislator. He served as our tenth principal from 1968 until 2006, and as an appointed legislator from 1991 to 1995.</p> <p>As principal, Ha introduced limited co-education into the sixth form in 1970. The same year, St. Paul’s began a collaboration with the Ebenezer School, admitting visually impaired students. The College added a swimming pool in 1978, and the South Wing was nearing completion at the time of his departure.</p> <p>Ha ensured St. Paul’s and its students were adequately equipped to face the future, purchasing computers for the library in the early 1980s and introducing Mandarin classes as early as 1985, one of the first secondary schools to do so. 1995 saw our first study abroad tour, and in 2002, Ha initiated our Global Classroom programme — overseas study trips — which have become a mainstay in College life, except during periods of global pandemics.</p> <p>Ha worked to foster unity and belonging in the College. Since 1971, the College has celebrated its anniversary every five years. In 1975, Ha reintroduced the College’s house system.</p> <p>In addition to St. Paul’s, Ha has served in key positions of education in the Sheng Kung Hui and continues to serve in a consultative capacity.</p> \r\n","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/79.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":80,"alumni_name":"John Richard Kennard","alumni_name_chi":"甘納德","lifespan":null,"grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"才藝敎育領袖 ","eng_alias":"Leader of arts-in-residence and academic-in-residence programmes","chi_desc":"<p>甘納德在二〇〇六至一四年間任聖保羅書院第十一任校長，在任期間爲校園生活質素持續改善，擴大環球敎室規模，帶領書院調整過渡「新高中」學制。</p> <p>二〇〇八年及二〇一〇年分別爲書院黃鳴謙堂及禮堂進行翻新工程，又於二〇〇七年推出駐校藝術家計劃，皆讓學生課外活動更爲多姿多彩。此外亦於二〇一三年推出駐校學者計劃，推廣學術交流，同年書院小學部再遷至薄扶林道新址。</p> <p>在港時又深硏本校歷史，爲本展覽提供寶貴參考資料，辭任後又曾於中國、馬來西亞、波蘭、瑞士及澳洲各學校擔任校長。</p> \r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>John Kennard is the College’s eleventh principal, serving from 2006 to 2014. His tenure is marked by continued quality-of-life improvements in the college, the expansion of the Global Classroom programme, and our transition to Hong Kong’s ‘New Senior Secondary’ scheme.</p> <p>Kennard oversaw renovations of the Wong Ming Him Hall (2008) and the College Hall (2010). He also launched the artist-in-residence programme (2007) and the academic-in-residence programme (2013), improving students’ extracurricular experiences. During his headship, the Primary School relocated to its current Pokfulam Road location in 2013.</p> <p>Outside of administrative work, Kennard has researched College history in depth, parts of which this exhibition adapts. He has since taken headships in China, Malaysia, Poland, Switzerland, and Australia.</p> \r\n\r\n","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/80.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":81,"alumni_name":"David Leung Chung Por MH","alumni_name_chi":"梁松波","lifespan":"(1938–2021)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"小學敎育理念改革家","eng_alias":"The Second Headmaster of St. Paul’s Primary School","chi_desc":" <p>梁松波在一九七〇至二〇〇一年間任聖保羅書院小學校長，於一九九三年領小學遷至山道。</p> <p>梁氏出身自聖公會家庭，畢業於聖保羅英文下午校（聖馬可中學前身），後入讀新亞書院。梁氏對學生英文要求特別高，在任校長期間，用心鞏固小學教育方針和理念，沿襲戰前英文書院八級舊制，訂立在小三局部轉用英文，小四增加使用英語，到小五全面使用英語教學，以銜接中學部授課。</p> <p>除致力辦學，梁氏又任聖公會期刊《敎聲》英文編輯，在一九九九年任中西區學校聯絡會主席，又曾效力中華基督敎靑年會，一生服務敎會及中西區，於二〇〇〇年獲頒特區榮譽勛章。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>David Leung was the second headmaster of St. Paul’s College Primary School from 1970 to 2001, overseeing its relocation to Hill Road in 1993.</p> <p>Leung was au fait with the Sheng Kung Hui (SKH) from a young age and was an alumnus of St. Paul’s English PM School (now St. Mark’s School) and New Asia College. As headmaster, Leung placed emphasis on his students’ English abilities. He set the Primary School on a distinct yet complementary course relative to the College, adapting the pre-war eight-class curriculum: teaching fully in English by Primary 5, preceded by two years of gradual transition.</p> <p>In addition to his educational work, Leung’s other passion is social work. He once edited the English edition of Echo, an SKH journal. In 1999, Leung chaired the Central and Western District’s Schools Liaison Committee, and he has also done teaching work for the Chinese YMCA. He was awarded the Medal of Honour in 2000.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/81.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":82,"alumni_name":"John Fryer","alumni_name_chi":"傅蘭雅","lifespan":"(1839–1928)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"晚淸漢學翻譯科學敎育家","eng_alias":"Late Qing Translator for Scientific Education","chi_desc":"<p>傅蘭雅生於英國肯特郡，爲翻譯及漢學家。在一八六一至六三年任聖保羅書院主任（headmaster），後赴京師同文館教授英文，在一八六五年赴上海，在一八六八至九六年間任江南製造局翻譯。傅氏一生致力爲中國引入科學知識，爲中國近代學識史的重要人物。</p> <p>傅氏自幼着迷中國。在英修讀期間，獲施美夫會督邀請來港授課，年僅廿二歲出任聖保羅書院主任。在任其間，他致力敎學，同時自己習粵語，學中文，鑽硏中國文化。施美夫對他評價甚高，褒其精神、判斷、才能出眾；傅氏又與學徒要好，當時學生包括有伍廷芳和陳言。</p> <p>傅氏在滬卅年漢譯逾百籍，與科學（時稱「格致」）密不可分，受梁啓超、譚嗣同等晚淸學者矚目，影響至今未減，當中所譯術語不少沿用至今，如化學元素等。</p> <p>一八七六年，傅氏參與創辦上海格致書院，爲中國首間科學圖書館，同年創辦中國史上首份科學期刊《格致彙編》。九年後創辦格致書室，梁氏譚氏皆爲書店常客。</p> <p>一八九六年，傅氏出任美國加州大學柏克萊分校首任東方語言敎授，至一九一三年退休。雖身處海外，傅氏仍關心中國敎育，多次捐建視障和聽障學校。其一生抱負，可由《格致彙編》末刊親筆得知：</p> <blockquote><p>「果覺此編有益於華，則燈下苦功、半生心血，猶未爲枉用於無何益之地矣！惟望中國多興西法，推廣格致，自富自強，漸昌漸熾，以成百世之盛。」</p></blockquote>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Kent, John Fryer was a translator and sinologist. He was a headmaster of St. Paul’s from 1861 to 1863, taught at the Tongwen Guan (1863–65), and translated for the Kiangnan Arsenal in Shanghai (1868–96). A pivotal figure in China’s modern intellectual history, Fryer dedicated his life to bringing Western sciences to China.</p> <p>A sinophile since his youth, Fryer was invited by Bishop George Smith to teach abroad while in Britain. He became St. Paul’s headmaster at the age of twenty-two and was by all accounts amicable and more than capable. Students during this time included Wu Ting Fang and Chan Ayin. Outside of teaching, Fryer learnt Cantonese and grew to master the Chinese language. Smith praised his ebullience, judgment, and raw talent.</p> <p>In Shanghai, Fryer became a well-respected scientific translator. He translated more than 100 books over the course of thirty years, and his work influenced late Qing thinkers like Liang Qichao and Tan Sitong. Some of his terminologies remain in use, such as the Chinese names of chemical elements. </p> <p>Fryer founded the Shanghai Polytechnic in 1876, China’s first science institution. In the same year, he started the first science journal in China, <i>Gezhi Huibian</i> (‘the Chinese Scientific Magazine’). Nine years later, he opened the Scientific Bookstore, where Liang and Tan were regulars. </p> <p>From 1896 to 1913, Fryer was the first professor of Oriental Languages at the University of California, Berkeley. Though abroad, Fryer remained committed to furthering Chinese educational progress by funding schools for the blind and deaf. One of Fryer’s paragraphs in the last issue of <i>Gezhi Huibian</i> sums up what drove him, as translated from the Chinese:</p> <p><i>If this collection proves to benefit China, then the long nights spent pouring one’s heart out would not have been in vain. One can only hope that China will continue to advance Western sciences for the sake of her own strength and enrichment, in order to become a brilliant light that will shine for a hundred generations.</i></p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/82.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":83,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend Mok Sau Tseng","alumni_name_chi":"莫壽增會督","lifespan":"(1866–1943)","grad_year":"1892","chi_alias":"維多利亞敎區首名華人副會督","eng_alias":"The First Chinese Bishop in the Diocese of Victoria","chi_desc":"<p>莫壽增在一八八一年畢業於聖保羅書院，於一九二九年成爲維多利亞敎區首名華人副會督，一生效力敎會，於粵港澳多處擴大敎會。</p> <p>莫氏幼年在廣東增城縣家鄕讀書，十五歲來港入讀聖保羅書院，期間接受洗禮，奉信基督。畢業後曾短暫任職政府部門，但後來立志傳敎，遂重返聖保羅書院，入讀神學班，在一八九二年再度畢業。</p> <p>畢業後，莫氏加入聖公會，成為傳敎士。在一九〇二年獲按立爲會吏，翌年晉升牧師。於一九〇四年，莫氏自薦前往廣州，創辦敎堂、學校、老人院、診所等設施。一九二九年，莫氏升任維多利亞敎區會吏長，爲首名華人擔任此職。在一九三五再獲推選爲敎區首名華人副會督及中華聖公會華南敎區主敎。日本侵華期間，雖然年事已高，莫氏仍投入救濟工作，奔波廣東各地，又設立澳門聖馬可堂和元朗聖馬提亞堂。</p> <p>一九七五年，聖公會於大埔創辦聖公會莫壽增會督中學，以莫氏命名，謹作紀念。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Mok Sau Tseng graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1881. In 1929, he was consecrated as the first ethnic Chinese bishop in the Diocese of Victoria. Throughout Mok’s life, he expanded the diocese’s presence across southern China.</p> <p>Mok’s childhood education was in his hometown of Zengcheng (Tsengcheng). He came to St. Paul’s when he was fifteen, where he converted and was baptised. Mok first joined the civil service after his studies but decided to return to St. Paul’s after a change of heart. He enrolled in the theological class and graduated again in 1892.</p> <p>Mok joined the Anglican Church as a missionary. He was made a deacon in 1902 and ordained the year after. In 1904, Mok went to Guangzhou, where he spent the next two decades building churches, schools, elderly homes, and clinics. He was installed as an archdeacon in 1929 within the Diocese of Victoria — the first Chinese to do so — and consecrated bishop in 1935 within both the Diocese of Victoria and the Chung Hua Sheng Kung Hui. As war broke out between Japan and China, Mok continued his mission across southern China despite his advancing age, founding St. Mark’s Church in Macau and St. Matthias’ Church in Yuen Long.</p> <p>The SKH Bishop Mok Sau Tseng Secondary School in Tai Po, founded in 1975, was named in his honour.</p> ","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/83.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":84,"alumni_name":"Chau Kwan Lam","alumni_name_chi":"巢坤霖","lifespan":"(1888–1953)","grad_year":"1900","chi_alias":"中學學校創辦敎育家","eng_alias":"Principal of the Evening School of Higher Chinese Studies","chi_desc":"<p>巢坤霖爲敎育家及外交家，在一九一五至二一年間曾於北京淸華學校（今淸華大學）敎授英文，抗戰時在澳洲爲國民政府宣傳抗日，又於香港力推華僑敎育。</p><p>巢氏早年就讀聖保羅書院【註㊀】，後赴英進修，在一九〇八年起先後於聖約翰學院【註㊁】、杜倫大學及倫敦大學就讀，本來修讀神學，後轉攻文學。</p><p>巢氏喜愛中華文化，尤好粵劇，曾助馬師曾改編莎士比亞作品成粵劇形式。同時，巢氏熱衷敎育工作，在北角創辦淸華中學，用國語授敎，並出任香港華僑敎育會負責人，在一九三五年曾安排學者胡適演講，於港推廣白話文。</p><p>香港淪陷後，巢氏逃至廣西桂林，在一九四三年獲國民政府委派前往澳洲悉尼，爲宣傳抗日而四處演講，尤其爭取澳洲大量華僑支持，並推動中澳合作。抗戰後留守澳洲，協助滯留同胞返國，在一九四九年才再次返港，及後一九五一年出任官立文商專科學校校長。</p>  <p>【註㊀】　雖知巢氏爲聖保羅舊生，但未知巢氏何時就讀本校，有猜測一九〇一或一九〇三年離校。</p> <p>【註㊁】　截至一九〇八年，「聖約翰學院」可指牛津大學或劍橋大學成員學院，英格蘭敎會當時於倫敦又設有俗稱聖約翰學院的神學院，一九〇九年又於杜倫設立同名學院，該學院於一九一九年成爲杜倫大學成員學院，未知巢氏就讀哪間。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Chau Kwan Lam was an educator and diplomat. He taught English (1915–21) at Tsinghua College (today Tsinghua University) and promoted Chinese education in Hong Kong. During the Second Sino-Japanese War, Chau was in Australia, helping China in its war propaganda effort.</p> <p>Chau studied at St. Paul’s College until the early 1900s.* In 1908, he continued his education in England, including at St. John’s College,† Durham University, and the University of London. Originally a theology student, Chau later focused on literature.</p> <p>Chau was a passionate proponent of Chinese culture and liked Cantonese opera in particular. In collaboration with artist Ma Si Tsang, he once adapted a Shakespeare play for the medium. While in Hong Kong, he continued his educational work, founding a Mandarin-taught secondary school and promoting vernacular written Chinese; he once hosted the scholar Hu Shih in a 1935 seminar.</p> <p>After the fall of Hong Kong, Chau fled to Guangxi and was sent to Sydney, where he gave frequent speeches and collaborated with the Chinese diaspora, thereby improving Sino-Australian ties. He assisted in repatriation after the war and returned to Hong Kong in 1949, where he was appointed principal of the Evening School of Higher Chinese Studies in 1951.</p> <p>* We do not know exactly when Chau entered or left St. Paul’s; one conjecture places his departure at 1901 or 1903.</p><p>† We are unsure which St. John’s College Chau studied at. At the time (1908), St. John’s College could refer to a constituent college of Cambridge or Oxford; it was also the informal name for an Anglican theological college in London, and the Church of England also founded St. John’s College in Durham in 1909, where it became a constituent college of Durham University in 1919.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/84.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":85,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend Peter Kwong Kong Kit GBS ","alumni_name_chi":"鄺廣傑榮休大主敎","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1957","chi_alias":"聖公會港澳敎區首位華人主敎","eng_alias":"the First Chinese Bishop in the Diocese of Hong Kong and Macau","chi_desc":"<p>鄺廣傑在一九五〇年入讀聖保羅書院，於一九五八年完成初六。一九六五年獲按立成聖品人，於荃灣荊冕堂任牧師。翌年敎會調配鄺氏至聖保羅堂及聖雅各堂接替各職務。在一九八一年獲祝聖爲聖公會港澳敎區首位華人主敎。</p> <p>在任期間，鄺氏致力確保教會於中華人民共和國政府對香港恢復行使主權之下繼續順利運作。一九九八年香港聖公會敎省成立，鄺氏獲選爲首任大主敎及敎省主敎長，繼續帶領聖公會為港服務，至二〇〇七年榮休。</p> <p>就讀書院時亦活躍參與基督敎活動；後來獲宗敎老師重用，負責統籌校內宗敎及團契活動。期內所累積的經驗，對其日後事奉影響深遠。</p> \r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Peter Kwong enrolled at St. Paul’s College in 1950 and completed his studies there in 1958. He joined the clergy in 1965, serving as a priest at the Sheng Kung Hui Crown of Thorns Church in Tsuen Wan. A year later, he was assigned to St. Paul’s and St. James’ Church. In 1981, he became the first Chinese to lead the Diocese of Hong Kong and Macau.</p> <p>Kwong worked to make sure that institutions operated smoothly as Hong Kong returned to the motherland. This led to the creation of the Anglican Province of Hong Kong in 1998. Kwong was elected its first Archbishop and Primate, continuing to lead the province until retiring in 2007.</p> <p>While at St. Paul’s, Kwong took a keen interest in the College’s religious activities. The teachers trusted young Kwong so much that they had him coordinate the College’s Christian Union activities, influencing his future path and ministry.</p> \r\n\r\n","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/85.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":86,"alumni_name":"The Right Reverend Dr Thomas Soo Yee Po JP","alumni_name_chi":"蘇以葆榮休主敎","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1963","chi_alias":"1963屆畢業 — 公益慈善主敎","eng_alias":"Former Bishop of West Kowloon ","chi_desc":"<p>蘇以葆一九五六年入讀聖保羅書院，一九六五年高六畢業，一九七九年獲按立爲聖公會牧師，一九九五年獲祝聖主敎，一九九八至二〇一一年榮休之間出任西九龍敎區主敎。</p> <p>蘇氏熱心公益，曾任香港基督敎靑年會義務顧問、雅麗氏何妙齡那打素醫院管治委員會主席、香港基督敎協進會主席、香港專業輔導協會督導及院士、政府監管釋囚委員會及表演藝術委員會成員等職務。</p> <p>就讀書院時，蘇氏活躍於學校團契。當時在校牧的帶領下，他幫忙逢星期五在學校的禮拜堂舉行活動。蘇氏為現任書院校董。</p> \r\n\r\n","eng_desc":"<p>Thomas Soo entered St. Paul’s College in 1956 and graduated from the sixth form in 1965. He was ordained by the Sheng Kung Hui in 1979, consecrated in 1995, and was Bishop of West Kowloon from 1998 until his retirement in 2011.</p> <p>Beyond the church, Soo has served many community roles, including as an honorary adviser for the YMCA of Hong Kong, chairing the Alice Ho Miu Ling Nethersole Hospital’s governing committee, chairing the Hong Kong Christian Council, being a fellow and supervisor of the Hong Kong Professional Counselling Association, and being on the government’s post-release supervision board and performing arts committees. </p> <p>While at St. Paul’s, Soo was active in the College’s Christian Union, organising activities under the College chaplain’s guidance every Friday. He currently serves on the College council.</p> \r\n\r\n\r\n","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/86.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":87,"alumni_name":"Emeritus Professor David Faure","alumni_name_chi":"科大衛榮休敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1964","chi_alias":"華南歷史敎育家","eng_alias":"Historian in South China Studies","chi_desc":" <p>科大衛一九五七年入讀聖保羅書院，曾任學生會會長及總學生長，一九六六年高六畢業。科氏爲歷史學家，專硏華南歷史，曾於美國印弟安納大學和英國牛津大學任敎，現於香港中文大學授敎，代表作包括《近代中國商業的發展》（二〇一〇年簡體中文初版）及《皇帝和祖宗》（二〇〇九年簡體中文初版，二〇一七年繁體中文初版）。</p> <p>硏究方面，科氏提倡使用歷史人類學之方式，着重實地硏究及口述史。其著作經過詳細考證碑刻、族譜、口述資料等而成，更能反映中國歷史社會經濟之複雜互動。於科氏而言，文獻只爲歷史一小部份，要透過與當事人對話，參與其中，才得以明瞭。</p> <p>就讀聖保羅期間，科氏曾與同學創立《Arts Association Journal》月報，本來只用以訓練自身寫作能力， 但備受好評使銷售良好，其後擴大刊物規模，初嘗出版過程之趣味。書院風氣自由，老師鼓勵學生寫作，自行探索、創作，爲科氏日後學術生涯打下穩健基礎。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>David Faure enrolled at St. Paul’s College in 1957. He was the president of the Students’ Association and the head prefect, and graduated from sixth form in 1966. A historian, Faure specialises in South China studies. He has lectured at Indiana University and at the University of Oxford, and he currently teaches history at the Chinese University of Hong Kong. Selected works include <I>China and Capitalism</I> (2006) and <I>Emperor and Ancestor</I> (2007). </p> <p>Faure is an advocate of historical anthropology in writing, which emphasises fieldwork and oral history; Faure’s works incorporate expanses of carvings, genealogy books, and oral interviews to build a more complete picture of China’s past, reflective of its socio-economic complexity. For Faure, sources provide only half the picture; only through conversation and participation can history’s impact on people be understood.</p> <p>In his College years, Faure co-founded the <I>Arts Association Journal</I>. Originally intended to hone his writing, the journal received favourable reviews and sales continued growing; this allowed him a glimpse into the world of publishing, an experience he remains fond of to this day. The College’s liberal atmosphere and teachers’ encouragement to research and create provided solid foundations for Faure’s academic career.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/87.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":88,"alumni_name":"Professor Frank Fu Hoo Kin BBS MH JP","alumni_name_chi":"傅浩堅敎授","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1966","chi_alias":"香港體育運動科學學科創辦人","eng_alias":"Founder of Hong Kong Sports Science","chi_desc":"<p>傅浩堅在一九六六年畢業於聖保羅書院，爲體育運動科學敎授。一九八八年，傅氏在香港中文大學任職講師時，開辦香港首個體育運動科學學士學位課程，爲本港體育學系發展立下基礎。一九九二年，傅氏加入香港浸會大學，曾任社會科學院院長及協理副校長，現任榮休敎授兼許士芬博士體康硏究中心榮譽顧問。其弟傅浩強同爲本校舊生。</p> <p>傅氏硏究範圍甚爲廣泛，包括體育和社會的關係，例如生活方式及政府政策；又研究體育與環境的關聯，例如空氣質素，亦鑽硏特定生理學方面的工作，及着手作比對硏究。除醉心於硏究以外，他致力推廣體育，創《Journal of Exercise Science & Fitness》學術期刊，曾任總編輯。他亦撰有《運動與健康》。此外，傅氏又曾任多項公職，包括二〇〇九至二〇一六年間曾任政府體育委員會成員。傅氏在二〇〇四年獲委太平紳士，二〇〇九年獲頒榮譽勳章，二〇二一年獲頒銅紫荊星章。</p> <p>傅氏曾言，他在聖保羅書院學習時已決定為體育界奉獻一生。在學時，他尤擅羽毛球，曾任書院羽毛球隊隊長，更曾代表香港參賽。傅氏又曾與胞弟一同效力書院籃球隊。畢業後，傅氏遠赴美國，先後於體育文化濃厚的達特茅斯學院及春田學院深造。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Frank Fu graduated from St. Paul’s College in 1966. A professor in sports science, Fu introduced Hong Kong’s first sports science undergraduate programme in 1988 while lecturing for the Chinese University of Hong Kong, advancing the academic field’s presence in the city. In 1992, Fu joined the Hong Kong Baptist University (HKBU), and once served as its social sciences dean and as associate vice-president. He now serves as emeritus professor and honorary consultant at the Dr Stephen Hui Research Centre for Physical Recreation and Wellness. His younger brother, Freddie, also studied at St. Paul’s.</p> <p>Fu’s research spans a broad range of topics, from the social aspects of sport, such as lifestyle and government policy, to environmental effects, such as air quality, to more specific physiological work, to comparative studies. Outside of his own research, Fu also founded the <I>Journal of Exercise Science & Fitness</I>, and had served as its editor-in-chief. Fu’s advocacy extends beyond academia: he authored <I>Sports and Health</I>, and had also served on the government’s Sports Commission from 2009 to 2016. He was appointed JP in 2004 and awarded the Medal of Honour and BBS in 2009 and 2021 respectively.</p> <p>This lifelong passion in sport blossomed while at St. Paul’s, says Fu. He excelled at badminton during his teenage years, having captained the College team and having been a youth representative for Hong Kong. Also, like his younger brother, Fu was part of the College’s basketball team. After graduating, Fu furthered his studies at Dartmouth and Springfield, two American universities with rich athletic traditions.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/88.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":89,"alumni_name":"The Reverend Moses Wu","alumni_name_chi":"吳東牧師","lifespan":"(1914–1995)","grad_year":null,"chi_alias":"聖保羅合唱團榮譽指揮","eng_alias":"Honourable Conductor of St. Paul’s College Choir","chi_desc":" <p>吳東生於廣州，一九五二年起於聖保羅書院任敎共廿三載，建立了書院戰後的良好音樂根基，曾領本校合唱團於校際音樂節贏得最佳男聲合唱團獎十次及會長盃（最佳合唱團）八次。吳氏又爲本校校歌編曲人。</p> <p>吳氏一家爲基督徒，早年居滬，一九四九年移居香港。吳氏爲人嚴肅，敬業樂業，對音樂之熱誠感動不少學生，合唱團員間猶如兄弟，啓發學生日後於音樂界內外成就驕人。同時，吳氏亦信任團員能力，讓學生於比賽中指揮合唱團。此「傳統」受繼任音樂主任沿襲，讓學生少時始獲寶貴經驗。</p> <p>一九七一年，吳氏加入《普天頌讚》（新敎中文詩歌集）編輯小組，協其一九七七年重版。退休後，吳氏移居英格蘭，爲各華僑禮拜堂服務，一九八二年獲英格蘭聖公會按立會吏，翌年獲按立牧師。書院値一四〇週年之際（一九九一年），吳牧師應邀返港指揮校友合唱團演出。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Born in Guangzhou, Moses Wu taught at St. Paul’s College from 1952 to 1975 and was largely credited for establishing the College’s post-war musical excellence. He led the College to ten Best Male Choir titles and took the President’s Cup eight times in the Hong Kong Schools Music Festival. Wu also composed the current College song.</p> <p>Born into a Christian family, Wu spent his early years in Shanghai before moving to Hong Kong in 1949. Known for his passion, dedication, and strict discipline, Wu fostered a sense of camaraderie and trust within his groups, in turn inspiring his students towards great achievement, both within and outside the music industry. This trust went both ways: Wu allowed his students to conduct the choir in competitions, and his successors have continued that ‘tradition’, affording students invaluable experience.</p> <p>In 1971, Wu joined the editorial board for the second edition (1977) of <I>Hymns of Universal Praise</I>, a protestant Chinese hymnal. After retirement, Wu settled in England, serving British Chinese congregations. He was made a deacon in 1982 and ordained the following year. During our 140th anniversary (1991) celebrations, Wu returned to Hong Kong to conduct the College’s alumni choir.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/89.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":90,"alumni_name":"Anissa Chan Wong Lai Kuen BBS JP","alumni_name_chi":"陳黃麗娟","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1977","chi_alias":"預科畢業 — 銅紫荊敎育家","eng_alias":"Educator with BBS ","chi_desc":"<p>陳黃麗娟在一九七七年於聖保羅書院預科畢業，一九九三至二〇〇四年間曾任聖公會莫壽增會督中學校長，二〇〇四至二〇一七年任聖保羅男女中學校長。</p> <p>陳太中學時期就讀於瑪利諾書院（今瑪利曼中學）。她一直鍾情科學，但當時該校預科不設理科班，最終選擇升讀聖保羅書院，迅速適應校園生活，兩年間曾任學長及參與《弘道》編輯工作。</p> <p>深造後於英華女學校任教化學，五年後擢升爲科主任。除擔任中學校長外，陳氏二〇〇二至二〇〇四年間任香港津貼中學議會主席，後任香港考試及評核局委員、香港校長中心召集人等職，爲香港敎育界服務，二〇〇八年獲委太平紳士，二〇一五年獲頒銅紫荊星章。</p> <p>回想當初，陳太認爲入讀聖保羅爲正確的抉擇。留下的回憶、朋友、所遇到的老師，皆形成她日後對敎育工作所抱持之理念和激情，秉承本校博雅敎育的精神。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Anissa Chan Wong Lai Kuen is an alumna of St. Paul’s College, having graduated from sixth form in 1977. She has served as principal of the SKH Bishop Mok Sau Tseng Secondary School (1993–2004) and St. Paul’s Co-educational College (2004–17).</p> <p>Graduating from Maryknoll Sisters’ School (today Marymount Secondary School), Chan’s passion lay in the sciences, though the girls’ school lacked a science matriculation stream. She ultimately chose St. Paul’s and quickly adapted; she was a prefect and <I>Wayfarer</I> subeditor.</p> <p>After further studies, Chan taught chemistry at Ying Wa Girls’ School, rising to lead the department after five years. Chan’s service to the city’s education extends beyond her headships; she chaired the Hong Kong Subsidised Secondary Schools Council between 2002 and 2004, was a member of the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority Council, and convenor of the Hong Kong Principals’ Institute. She was appointed JP in 2008 and awarded a BBS in 2015.</p> <p>Chan said in retrospect that entering St. Paul’s was the right choice for her. The teachers, friends, and anecdotes she gathered influenced her passion and liberal approach towards her future teaching career.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/90.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"},{"id":91,"alumni_name":"Young Tak Sun (Sik Hin Tak)","alumni_name_chi":"楊德新（衍德法師）","lifespan":null,"grad_year":"1983","chi_alias":"佛學心理敎育家","eng_alias":"Assistant Professor of Buddhism, Healthcare, Psychology, and Psychotherapy","chi_desc":" <p>楊德新，法名衍德法師，一九八三年畢業於聖保羅書院，中六移居澳洲，一九九二年於悉尼大學醫學院畢業後行醫。他一九九七年出家，於大嶼山寶蓮禪寺靜修，三年後獨自修行，到訪亞洲各地硏修各種佛敎傳統。現爲珠海學院佛學研究中心助理敎授。硏究領域主要圍繞佛敎與醫療、心理學及心理治療之關係。</p> <p>楊氏在聖保羅書院接觸基督敎信仰多年，離校後自己也吸收不少有關佛學的知識，深深明白宗敎之間的異同。雖然佛敎不相信造物主，與基督敎不一樣，但他表示兩個宗敎都主張希望人類有快樂，要互相尊重及理解去進行交流。</p>","eng_desc":"<p>Young Tak Sun, also known as Sik Hin Tak (his Dharma name), graduated from St. Paul’s in 1983. He moved to Australia the following year and graduated from the University of Sydney’s Medical School in 1992. Initially a medical practitioner, Young joined the Po Lin Monastery on Lantau Island in 1997, becoming a Buddhist monk. Three years later, he decided to travel across Asia alone to study different branches of Buddhism. Young is currently an assistant professor at Chu Hai College’s Centre of Buddhist Studies. His research primarily focuses on the relationship between Buddhism, healthcare, psychology, and psychotherapy.</p> <p>Young’s time at St. Paul’s acquainted him with Christianity, and the years that followed saw his devotion to Buddhism. Throughout many years, he gradually understood the similarities and differences between the two religions. Although Buddhism is not creationist, unlike Christianity, he notes both religions advocate for human happiness as well as mutual respect and understanding in communication.</p>","category":"teachers","exhibition_thumbnail":"/data/media/upload_files/90_alumni/91.png","exhibition_video":"","status":"A"}]}};
